AB  - Purpose: American Indians/Alaska Natives (AI/ANs) are significantly impacted by alcohol use. Developing culturally centered substance use treatment interventions may help decrease the burden of alcohol use among this population. Recent research has revealed that qualitative methodologies and community-based participatory research (CBPR) approaches are very commonly used in the development of interventions targeting AI/ANs with a broad range of health conditions. Harnessing culture-centered knowledge and perspectives from AI/ANs communities can help to ensure that substance use interventions developed for this population are culturally appropriate and acceptable to this population. Drum-Assisted Recovery Therapy for Native Americans (DARTNA) is a new and promising substance use treatment intervention for AI/ANs that utilizes drumming as itsmain component of treatment. Method(s): This presentation will describe community-based strategies employed to finalize the development of DARNTA. Research activities conducted to finalize the development of DARTNA included: 1) conducting an initial pretest of DARTNA provided to 10 AI/ANs with histories of substance use disorders, and 2) conducting three subsequent focus groups among AI/AN DARTNA pretest participants, substance use treatment providers, and the DARTNACommunity Advisory Board. Result(s): Participants in the DARTNA pretest reported either maintenance of sobriety or reductions in drug and/or alcohol use. In addition, significant improvements were found in medical status (p < 0.05), psychiatric status (p < 0.05), spirituality (SP 12 total subscale) (p < 0.05), (meaning/ peace subscale) (p < 0.01), and in physical/functioning levels (p < 0.05). Furthermore, participants enjoyed their experience in the DARTNA program. Conclusion(s): Results fromthe DARTNA pretest and focus groups provide support for DARTNA as a culturally appropriate and acceptable substance use treatment for AI/ANs. Furthermore, results suggest that DARTNA may be beneficial for AI/ANs with substance use problems. Utilization of communitybased participatory research (CBPR) techniques helped to ensure that DARTNA was developed in a culturally-appropriate manner. The final development of DARTNA led to a follow-up feasibility pilot randomized clinical trial, which is near completion, to analyze the potential benefits of DARTNA.
