AB  - The interaction between science and ideology is of significance to people who are marginalised. [...]it is both prudent and wise to question assumptions and values driving scientific research to insure the research does not reflect the generally accepted differential treatment of some groups of people' (L'Institut Roeher, 1996, p. 5). Criticism has been levelled at researchers who inadequately incorporate the objectives of research subjects. Since professionals may not always act in the best interest of the individual, but may act to advance their own interests, we must consider whether research always has the individual's point of view and perspective as the highest in priority (Turnbull, 1988, p. 374). [...]legal advocates should ensure that equal protection is provided each client, regardless of whether this is ensconced in constitutional or other legal principles. Note that Carson's model presumes benefit to the individual. [...]consideration should be given to whether current ethical standards and legal proposals sufficiently address the therapeutic outcomes for the individual who may be subject to genetic research.
