AB  - Examined the effect of scheduled intermittent hospitalization on the hospital utilization, community adjustment, and self-esteem of 57 male veterans (aged 28–65 yrs) with serious and persistent mental illness. Ss were frequent utilizers of inpatient care over the previous 2 yrs and were randomly assigned to 2 groups. Ss in the experimental group were prescheduled for 4 hospital admissions, each lasting 9–11 days, per yr for 2 yrs. Patients in the control group had traditional access to hospital care. Psychiatric bed days, community adjustment, and self-esteem were assessed at intake, at 1 yr, and after the intervention. The experimental group showed improvement in self-esteem, affect, and complaints of physical symptoms at one year. No significant differences between groups were found in hospital utilization, financial management, substance abuse, or psychological well-being at 1 yr. It is concluded that scheduled intermittent hospitalization may be an appropriate and promising alternative to traditional care for revolving-door patients. This intervention could maintain patients at a higher level of wellness than traditional care and reduce the recurrence of the crises that precipitate hospitalization. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
