AB  - Background: Cigarette smoking, the leading preventable cause of death in the United States, accounts for more than 480,000 deaths annually. Individuals with substance use disorders (SUDs) and/or mental illness have two 4-fold higher prevalences of smoking cigarettes than the general population. Alternative nicotine delivery systems such as e-cigarettes have become increasingly popular, with sales doubling every year in the United States since 2008. In a large meta-analysis of observational studies, previous e-cigarette use was estimated to be 16.8%, and current use was estimated to be 11.1%; the perception that e-cigarettes were less harmful than regular cigarettes was 53%. However, there is little research on e-cigarette use and perceptions among those with mental disorders and SUDs. Herein we investigate perceptions and attitudes toward e-cigarette in terms of risk, harm, and addictiveness in this vulnerable population. Method(s): A total of 100 veterans with SUDs and/or mental illnesses receiving psychiatric care were recruited at the San Francisco VA Medical Center outpatient clinics. Each participant completed a paper survey inquiring about their smoking, e-cigarette and marijuana use as well as their health conditions and other substance use. Result(s): Out of 100 veterans (96% male, 88% white, mean age = 56 years; SD = 12) surveyed, 91 were current or past smokers, 9 were never smokers, 37 were ever e-cigarette users and 61 never e-cigarette users. (No significant difference in demographics or smoking history between never and ever e-cigarette users.) Most respondents believe that e-cigarettes are not safe for human use (79%) and potentially dangerous or harmful (78%). Fifty-three percent felt they were safer to use than regular cigarettes. In terms of potential addictiveness, most respondents agreed with the statement that if they use e-cigarettes daily, they will become addicted (77.8%), whereas 37% felt e-cigarettes were less addictive than regular cigarettes and 50% felt they were equally addictive. There were no significant differences comparing e-cigarette ever and never users for measures related to harm and addiction potential. Fifty-four percent of respondents felt e-cigarettes could help people quit smoking regular cigarettes completely and there was a significant difference in this belief when comparing never users (68% agreed) and ever users (31% agreed) of e-cigarettes; x =9.218, P = 0.003. Conclusion(s): Veterans with mental illness and/or SUDs perceived e-cigarettes as harmful, unsafe and addictive, and there was no difference in past use of e-cigarettes between groups. Compared with previous studies of other populations, ever use of e-cigarettes was higher, whereas risk perception was similar in our sample. However, never users of e-cigarettes noted more potential to quit smoking with e-cigarettes compared with ever users. In this sample, which highly accesses addiction psychiatry/medicine providers, there is skepticism about e-cigarettes among ever users, which may reflect effects of provider counseling. Given the increasing prevalence of e-cigarette use, larger studies on perceptions as well as objective measurements of risk, harm, addiction potential and smoking cessation potential of e-cigarettes may help reduce morbidity and mortality among those with mental illness and SUDs. Regulatory bodies may also benefit from this research to guide policy, packaging, and public health education related to e-cigarettes.
