AB  - This dissertation presents three self-contained essays, two of which examine the effects of policy changes on suicides, and a third essay which addresses the lack of diversity in the Economics profession. What unifies these three essays is the underlying goal to understand how changes in policy effect certain under-studied and under-represented populations like persons dealing with mental illness as well as women and minorities.The first essay asks the question: How did the Federally Funded Community Mental Health Center (CMHC) Act of 1963 effect county-level suicides. To answer this question, I use data on when and where federally funded CMHCs were established, and find that the presence of operating centers is associated with a reduction in suicides by 2.5 percent. Strongest effects are found for males aged 15 to 34 and males over 65 who experienced 4.5 percent and 5.4 percent fewer suicides when there was an operating CMHC in a county, respectively. This essay presents empirical evidence suggesting that operational CMHCs and the services they provided increased the availability and utilization of treatment for mental illness, leading to improved mental health as measured by decreased suicides for men.The second essay examines the effect of unemployment insurance (UI) on suicide rates by exploiting cross-state variation in the maximum UI benefit durations available during the Great Recession. Starting at the end of 2007, the Great Recession brought with it large increases in UI benefit durations across the US, as well as a reported increase in suicides rates. Between 2008 and 2014, two US policies provided up to 73 weeks of extended UI benefits, in addition to regular UI benefits already in place, to states meeting certain unemployment rate thresholds and criteria. With the extended benefits from these two policies, many states saw their UI benefit durations increase from 26 weeks of regular benefits up to a maximum of 99 weeks. Comparing adjacent counties located in neighboring states, I find that the maximum increase in benefit duration leads to a statistically significant decrease in male suicide rates. In particular, I find that a 73 week increase in UI benefits is associated with an 11% decrease in the monthly male suicide rate. This study is able to provide micro-level empirical evidence suggesting that expanding UI benefits during times of economic downturn has a protective effect on population suicide levels.Finally, the third essay, joint with Kelly Bedard and Shelly Lundberg, examines the possibility of nudging women and underrepresented race/ethnicity groups into the study of economics with positive feedback by conducting an experiment. Our experiment involved sending 'treatment' and 'control' letters to undergraduate students after completing their first principles of economics course. A random sample of students earning a B or better in the course were sent a personalized letter inviting them to an informational meeting, describing the economics majors and associated career opportunities, and explaining that their performance in the course suggests that an economics major might be of interest to them. Control letters were sent to the remainder of students with a C or better that included the meeting invitation and the description of the major and career opportunities, but excluded the part about their performance. This performance feedback, or 'nudge', increased the likelihood that students attended the informational meeting for both men and women. It also increased the number of women entering the Economics and Accounting Major by about five percentage points, induced about the same fraction to enter the Economics Major (with some switching from accounting), and significantly increased the number of Hispanic men and women entering Economics and Accounting. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
