AB  - There exists a significant amount of interest in understanding the effects of computer-based activities on the symptoms of schizophrenia. It has been shown that computer activities affect both cognitive deficits in the areas of concentration and stimuli integration, and affective deficits in the areas of motivation, initiation, self-esteem, and capacity for enjoyment. However, few studies have examined the cognitive and affective benefits to schizophrenia clients when exposed to computer-based recreational activities. The purpose of this study is to examine the effects of exposure to a computer leisure activity on 12 schizophrenia clients. Research questions are explored by introducing 12 day-treatment schizophrenia clients to four 45-minute sessions of computer activity. Participants were encouraged to write a story using the software 'Storybook Weaver.' Participants also attended a focus group interview and filled out a pre and post self-esteem Likert scale, a leisure scale, and time and attendance logs. Stories developed on the computer were examined through systematic analysis. Results indicated that participants, on the average, found the computer activity pleasurable because of its sophistication and user-friendliness. All participants showed a high level of concentration and most demonstrated high attendance rate. A majority of the participants were highly motivated in continuing the computer sessions and showed a significant degree of interest in the activity. All participants were able to integrate visual and auditory stimuli and were able to understand the techniques needed to write stories on the computer. However, while some participants were more interested in creating coherent stories, others were more interested in exploring the computer program. Results also indicated that participants' self-esteem in relation to computer utilization was positively affected. Overall results of this study suggest that children's computer software utilized in this computer-based recreational activity was overwhelmingly accepted by the participants and that it affected their cognitive and affective skills in positive ways. Results also suggest that large scale studies in computer-based recreational activities are worthwhile. In summary, computer-assisted recreational activities for schizophrenia clients are bound to gain acceptance by mental health services providers. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
