T1  - Effect of a brief cognitive-behavioral group intervention during rehabilitation for women with breast cancer
JF  - https://trialsearch.who.int/Trial2.aspx?TrialID=DRKS00017571
A1  - DRKS00017571
KW  - eppi-reviewer
PY  - 2019
DA  - 2019///
Y1  - 2019///
AB  - INTERVENTION: Intervention 1: The intervention consists of three 50‐minute sessions, i.e. one session per week, and is performed as an open group intervention. In the group intervention proven behavioral techniques are applied like mindfulness therapy and acceptance and commitment therapy. The group intervention is complementary to the psycho‐oncological treatment modules which are provided according to the rehabilitation guidelines of the German Pension Insurance. The intervention is conducted by Dr Dr Johannes Mattes. During the primary treatment patients do not really have time for the mental processing of their disease. Active psychological coping needs a certain rest. Inpatient rehabilitation allows such a time‐out. Patients start to reflect their disease and its consequences (e.g. anxiety, sorrows, partnership issues, fatigue, feelings of insufficiency). An accompanying support lasting several weeks would be helpful in many cases but rapidly and easy available services are lacking in sufficient numbers. Moreover, many patients hesitate to admit being scared or depressed and have difficulties to look for professional support. A post‐acute rehabilitation program is therefore for many patients the first opportunity to discuss anxiety, depression, sorrows, fatigue and pessimism. Patients in rehabilitation can gather experiences with psychotherapeutic techniques, and it is clarified if further psychotherapeutic support is needed. The intervention supports people to help themselves, to arrive in the here and now, to cope with negative feelings, and to recognize early the inability to master demands, and to apply protective strategies. Intervention 2: The control group receives an unspecific talk therapy with the same frequency and duration. This group therapy is also conducted by Dr Dr Johannes Mattes. CONDITION: C50 ‐ Malignant neoplasm of breast PRIMARY OUTCOME: Primary outcome is the anxiety score measured by the Hospital Anxiety and Depressions Scale (HADS). The HADS is a questionnaire for the self‐report of depression and anxiety which was developed for patients with physical illness (Zigmond and Snaith 1983). The anxiety module of the HADS consists of 7 items. Symptoms of anxiety during a period of one week are scored with 0 to 3 points. The total score ranges from 0 to 21 points. The level of anxiety is assessed at the beginning and the end of the rehabilitation program and 12 weeks after completing the program. The primary endpoint is the 12‐week measurement. SECONDARY OUTCOME: Depressive symptoms: Depressive symptoms are also assessed with the HADS (Zigmond and Snaith 1983). The depression module consists of 7 items as well. The total score ranges from 0 to 21 points (assessment: at the beginning and the end of the rehabilitation program and 12 weeks after completing the program).; Distress thermometer: Patients report their distress during the last week using 0 (patient is feeling very good) to 10 points (patient is feeling very bad). The distress thermometer was designed to evaluate psychosocial distress of cancer patients. It was developed by the American National Comprehensive Cancer Network, a collaborative partnership of 28 cancer centers in the United States. In Germany the distress thermometer is highly accepted (Mehnert et al. 2006) (assessment: at the beginning and the end of the rehabilitation program and 12 weeks after completing the program).; Fatigue: Fatigue is measured by the Brief Fatigue Inventory which scores the severity (3 items) and the impact (6 items) of cancer‐related fatigue (Mendoza et al. 1999). All items are scored with 0 to 10 points. Raw scores are averaged so that the fatigue total score also ranges from 0 to 10 points (assessment: at the beginning and the end of the rehabilitation program and 12 weeks after completing the program).; Quality of life (EORTC QLQ‐C30): Quality of life is assessed with the EORTC QLQ‐C30 (Aaronson et al. 1993). The questionnaire provides scores for measuring physical functioning (5 items), role functioning (2 items), emotional functioning (4 items), cognitive functioning (2 items), social functioning (2 items) and global health (2 items). Additional 13 items cover diverse symptoms and complaints such as pain and financial difficulties. The sub‐scores are transformed into a range of values from 0 to 100 points. Higher scores indicate better health and quality of life more complaints in case of the symptom scores respectively (assessment: at the beginning and the end of the rehabilitation program and 12 weeks after completing the program).; Other variables: Socio‐demographic characteristics are collected according to the recommendations of Deck and RÃ¶ckelein (1999). The duration of the rehabilitation is registered by the rehabilitation facility. Provided services are documented according to the classifications of therapeutic treatments (Deutsche Rentenversicherung 2014). Additional items evaluate if the intended contents and learning objectives are actually perceived or reached by the patients. To describe participating patients and for reasons of subgroup analysis the following characteristics are collected: type of rehabilitation (post‐acute rehabilitation or repeated rehabilitation), physical capacity before rehabilitation treatment, received cytostatic chemotherapy, received or ongoing immunotherapy, ongoing anti‐hormonal therapy, polyneuropathy after chemotherapy, tumor stage, type of tumor surgery (breast conserving surgery or mastectomy), and the level of satisfaction with the result of tumor surgery (yes, rather yes, rather no, and no). INCLUSION CRITERIA: We include women with breast cancer (tumor stage I‐IV) undergoing rehabilitation having a score of at least 5 points on the distress thermometer.
