AB  - An exploration of the application of social control in informal, everyday settings. Considering substance abuse intervention as a form of therapeutic social control, current understanding of this activity is enhanced by examining some of the processes through which behavior is labeled as abuse, & therapeutic control is prescribed & applied. Prior to intervention, individuals must define the behaviors of a substance abuser as deviant or troublesome; when they engage in the process of finding assistance or treatment for the substance abuser, they are doing social control. Data from focus group interviews suggests that intimacy between the intervenor & the substance abuser is an important determinant of the application of labels, as well as intervention. However, intervention is not automatic; the influence of intimacy is mediated by numerous other considerations. Several factors that may account for variations within the field of therapeutic social control are identified.
