AB  - Qualitative methods were used to develop a contextually relevant quantitative measure of parenting stress and inform the design of family-focused interventions aimed at preventing adolescent mental and behavioral health problems. The study focused on the experiences of 33 low-income 1- and 2-parent families (single mothers, mothers and fathers, and their 7th- or 8th grade adolescents) representing 3 ethnic groups (Mexican, African, and European Americans) living in low-income neighborhoods in a large Southwestern city. Four stressor factors or domains were mentioned most frequently across ethnic groups (1) out-of-household environmental hazards and negative influences on children, (2) difficulties related to adolescents' oppositional or independence-oriented behavior, (3) financial strain, and (4) time and energy strain imposed by life circumstances. Results were used to develop a new measure of parenting stress and inform future program design. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
