T1  - Mirtazapine and nortriptyline similarly effective third-line treatments for depression.
JF  - Evidence-based Mental Health
A1  - Dunner DL
KW  - eppi-reviewer
PY  - 2007
DA  - 2007/02//
Y1  - 2007/02//
AB  - Is mirtazapine after two failed drug regimens more effective for treating depression than nortriptyline?METHODSDesign: Randomised controlled trial (RCT).Allocation: Unclear.Blinding: Single blind (assessors blinded).Follow up period: Fourteen weeks (treatment period only).Setting: Eighteen primary practices and 23 psychiatric practices in Maryland, USA: enrolment 2001 to 2004.Patients: 235 outpatients with a primary diagnosis of DSM-IV non-psychotic major depressive disorder on entry to the STAR*D study, who did not achieve an adequate response (Quick Inventory of Depressive Symptomatology (QIDS-SR[16]) score </= 5) or could not tolerate two previous drug regimens delivered in the first two phases of this RGT. Exclusions: primary diagnosis of obsessive compulsive or eating disorders; bipolar or psychotic disorders; conditions contraindicating the use of antidepressive medication; substance abuse needing hospitalisation for detoxification; prior non-response to antidepressive medication; pregnancy, or breastfeeding.Intervention: Mirtazopine (15 mg/day titrated up to a maximum of 60 mg/day) or nortriptyline (25 mg titrated up to a maximum of 150 mg/day). Recommended dosage regimens were provided but these were flexible to allow for clinician judgement guided by symptom and side effect ratings.Outcomes: Symptom remission (defined as a score </= 7 on the 17 item Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (HRSD[17]), or a score 5 on the QIDS-SR[16] at study exit); response (defined as reduction of QIDS-SR[1]6 score from baseline); adverse events (Frequency, Intensity and Burden of Side Effects Rating scale).Patient follow up: 33.3% the mirtazapine group and 30.6% of the nortriptyline group completed 12 weeks' treatment. Conservative intention to treat analyses included 100% of participantsMAIN RESULTSThere was no significant difference between mirtazapine and nortriptyline in symptom remission using either the Hamilton or Quick Inventory measures, or in response (HRSD[17] remission: 12.3% with mirtazapine v 19.8% with nortriptyline, p = 0.27; QIDS-SR[16] remission: 8.0% with mirtazapine v 12.4%, p = 0.45; QIDS-SR[16] response: 13.4% with mirtazapine v 16.5% with nortriptyline, p = 0.57). There were also no significant differences between groups in any serious adverse event (3.5% with mirtazapine v 2.5% with nortriptyline, p = 0.65) or psychiatric serious adverse events (3.5% with mirtazapine v 0.8% with nortriptyline, p = 0.16).CONCLUSIONSMirtazapine and nortriptyline offer similar symptom remission rates in people with major depressive disorder for whom two prior antidepressant treatments have failed. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Evidence-based Mental Health is the property of BMJ Publishing Group and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
