AB  - A volume in IV Parts, 10 Chpt's, & an Author's Preface & Acknowledgments. I-HISTORY AND CONTEXT OF THE PROBLEM-contains: (1) The Legislation of Morality-which discusses the moral insight & indignation, knowledge of physiology & the force of law that the 1900's brought with them. Heroine & morphine could no longer be purchased at the corner druggist for a nickel. An examination is made of the connection between laws & morals by historically tracing the evolution of narcotics morality. In 1914 narcotics became illegal with the Harrison Act, introducing the idea of the immorality of narcotics-this morality was a direct result of legal change. An introductory theoretical statement suggesting the cliff effects of law in diff kinds of `moral' situations in which the law is violated is presented. (2) The Effect and Use of Narcotics and Moral Judgment-points out that the moral arguments concerning the use of drugs are largely based on beliefs about the physiological effects of the drug. (3) Who is a Drug Addict ?-states that the only addict focused on in theory & speculation is the addict who is criminally DEL in ways other than just using drugs. The known addict is infused with the total identity of the immoral person with an inadequate personality. This stereotype is seen to come from a specific stereotyped soc context. II-THEORETICAL CONSIDERA- TIONS-includes: (4) Analytic and Empirical Approaches to the Study of Morality-which considers what people describe as moral, analyzes the processes of choice & rejection & observes what is done once behavior is characterized. 3 levels of moral issues are investigated-how the individual manages a set of principles of behavior; the community or soc system's moral order & elements permeating a whole culture. An empirical basis for the discussion of morality is also examined: how the moral order is invoked & maintained & the sanctioning of men in a community. (5) Deviance and the Reaction of Societypresents arguments & their consequences for the development of underdevelopment of theory in the sociol of.deviance. 6 case studies of 'normal' persons in every day contact with drug addicts are given showing how the person or group labeled as deviant responds to others' characterization of his deviance & the notion of societal reaction as a framework. III-AN EMPIRICAL STUDY-consists of: (6) The California Rehabilitation Center-which considers the CRC, a state operated instit handling the criminal addict pop of the state. The physical setting, commitment procedure, therapy techniques & methods of rehabilitation are discussed. (7) The Moral Implications of Psychic Maladjustment in the Deviantstates that the community consensus of appropriate behavior is the standard by which individual adjustment or maladjustment is set. A study is undertaken to determine to what extent the moral interpretation of narcotics use exists in a setting where the participants are intentionally committed to a psychol'al view of addiction & how the moral interpretation or lack of it affects the addict's self-view, the treatment he receives & his relationship with society. Another purpose is to produce a soc profile of the resident addict pop. 1,300 persons at the CRC, from 1964-1965, are interviewed. (8) Perspective on the Addict's World View-considers the double standards of morality in society, the normality of the deviant's world, the correctional counselor's concept of normal society, relapse & the addicts' view of it. IV-GENERAL COMMENTS AND CONCLUSION-presents: (9) Mental Illness and Criminal Intent-which discusses Western man's central dilemma in the treatment of law violators: to explain crime without imparting illness. Diff views of the criminal as immoral, as psychically inadequate, & as a victim of external forces are considered. The idea that `One must be mentally healthy to commit a crime, but commission of a crime reflects an unhealthy mental state' is discussed. (10) Law and Morality-some Summary Conclusions-presents the argument that the apprehension & rehabilitation approach to addiction will fail when the addict is viewed as morally diff from others. A legal change altering the conditions of the addicted is seen to be a solution. A clinical system for narcotic dispension is suggested. It is concluded that the law can change the soc conditions & therefore the climate for a reinterpretation of the moral issues. Notes, a joint subject & author Index. H. Rosen.
