AB  - As American society struggles with the economic demands for managed health-care reform, the effectiveness of psychological models for the treatment of chronic mentally ill adults requires investigation. The milieu treatment model is an interdisciplinary treatment approach applied in psychiatric therapeutic settings in which the total environment is thought to have therapeutic potential. Current patterns of inpatient treatment have been altered by such considerations as budget constraints, emphasis on streamlining patients' return to the community, the use of pharmacological agents, and the increasing numbers of seriously ill, dangerous, and difficult patients. The purpose of this study was to explore the effectiveness of the milieu therapeutic treatment model in treating chronic mentally ill adults. The research compared residential and nonresidential groups of chronic mentally ill adults within a mental health agency by analyzing demographics and treatment plan records. The demographics, which were extracted from archival records, were age, ethnicity, gender, years of education, the specific treatment program of which the client had been a member, marital status, principal source of income, use of private insurance, and duration of disability. Results indicate there were no statistically significant relationships between nonresidential subjects and residential subjects; however, treatment plan outcomes between settings were statistically significant. Residential subjects who received continual traditional treatment achieved a higher rate of success in achieving specific and general goals. Global assessment functioning (GAF) ratings for subjects entering and exiting were significantly different. GAF ratings were higher for residential subjects entering a setting and were lower among nonresidential subjects when exiting a setting. Nonresidential subjects did not recover at the same rate as subjects who were in a residential setting. There were no statistically significant relationships between use of medication and setting membership. Residential subjects reached treatment goals more frequently than those in a nonresidential setting. Setting membership provided no statistically significant differences with regard to duration of goals. Recommendations for future research should include designing a more effective milieu therapy program. Implications of this research indicated that specific information regarding diagnoses and Global Assessment Functioning should be more fully developed as this information could be helpful in treating chronic, as well as acute, patients in the same treatment setting. Information assessed and derived from the milieu could become an arena for making decisions diagnostically. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
