AB  - The article presents the findings of a study that examined the impact of community-based mobile crisis services on post crisis community-based mental health service use and user characteristics related to likelihood of post crisis service use. The Cox regression analytic procedure was applied to matched samples to examine the impact of the community-based mobile crisis intervention on the receipt of community aftercare services. In this design, the approach was to make the pre-intervention and post-intervention groups as similar as possible through the use of the matching procedure on key variables. This approach avoids the limitation imposed by using regression, which only controls for the linearity of the relationships of the variables. Several factors such as the availability of services were investigated and found to be unlikely to have influenced the results. Other factors, however, such as changes in key administrative personnel within agencies or changes in training provided for staff could not be examined.
