AB  - Compared cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) with behavioral placebo therapy (PT) and with relaxation therapy (RT), for treating primary sleep maintenance difficulties. 75 Ss (ages 40–80 years) who met criteria for persistent primary insomnia (PPI) were placed into various treatment groups. CBT Ss were exposed to a cognitive module to correct misconceptions about sleep. The RT group received progressive muscle RT to overcome the conditioned arousal that perpetuates nocturnal wakefulness. PT Ss received a quasi-desensitization treatment to eliminate the conditioned arousal which prolongs nocturnal awakenings. After treatment CBT and RT recipients were asked to return for outcome assessments in 6 months. PT participants were immediately offered active treatment. Cognitive behavior therapy produced the largest effects on measures of sleep fragmentation. Both sleep logs and polysomnography showed that CBT produced greater short-term sleep efficiency improvements. Sleep log estimates and quality ratings favored CBT over RT across the 6-month follow-up period. Only the CBT group showed both subjective (though modest) and objective sleep time increases through treatment. CBT may be important in the clinical management of PPI patients with sleep maintenance difficulties. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
