AB  - 26 women (aged 20–67 yrs) who were inpatients in a women's unit in a private psychiatric hospital completed a survey that explored their quality of life, social support network, ratings of the program, and feelings of self-esteem after discharge. Ss were admitted for depression, anxiety attacks, marital problems, and eating disorders. The average length of stay was 3–4 wks. Results pointed to the effectiveness of the program as indicated by Ss' positive ratings of their current life situation, self-esteem, ability to resume work after discharge, and written appreciative comments. Therapies rated as effective included group therapy, individual therapy, assertiveness training, victim survivor therapy, steps study, esteem building, and psychodrama. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
