AB  - A nurse-facilitated social media group was used to train caregivers of school-aged augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) users. Training was aimed at the caregiver of emergent AAC users and occurred in the home and community settings using a modeling strategy. Module development utilized behavior skills training techniques, consisting of a) instruction, b) modeling, c) practice, and d) feedback and coaching in the community setting. Over the course of three weeks, instruction was primarily delivered to the convenience sample (N = 4) in three 15-minute training sessions using Facebook. In addition to social media instruction, the caregiver-child teams met with other participants to practice newly learned skills during community activities. Activities were approximately one hour in length and included coaching and feedback. A mixed, single-subject AB design was used to evaluate the effects of community instruction on caregiver AAC modeling. Frequency of caregiver modeling was the dependent variable measured by direct observation. One participant failed to complete the study. The use of community-based social media groups is a feasible strategy to teach caregivers AAC. Social media groups need diversity and a facilitator to encourage engagement. Future recommendations include evaluation of long-term effects and maintenance of community-based social-learning groups and identifying factors that motivate caregivers to participate in social- media groups.
