AB  - The current study sought to identify specific needs of battered women and children from various cultures who reside in domestic violence shelters. The researcher interviewed shelter service providers including administration and shelter staff to gain a better understanding of the needs of the residents and current treatment and intervention models in place at four Bay Area domestic violence (DV) shelters. The researcher examined services that are currently provided and how these services address what is known about the psychological consequences of violence in women and children. Findings indicate that the needs of women and children are often determined individually and based on a women's existing resources, current mental health status, and whether she has children. Mental health needs can be influenced by the type, severity, and duration of abuse, history, impact on parenting and coping abilities/skills, resilience, and cultural factors. Services for women and children remain focused on basic needs such as food, clothing and shelter. Mental health services included psychoeducation, support groups, individual, group and family therapy, and referrals for outside resources. Another goal was to gain insight about the influence of culture on one's experience of violence and how shelters address the cultural needs of the women and children they serve. While shelters take into consideration culture and mental health needs by providing language services and a variety of mental health services, staff also found it difficult to describe needs of ethnic minorities and how culture influences their experience beyond language ability. The study concludes with recommendations for how shelters can adapt current models to provide more culturally competent services to better serve diverse populations. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
