AB  - Background: Cognitive dysfunction post stroke may result in reduced efficiency, speed and effectiveness in the performance of ADL. Post‐stroke patients may present with cognitive dysfunction, extensive functional disability and depression at discharge from acute hospital settings, which results in increased length of stay in rehabilitation facilities and increased duration of rehabilitation. Because of the significant association between cognitive dysfunction, depression and functional ability, the effect of visual scanning exercises on these three aspects should be investigated. Purpose: To determine the effect of visual scanning exercises (VSE) integrated with task‐specific activities as an intervention approach on participants' (a) cognitive functioning; (b) depression; and (c) functional ability post‐stroke. Methods: A matched‐pair randomized control trial was conducted. Subjects were matched according to their functional activity level and allocated to either a control (n = 12) or experimental group (n = 12). All patients received task specific activities for a four‐week intervention period. The experimental group received saccadic eye movement training with VSE integrated with task specific activities as an "add on" intervention to task based intervention. Re‐assessments were conducted weekly over the intervention period. Results: Pre‐intervention there was no statistical difference between participants in both groups. Participants presented with mild cognitive dysfunction (p = 0.98); a state of depression (p = 0.642) and severe dependence in the performance of ADL (p = 0.54) at baseline. No statistically significant difference (p = 0.096) in cognitive function was noted over the four‐week intervention period between the groups. However, participants' cognitive function at baseline was compared with a reference group based on age and education level. Interpretation of participants' level of cognitive function in terms of the values of the reference group, indicated that two‐thirds (66.67%) of Group 1 and one third (33.33%) of participants from Group 2's cognitive function improved during the intervention period. Participants level of depression and functional ability, in Group 1 improved statistically significantly more than Group 2 (p = 0.0175; p = 0.04 respectively). Conclusion(s): (1) The effect of saccadic eye movement training with VSE integrated with a task based approach, has a significantly effect on participants' cognitive function and level of depression post‐stroke. (2) The significant improved cognitive function and depression translate to significantly better functional ability following the stroke. (3) The presence of depression may significantly be associated with the presence of cognitive function and impaired functional ability post stroke. Implications: Evidence from the study shows that saccadic eye movement training with VSE integrated with task specific activities as an intervention has a significant effect on (a) cognitive functioning; (b) depression; and (c) functional ability post‐stroke. The evidence will contribute to an evidence‐based holistic understanding of post stroke pathology and contribute to improve post stroke rehabilitation.
