AB  - [...]1) the DMH's role in coordinating linkage between acute care in the managed care organization and DMH services was unclear; ambiguity existed concerning the mental health authority-was it the managed care organization or the DMH ľ (2) the DMH and the managed care organization developed overlapping networks of hospitals and duplication of case management services; (3) the DMH and the managed care organization lacked a shared database for joint planning and monitoring; and (4) acute care referral decisions increasingly were made solely on the basis of payment source. The DMH will hold Medicaid accountable for acute care through its interagency service agreement's purchasing specifications. [...]access, types of services, program/clinical standards, performance indicators, quality and utilization management expectations, reporting requirements (including critical incidents), human rights procedures, licensing, and so forth, will be addressed in Medicaid's managed care organization contract. Savings will be reinvested to expand community-based continuing care services, thus preventing unnecessary use of acute services; upgrade clinical capacity; enhance the quality and utilization management infrastructure; and provide improved access to mental health services, thus reducing the use and costs of general medical services.3 Oversight provisions. J. Callahan et al., "Evaluation of the Massachusetts Medicaid Mental Health/Substance Abuse Program" (Unpublished report submitted to the Massachusetts Division of Medical Assistance by the Heller School for Advanced Studies in Social Welfare, Brandeis University, Waltham, Massachusetts, 24 January 1994); and J.J. Callahan et al., "Mental Health/Substance Abuse Treatment in Managed Care: The Massachusetts Medicaid Experience," Health Affairs (Fall 1995): 173-184.
