AB  - The relative impact of unmet parental expectations for child therapy and therapist personality characteristics on treatment continuance was evaluated for a sample of low income families living in urban to rural areas. The 120 families formed three groups based on their treatment status at the follow-up assessment: Continuers (n = 82), Dropouts (n = 16), and Noninitiators (n = 22). A dropout rate of 16% was evidenced by the families who initiated treatment. The results generally supported the negative influence of unmet parental expectations on treatment continuance. Unmet expectations for therapy events were significantly greater for parents who dropped out of treatment when such unmet expectations concerned consumer needs but not when they concerned parent support/involvement. Unmet expectations for therapist characteristics also negatively impacted treatment continuance, with the parents who dropped out perceiving the therapist as significantly less caring and involved. The finding that unmet parental expectations for child therapy did not have a greater impact on treatment dropout than did unmet expectations for therapist characteristics indicates that these variables work together to influence a parent's decision to end treatment prematurely. The level of family rurality did not significantly influence the effects of unmet parental expectations for either therapy events or therapist characteristics. This suggests that families living in rural areas were not more likely to be influenced by unmet expectations than those living in urban areas. Taken together, these findings emphasize the need for parents who request mental health services for a child to be considered consumers of a service, complete with their own expectations for that service. It is important for therapists to empower parents to identify these expectations for treatment so that discussion can address them throughout treatment. This will potentially decrease the chance that parental expectations w (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
