AB  - Substance abusers constitute a population especially at risk for STD/IIIV transmission. Although substance abusers readily acknowledge that they constitute a risk group, they seldom consider themselves to be at risk as individuals. Publicly funded substance abuse treatment providers are well-poised to provide HIV-prevention interventions as HIV infection rates among drug users entering treatment can be as high as 35 percent; the socioeconomic factors associated with economically disadvantaged drug users place them at higher risk than their economically self-sufficient counterparts. During 2002 and 2003, a two-session group intervention that included IIIV/STD/AIDS information, proper condom application, and condom negotiation skills was conducted among 113 clients in a public housing-based substance abuse treatment program in Key West, Florida. Pre- and post-test data were collected and analyzed using principles of the Transtheoretical model and Bandura's self-efficacy. Results found, at minimum, at least one stage progression among participants as well as increased confidence to apply newly learned skills. Recommendations for implementing similar interventions in substance abuse treatment facilities are provided. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Alcoholism Treatment Quarterly is the property of Taylor & Francis Ltd and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
