AB  - The antisocial personality disorder (ASPD) guideline is a thorough document: the literature search and recommendations were not limited to the risk assessment and treatment of adults with ASPD but also included prevention strategies and treatment of children and adolescents with conduct disorder. Reviewing the National Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence clinical guideline on the management and treatment of ASPD, we are impressed by the amount of work that has been done to retrieve, evaluate and interpret the research in this area. The guideline does not discuss the role of interaction with delinquent peers during group based treatment interventions and group anger control treatment, but nonetheless it recommends group social problem solving. In the guideline, it is concluded that there is relatively robust clinical evidence indicating that cognitive and behavioural interventions are moderately effective for adult offenders: ‘The GDG judged that it would be reasonable to conclude such interventions were likely to be effective for people with antisocial personality disorder’. However, delinquent behaviours and ASPD are not the same. Alcohol and drug use disorders are a serious problem in forensic patients. Based on relatively few studies the committee recommends that people with ASPD who are in institutional care and who misuse or are dependent on drugs or alcohol, should be referred to a substance use disorder focused therapeutic community. The Guideline on ASPD does little to address the issue of the therapeutic relationship. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
