AB  - [...]employers have a strong economic interest in pressing for health care delivery arrangements that integrate medical and mental health services and improve mental health status while reducing inappropriate use of medical care. From the perspective of employer-purchasers, they are headed in the right direction. Since the early 1990s members of the Washington Business Group on Health (WBGH), an organization of large private- and public-sector employers, have advanced a health system model in which consumers receive a full continuum of care from delivery systems that are clinically and fiscally accountable for outcomes and the health status of the enrolled population. The research concluded that employer plans with good access to outpatient mental health services have lower psychiatric disability claim costs than plans with more restrictive arrangements. [...]the researchers found that when front-line managers and supervisors play an active role in disability return-to-work efforts, claim rates were 50 percent lower.4 As a result of such studies, many employers are putting less emphasis on managing the medical benefit and more on early intervention, disability prevention and management, and return-to-work programs. [...]in aggregate, mental health benefit design drove inappropriate use of services.
