AB  - Six patients, resident in an old-age home and diagnosed to have a chronic brain syndrome, were treated with group psychotherapy and sensory stimulation in a program designed to alleviate social and emotional isolation. On the basis of screening tests and clinical examination this treatment was shown to be effective in alleviating those symptoms that had served as the basis for the original diagnosis. Thus, there is evidence that isolation in all its aspects—social, emotional, and physical—plays a major role in the development of psychiatric symptoms in the elderly, and that therapy designed to counteract isolation also will help to alleviate the symptoms. (9 ref) (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
