AB  - Background: The effectiveness of relapse prevention medications, such as extended release naltrexone (XR-NTX) and buprenorphine for opioid addiction in youths for the most part remain unknown. Objective(s): To examine the relationship between use of relapse prevention medications on engagement in young adults with Opioid Use Disorders (OUD) transitioning from residential to outpatient care having initiated buprenorphine, XR-NTX, or no medication at discharge from residential care. Method(s): This was a naturalistic study using retrospective chart review of (N = 92) serial admissions into an inpatient community treatment program of youth with OUD, referred to the outpatient component of the program. Result(s): Patients were mean age 19.8 (range 15-24), 65% male, 84% Caucasian, and discharged from inpatient care with either buprenorphine (60%) or XR-NTX (24%) or no medication (16%). The cohort represented 44% of the inpatient OUD population over a 9-month period, the rest of whom were referred elsewhere because of geography or preference. Among those receiving either medication, 73% initiated outpatient treatment compared to 53% of those discharged from inpatient on no medication, x (1, N = 92) = 18., p <.01. Of the combined medication group, 49% were retained in outpatient treatment at 8 weeks and 30% at 12 weeks, compared to 20% (t = 8.67, p<.01) and 20% (t = 5.0, p<.05) respectively in the no-medications group. There were no significant differences in initiation or retention between the two different medication groups. Conclusion(s): Youth treated with relapse prevention medication for OUD were more likely to initiate and be retained at 8 and 12 weeks in outpatient continuing care following inpatient discharge. Our results suggest that relapse prevention medications for OUD, including both buprenorphine and XR-NTX, can be effectively incorporated into inpatient care for youth with OUD. Additional research exploring use of relapse prevention medication as a clinical tool to facilitate treatment engagement among youths is warranted.
