AB  - Marketers create or increase product demand transposing conventional educational programs and celebrity endorsements to the domain of research; for example, Healy illustrates how psychiatric disorders are reified and their treatments promoted by clinical trials and ghostwritten articles in symposium proceedings and professional journals. Veena Das and Ranendra Das's ethnography in Delhi neighborhoods explores connections between practitioners and households, taking issue with terms such as self-medication and noncompliance, which, they argue, pathologize the actions of poor people and draw attention away from how "global and national policy, regional markets, and livelihoods" (p. 172) shape patterns of pharmaceutical use.
