AB  - Aims: Treatment burden refers to the demands of treatment and selfcare for chronic conditions and their impact on well-being. We tested known-groups validity and responsiveness to change of the Patient Experience with Treatment and Self-management (PETS, vs. 2.0), a measure of treatment burden. Method(s): A survey consisting of the PETS and other standardized measures was mailed at baseline and 12-month follow-up to a cohort of people diagnosed with multiple chronic conditions in southeast Minnesota, USA. A total of 365 people (mean age = 62 years, 64% female, median number of conditions = 5) completed surveys at both time points. Twelve month and change over time of the following six PETS scores were examined: workload/tasks (an aggregate score of the medical information, medications, medical appointments, and monitoring health scales), impact (an aggregate score of the activity limitations and exhaustion with self-care scales), and the diet, exercise, medical expenses, and healthcare services burden scales. Clinical anchors used to test validity included medication adherence (optimal vs. suboptimal), health literacy (optimal vs. suboptimal), change in self-efficacy (improve vs. decline), change in PROMIS global physical/mental health (improve vs. decline), number of conditions (2-4 vs. 5 +), and mental health diagnosis (yes vs. no). Independent samples t-tests were used to compare PETS scores across groups. Result(s): Suboptimal medication adherence and suboptimal health literacy were associated with worse 12-month burden scores in workload/tasks, impact, exercise, medical expenses, and healthcare services burden (P<0.05). Declines in selfefficacy at 12 months were associated with increased burden in workload/tasks, impact, diet, exercise, and medical expenses (P<0.05); declines in global physical and global mental health were associated with increased burden in workload/tasks, impact, and medical expenses (P<0.05). While there were few differences in burden scores across number of conditions, having a co-morbid mental health condition was associated with higher 12-month burden scores in workload/tasks, impact, exercise, medical expenses, and healthcare services (P<0.005). Conclusion(s): Analyses support the known-groups validity and responsiveness to change of prospective PETS scores. Future use of latent growth curve analysis might help identify trajectories of change in treatment burden and their predictors among those with chronic conditions.
