AB  - Presents an evaluation of a parent and family support intervention for 32 mothers and 17 fathers of children (aged 3–14 yrs) with severe disabilities. Using an experimental design, 2 randomly assigned groups of parents were compared: One group received a modest level of support consisting of respite care and case management; the other group received an intensive intervention that consisted of stress management and parenting skills training, support groups, and additional community-based respite care. Separate multivariate analysis of covariance (MANCOVA) analyses were conducted for mothers and fathers. Mothers showed significant improvement on measures of depression and anxiety, with more intensive support group members achieving a clinically significant improvement. A power analysis revealed large treatment effects for fathers as well as mothers. Analyses of 1-yr follow-up data revealed that treatment gains were maintained for mothers. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
