AB  - Purpose: The discrepancies between the evidence-based recommendations made in international osteoarthritis (OA) management guidelines and the actual care people receive are well-documented. Coordinated OA management programs (OAMPs) have been implemented to address this evidence-practice gap. Participants of OAMPs are often prescribed multimodal treatments that include lifestyle interventions, particularly weight loss and exercise. Previous studies suggest that participant confidence, knowledge, attitudes and motivation play a critical role in the health behaviour changes related to lifestyle interventions. These potential determinants of health behaviour change can be measured using the Patient Activation Measure-13 (PAM-13). The primary aim of this study was to examine the relationship between baseline patient activation and short-term symptomatic outcomes (pain and function) following participation in an OAMP. This study also examined the relationships between symptomatic outcomes and sociodemographic, comorbidity count, functional performance and total joint arthroplasty waitlist status. Another secondary aim was to quantify the change in PAM-13 scores following participation in an OAMP. Method(s): A clinical cohort of consecutive participants with symptomatic and radiographic knee OA was recruited from the Osteoarthritis Chronic Care program (OACCP) in New South Wales, Australia. The objectives of the OACCP are to: reduce OA pain, increase functional abilities and quality of life of participants through the provision of tailored interventions delivered by a multidisciplinary team and referral to appropriate community-based services. At the initial assessment, an experienced physical therapist/exercise physiologist provided participants with education about their OA and associated comorbidities, set patient-oriented goals, prescribed behavioural change strategies and an individually tailored exercise program. Participants also attended multidisciplinary clinics for consultations with a rheumatologist, dietitian, occupational therapist, social worker or orthotist according to their individual clinical needs. One-hundred-and-ten OACCP participants with knee OA were assessed at 0, 12 and 26-weeks. Sociodemographic variables (sex, age, employment status) comorbid conditions, joint arthroplasty waitlist status, timed-up-and-go (TUG), PAM-13, Depression Anxiety Stress Scale (DASS) and Knee injury and Osteoarthritis Outcome Score (KOOS) were collected. The WOMAC pain and function scores were calculated from the KOOS scores. Multivariable linear regression was used to examine the relationships between WOMAC pain and function change scores at 12- and 26-weeks, and baseline PAM-13, sociodemographic, comorbidity, functional performance variables and waitlist status. First, an unadjusted analysis was performed using simple linear regression. Second, baseline PAM-13 scores and variables with a P-value of <= 0.1 were included as candidate variables in multivariable models which were built using a backward selection, forced entry technique. At each step, the least significant variable (p-value<0.05) was removed from the model and the beta coefficients were checked; if the coefficients changed by >= 10% the variable was retained in the model as a confounder, if not it was removed. Result(s): Complete 12 and 26-week data were available for 89 and 66 participants respectively: mean age 67.1 years; 75% female; 14% waitlisted for total joint arthroplasty. Baseline PAM-13 was not significantly associated with pain (beta=0.10 (95%CI-0.12, 0.31) p=0.50) or function (beta=0.08 (95%CI-0.11, 0.28), p=0.40) at 12 weeks and there were no significant relationships at 26-weeks either. Employment status and TUG were independently associated with changes in 12-week WOMAC pain and function scores (tables 1 and 2). No variables were significantly associated with changes in 26-week WOMAC pain or function. Following 26-weeks of the OACCP, PAM-13 scores improved by 5.8 points (n= 66, 95%CI 1.89, 9.78). Conclusion(s): This study suggests that PAM-13 may not be a useful tool to predict changes in symptoms following the OACCP. However, the significant improvement in mean PAM-13 scores demonstrated following the OACCP were encouraging given that the main aim of the program is to educate and support participants to self-manage their OA. Baseline TUG and employment status were significantly associated with changes in pain and function. These variables should be examined further as potential predictors of outcomes of OAMPs. [Figure presented] [Figure presented]Copyright © 2019
