AB  - Background: An urban Emergency Department collaborated with a non-profit community addiction program-FAVOR (Faces and Voices of Recovery). FAVOR is a no-cost program that provides services for addiction recovery.The program utilizes peer recovery coaches have been in recovery for at least 2 years, and are trained and credentialed. A protocol was put in place, to contact these coaches for all potential opioid overdoses. The coaches were on call and came to the emergency department 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. It was thought that evaluating and engaging these patients in the Emergency Department after an opioid overdose would result in higher rates of recovery. Method(s): An IRB-approved project began enrolling patients in January 2018. Patients who were identified as accidental opioid overdoses were enrolled. Enrollment was voluntary and informed consent was obtained. A recovery coach from FAVOR was contacted and met with these patients in the ED. Patients and their families were counseled by these peer recovery coaches and offered treatment. Patients were still offered treatment and services from FAVOR even if they opted out of the study. The coaches would offer a variety of options including group treatment, recovery coaching, family support services and transportation. After the initial encounter, coaches would contact and re-engange these patients the next day by phone or in person, and continue this enganement at greater intervals after. Result(s): As of September 2018, 150 patients were evalutated, 146 (97.3%) agreed to services. Ninety-one (66%) remained active in the recovery process, and 61% of those who agreed to treatment were linked to other services. Patients could be linked to multiple services-including AA/NA/12-step, FAVOR meetings, Inpatient Rehab, MAT, active recovery coaching. 8.2% have returned to the hospital, and 1 patient has died due to overdose. Conclusion(s): This model has proven to be quite successful. It is thought that those with previous opiate addiction serve to be effective coaches and a key part of the recovery process, and perhaps a serious event such as overdose may drive patients to agree to recovery. FAVOR is supported by community donations, and is no cost to the health system or patient. Funding source: South Carolina Department of Alcohol and Other Drug Abuse Services.
