AB  - Reviews the myths and facts concerning attention deficit disorder (ADD), with or without hyperactivity, a common but misunderstood and frequently underdiagnosed condition in college students. It may affect students' academic and social success and emotional development. ADD is an impairment of cortical regulation of activity and impulse control that is often hereditary. It may increase the risk of drug abuse, delinquency, incarceration, job failure, marital discord, and divorce. Methods for treating ADD in college students are reviewed, and a multimodal program is described that combines medication; individual, family, and group psychotherapy; career counseling; cognitive control; electronic prostheses (e.g., tape recorders, white-noise generators); and special accommodations in the college classroom. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
