AB  - Telephone-based referral-care management (TBR-CM) intervention on psychiatric health outcomes was evaluated. Primary care patients (n= 113) at the Philadelphia Veterans Affairs Medical Center completed a phone-based psychiatric interview. Patients were randomized into TBR-CM usual care or one of three intervention groups. Baseline and 6-month interviews were used to obtain study data. Results indicated that there was improvement in mental health functioning (p< 0.0001), depression (p< 0.0001), and binge (p< 0.0332) outcomes over time at the 6-month interview (78% retention). However, there was no change observed for physical health functioning and drinks/week outcomes. Patients who are exposed to the intervention have similar health outcomes as patients in usual care. In conclusion, the TBR-CM intervention does not lead to relatively improved psychiatric health outcomes. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Telemedicine & e-Health is the property of Mary Ann Liebert, Inc. and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
