AB  - This review summarizes major findings concerning cognitive deficits in chronic abusers of alcohol and other psychoactive substances and explores ways to incorporate this information into treatment plans. The psychiatric sequelae of cognitive impairments can include irritability, low frustration tolerance, impulsivity, depression, paranoia, and denial. Recommended treatment approaches include a formal neuropsychological assessment, the integration of data on cognitive impairment into the client's treatment plan, involvement in a 12-stop support group, a therapeutic intervention that addresses cognitive decrements, and a highly structured coping skills group. A case example of a 45-yr-old man illustrates the potential impact of impaired cognitive functioning on treatment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
