AB  - A group of mothers of retarded children were encouraged to grieve the 'loss' of the normal children that had existed in their fantasies during pregnancy. Interventions were based upon the theoretical framework of the grief process as described by Engel and consisted of helping the mothers to identify their losses and recognize that their feelings were a normal reaction to such losses. Over the eight months of group therapy, the group proceeded from denial, to developing awareness, and finally to the stage of restitution. During this process, members of the group assumed increasing responsibility for making interventions supportive to other members of the group.
