AB  - Conjoint therapies for problem drinking and AUD (i.e., those involving spouses in treatment in addition to the identified problem drinker) are more effective in reducing drinking and promoting abstinence than individual therapies. The mechanisms through which the spouse influence occurs, however, are not well understood. The purpose of the present secondary analyses was to examine the role of social control - that is, specific strategies used by spouses to influence client drinking - in predicting reductions in client drinking over the course of outpatient group treatment. Data were drawn from the pretreatment and treatment end assessments of a larger drinking reduction treatment study formale problem drinkers (N = 64) and their female spouses/partners. Treatment involved participation in 10 weekly 2-hr group sessions. Clients reported their own drinking and their spouses' use of social control strategies at pretreatment and treatment end. Analyses examined the relationship of spouse social control strategies before and during treatment with changes in client drinking (percent days abstinent [PDA], percent days drinking heavily [PDH]) from pretreatment to treatment end. All analyses controlled for study experimental conditions; this was not significant in any model. Findings were mixed regarding the effect of negative social control strategies. Spouse punishment of drinking prior to treatment (b = 13.29, p < 0.05), and complaints about drinking during treatment (b = 11.943, p < 0.05), predicted increases in PDA. However, spouse withdrawal prior to treatment (b = -23.90, p < 0.05), and criticizing the drinker during treatment (b = -10.39, p < 0.05), predicted decreases in PDA. In contrast, spouse use of positive social control tactics was consistently associated with reductions in drinking. Spouse efforts to support sobriety during treatment predicted increases in PDA (b = 10.04, p < 0.05), and their provision of instrumental and emotional support during treatment predicted decreases in PDH (b = -5.57, p < 0.05). Findings demonstrate beneficial effects of both positive and certain kinds of negative social control strategies used by the spouse. Negative social control strategies focused on the drinking behavior, but not the drinker himself, appeared to promote increases in abstinence over the course of treatment. Additionally, the use of more positive social control tactics during treatment played a role in helping clients reduce heavy drinking and increase abstinence.
