AB  - The United States is extremely disproportionate when it comes to health care spending per capita. Only 5% of the country's population is responsible for 50% of annual spending on health care-related costs. New Jersey's Camden Coalition of Healthcare Providers introduced their core model to find patients who utilize health care resources at a disproportionally higher rate than the average person, known as "superutilizers." This targeted identification is known as "hotspotting." The Coalition's Camden Core Model assigns comprehensive care teams and resources for each patient after their hospital stay has ended. This study, involving the Camden Coalition aimed to assess the program's impact on readmission rate for superutilizers. This randomized controlled trial assigned 800 superutilizers to the coalition's program or to standard care. Selected patients must have been hospitalized at least once in the past 6 months, be between the ages of 18 and 80 years, experience 2 or more chronic conditions, and experience 2 or more conditions from this list: use of 5 or more outpatient medications, barriers to accessing services, lack of social support, current mental health condition, drug use, or homelessness. Hospital readmission within 180 days after discharge was the trial's primary outcome under observation. The superutilizers assigned to the interventional program group experienced a 62.3% readmission rate, whereas the control group with standard care experienced a 61.7% readmission rate over 180 days following hospital discharge. The difference in outcomes between groups was insignificant (95% confidence interval, -5.97 to 7.61). However, analysis of readmissions prior to and after enrollment showed a decline in the intervention group's hospital admissions, but a similar decline was seen in the control group. This randomized trial did not find involvement in the coalition's interventional program to be significant in impacting the 180-day readmission rate. These findings likely show regression to the mean and the difficulty in reducing readmission rates due to highly complex social and medical variables in the United States.
