AB  - Purpose: This study assessed the extent of different types of abuse, repeated & multiple abuse experiences among women aged 60 & older, & their effects on the women's self-reported health. Design and Methods: A cross-sectional study of a clinical sample of 842 community-dwelling women aged 60 & older completed a telephone survey about type & frequency of abuse, self-reported health status & health conditions, & demographic characteristics. Bivariate & multivariate analyses were performed using SPSS 11.5 & STATA 7.0. Results: Nearly half of the women had experienced at least one type of abuse -- psychological/emotional, control, threat, physical, or sexual -- since turning 55 years old. Sizable proportions were victims of repeat abuse. Many women experienced multiple types of abuse & experienced abuse often. Abused older women were significantly more likely to report more health conditions than those who were not abused. Women who experienced psychological/emotional abuse -- alone, repeatedly, or with other types of abuse -- had significantly increased odds of reporting bone or joint problems, digestive problems, depression or anxiety, chronic pain, & high blood pressure or heart problems. Implications: It is important that health care & service providers acknowledge psychological/emotional, control, threat, physical, & sexual abuse against older women & understand their health implications. In addition, it is important for providers to be trained in both aging & domestic violence services & resources. Tables, References. Adapted from the source document.
