AB  - Background: This study describes the implementation of medication treatment for youth with opioid dependence in a community-treatment program, and the association between medication use and treatment outcomes. Method(s): Data were abstracted retrospectively from clinical charts of 133 patients admitted between July 4 and October 1 to the adolescent/young adult outpatient opioid track at Mountain Manor Treatment Center in Baltimore. Attendance, urine drug test results and documented self-report of drug use were examined from intake through 26 weeks postadmission. Result(s): Patients were 54% male, 94% Caucasian, mean age 18.2 (range 13.8-20.9). Although the mean retention until initial dropout (defined as 2 weeks without any treatment contact) was 9.6 weeks, 49% of patients returned to treatment after dropout, with 1.9 mean episodes of treatment and mean cumulative retention of 13.5 weeks. A total of 61% took relapse prevention medications (39% buprenorphine, 19% extended release naltrexone, and 3% oral naltrexone) and 39%were treatedwithout medications. Mean cumulative attendance during the first 26 weeks after admissionwas 9.7weeks for the total sample, 11.3weeks for those on any relapse prevention medication, and 7.3 weeks for those on no medications. Mean cumulative time without opioid use (combining self-report and urine testing) was 9.8 weeks for the whole sample, 11.5 weeks for those on any relapse prevention medication versus 7.0 weeks for those on no medication (p = .0003). Conclusion(s): Medication treatment for adolescent/ young adult opioid dependence is feasible in a communitytreatment setting. Use of medications for relapse prevention seems to be associated with increased retention and decreased opioid use in this population in this small, nonrandomized sample.
