AB  - Background: Lofexidine HCl (LFX) is an a-2 adrenergic receptor agonist originally developed as an anti-hypertensive agent. LFX has been developed as a non-narcotic treatment for the management of opioid withdrawal and is approved for this indication in the UK and other countries, but not USA. Earlier controlled studies of lofexidine have evaluated its safety and efficacy in patients undergoing withdrawal from short-acting opioids in an inpatient setting and have established a safe and effective dose range of 2.4 mg to 3.2 mg daily. This abstract provides details of an open label tolerability study. The primary objective of this study was to continue to investigate the safety of LFX when administered for at least 7 days in a variety of clinical scenarios in both inpatient and outpatient settings. Efficacy was also assessed. Method(s): This was a phase 3, multi-center, open label study. The planned starting dose was 3.2 mg daily (0.8 mg QID), which could be lowered to 2.4 mg daily (0.6 mg QID), if required, based on clinical judgement. Efficacy (as assessed by the SOWSGossop scale and COWS), adverse events, and vital signs (systolic and diastolic blood pressure and pulse rate) were analyzed by 3 treatment goal categories: withdrawal from short-acting opioids, withdrawal from long-acting opioids and agonist-assisted withdrawal. Result(s): A total of 286 subjects received at least 1 dose of LFX and comprise the safety population. Safety populations were 189, 18 and 79 for short-acting, long-acting and agonist-assisted treatment goal categories respectively. Among the 286 subjects exposed to study drug during Days 1 to 7, the most frequently reported treatment emergent AEs were insomnia (49.7% of subjects), anxiety (37.1% of subjects), orthostatic hypotension (31.8% of subjects), hypotension (29.0% of subjects), and bradycardia (25.2% of subjects). The vast majority of the hypotensive and bradycardia events were considered treatment-related, whereas the vast majority of insomnia and anxiety events were considered to be related to opioid withdrawal. Mean seated and standing systolic blood pressure (SBP) decreased from screening, with the magnitude of change greater for standing values versus seated values. The greatest mean decreases on Day 1 through Day 7 in SBP ranged from 7 to 17 mmHg below screening and the magnitude of the mean change decreased over time. The changes in diastolic blood pressure followed a similar profile as SBP but of lesser magnitude. A total of 168/286 subjects (58.7%) completed the study (i.e., took at least 1 dose of study drug on Day 7). Completers in each treatment goal group were 94/189 (49.7%), 15/18 (83.3%) and 59/79 (74.7%) for short-acting opioid withdrawal, long-acting opioid withdrawal, and agonist-assisted withdrawal groups, respectively. Although there was no placebo group for comparison the mean SOWSGossop and COWS scores generally decreased over the treatment period for all 3 groups. Conclusion(s): Significant numbers of subjects completed the study, tolerated LFX, and saw a reduction in withdrawal symptoms over the treatment period. Summary: Lofexidine in doses ranging from 2.4 to 3.2 mg/day was generally well tolerated. Reported AEs were primarily related to known, hypotensive effects of lofexidine and symptoms of opioid withdrawal. Lofexidine is safe and useful as a non-opioid-agonist option for the treatment of symptoms of opioid withdrawal, and is well tolerated both on its own and in combination with an agonist.
