AB  - Compared phenomenology, psychosocial correlates, and treatment seeking in DSM-III-R major depression and dysthymia among 3,287 adolescents (aged 11–20 yrs) in France, who were given a self-report questionnaire. 205 Ss were interviewed with a structured diagnostic schedule, and 49 Ss with major depression (MD), 21 Ss with dysthymia, and 135 controls were compared. Nearly 30% of controls had at least one current symptom of depression. Patterns of affective symptoms were similar in MD and dysthymia but significant differences emerged in comorbid conditions and stressor at onset. Experiences of loss during the prior 12 mo were associated with both forms of affective disorder, while poor family relationships were specific correlates of dysthymia. Peer relationships and pathological behaviors did not differ between depressed Ss and controls, Although psychosocial functioning was significantly impaired in both groups of depressed Ss, treatment seeking was limited to 34.7% for MD Ss and 23.8% for dysthymic Ss. Results indicate that MD and dysthymia in adolescence are equally severe but may have distinct patterns in associated factors. Despite free access to health care, the rate of treatment seeking for mood disorders in France is similar to that reported in US studies. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
