AB  - This exploratory-descriptive study explored the relationship among pre-treatment and in-treatment variables, the length of stay in treatment, and the progress made in treatment by a group of male recidivist alcoholics without prior stable residences who were discharged from an extended care residential substance abuse treatment facility. More specifically, the study describes what information routinely gathered during the pre-admission screening and intake interview or during the resident's stay in treatment is associated with predicting the progress a resident would make while in treatment. The records of 100 alcoholic men who were discharged from an extended care residential alcoholism program during 1993-95 were studied. This sample of residents were characterized by means of various demographic, background, substance abuse, and in-treatment variables. Data analysis, employing non-parametric statistics, explored associations among the pre-treatment and in-treatment variables, the length of stay in treatment, and the measures of progress in treatment. Chi-square analyses, using 2 x 2 or 2 x 3 contingency tables, were used to investigate the effects of the independent categorical variables on the dependent measures of progress in treatment. The pre-treatment and in-treatment variables include: social/personal characteristics, physical health, comorbid psychiatric diagnoses, extent of alcohol and drug use, social support and involvement, length of stay, behavior within the residence, and experiences outside the residence. The measures of progress in treatment were: substance use at discharge, securing employment, and the establishment of stable housing. The study found that one quarter of the residents made progress on all three measures of progress in treatment and only one quarter of the residents were unable to make progress on any measure of progress in treatment. Background variables had little predictive value whereas measures of substance abuse severity and (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
