AB  - Research Objectives: To investigate the social determinants of health needs of individuals with physical disabilities admitted to a primary care medical home, along with potential benefits of addressing those needs.DesignA retrospective chart review for patients referred to home and community-based services (HCBS) who did not qualify through a Medicaid Waiver. Setting(s): Fully accessible community-based primary care medical home for persons with physical disabilities supported in part by a Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Health Care Innovation Award. Participant(s): Fifty-one medical home patients referred for HCBS services: 23 Male, 29 female; average age 49.3 years; 82% had a diagnosis of brain injury, other diagnosis included spinal cord injury, amputation, blindness, fibromyalgia, and degenerative disc disease. Intervention(s): Participants were seen in their homes by HCBS providers an average of 8 times a month, addressing participant-identified problems over an average of 20 months. The most common problems identified were housing, problem solving, interpersonal relationships and communication, safety, orientation and mobility, personal and health care management, time management, homemaking or PCA, purposeful activity, household management, and nutritional management.Main Outcome Measure(s)Depression as measured by PHQ-9, and patient perceived health as measured by Healthy Days. Result(s): Patients in HCBS had an average PHQ-9 score of 11.3 points at enrollment, improved to 9.9 points over 5 months; average health days score of 8.23 days, improved to 12.7 days over 5 months. Persons referred for HCBS had higher average depression scores, lower healthy days scores, and improved more than patients in the medical home not referred to HCBS. Conclusion(s): Addressing social determinants of health in the home or community setting for individuals with disabilities is an important intervention, and may help improve both mental and perceived physical health.
