AB  - It is widely believed that outcomes assessment can help to determine what services should be covered by insurance, how managed care performance should be measured, and where health care costs can be saved. Expectations of outcomes assessment are very high, yet there is little experience in using outcomes information to make any of these major decisions. This is especially true in mental health and substance abuse treatment, an area in which there appears to be few, if any, accepted methods to measure the effectiveness of treatment. To address this gap in outcomes assessment, the National Alliance for the Mentally Ill and The Johns Hopkins University initiated the Outcomes Roundtable in late 1994.
