AB  - Developed under the auspices of the PTSD Treatment Guidelines Task Force of the International Society for Traumatic Stress Studies, this tightly edited work is the definitive best-practice reference for mental health clinicians caring for any trauma population. Leading authorities systematically evaluate the status of established and emerging treatments and help the reader determine which approaches to use, and for whom. The revised and expanded second edition incorporates important advances in the field, including a wealth of new data on child and adolescent therapies. Following an introductory overview, the book discusses assessment and diagnosis of PTSD in both adults and children. The core of the book then examines the current empirical literature on major therapeutic approaches. Coverage encompasses early intervention and prevention strategies; psychosocial therapies; group, couple, and family treatments; and pharmacotherapy. Six new chapters are devoted to child and adolescent interventions, the focus of a rapidly growing number of studies. Another new chapter addresses PTSD treatment in the presence of comorbid psychiatric disorders. Next, the Task Force recommendations are presented in the form of succinct treatment guidelines that the reader can use for quick reference. Finally, the integrative conclusion explores ways to optimize care for individual patients. It offers insightful guidance to help clinicians select the most suitable therapy, set clear goals, and overcome frequently encountered obstacles. Key treatment questions that remain unanswered are also identified. Comprehensive and user friendly, this is an essential resource for anyone who treats clients or patients with PTSD, including clinical psychologists, psychiatrists, social workers, nurses, and counselors. It serves as a state-of-the-art text for clinical graduate students. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
