AB  - In this paper, I examine the varying ways in which the health of three groups, poor people, African Americans and women, has been framed over the last century by advocates and public policies. At the diagnostic level, I examine the degree to which the health of various groups has been framed in terms of the special needs of particular groups or in terms of inequalities between groups. At the prognostic level, I examine claims about the causes and solutions of the main health problems and inequalities of racial and ethnic minorities. The main solutions offered have included medical treatment, medical insurance, biomedical research, wellness and prevention, and non-health social welfare policies. At the motivational level, I examine claims about the efficacy of various frames for motivating action among various audiences, including policy-makers. Advocates for the health of racial and ethnic groups have debated whether they should focus on inequalities in health status, medical insurance or medical treatment; whether policies should address race, class or both; whether policies should focus on single or multiple groups; and whether medical inequalities should be framed as instances of racial bias or medical error. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Conference Papers -- American Sociological Association is the property of American Sociological Association and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
