AB  - In 1990, some $54 billion was spent on alcohol/drug abuse and mental health treatment. These expenditures were concentrated in the area of inpatient psychiatric care and on persons with severe mental health and substance abuse problems. The data on expenditure patterns for mental health and substance abuse suggest that successful health care reform in this area must implement mechanisms for controlling inpatient utilization and managing the care of persons with the most severe disorders. There are 3 important challenges for health care reform that emerge from the data: 1. The heavy reliance on inpatient mental health/substance abuse care suggests that successful cost control depends on mechanisms to manage and control inpatient use. 2. Full integration of mental health/substance abuse care means developing systems within health plans that can efficiently manage the complex and extensive treatment needs of people with severe mental health/substance abuse illnesses. 3. A mental health/substance abuse benefit needs to be implemented within the framework of managed competition that is fair to all people.
