AB  - Self-reported levels of depression, hopelessness, and overall levels of symptoms were examined before and after treatment in a partial hospital/intensive outpatient non-standard Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) skills group and in a general psychotherapy group (GEN). The sample was composed of 43 Caucasian subjects between the ages of 18 and 78 who were drawn from a rural community hospital population. 19 received DBT, 24 received GEN. The majority of patients were female, not living alone, unemployed, and recently hospitalized for psychiatric treatment. The sample was predominantly depressed; many patients also had comorbid Axis I and Axis II disorders, as well as concurrent physical illness. Self-report measures used were the Beek Depression Inventory (BDI), Beck Hopelessness Scale (BHS), and Target Symptom Scale (TSS), a Likert-type measure modeled after the DBT diary card which asked patients to rate severity of self-harm urges and actions, suicidal ideation, misery, and depression. Overall, patients showed modest improvement on the TSS (p <.007) and BDI (p <.026); only the TSS finding was significant with the application of Bonferroni's stringent correction ( p <.016). DBT patients as a group showed improvements from pre- to post-test on the TSS (p <.01), but not on the other measures. Comparison of DBT and GEN subjects revealed no differences in levels of improvement from pre- to post-test, although a greater number of DBT patients than GEN patients improved on the TSS from pre- to post-test. DBT subjects reported much higher levels of initial symptom severity than did GEN patients on the BDI (p <.0005), BHS ( p <.00004), and TSS (p <.029), and they were in treatment longer than were GEN subjects (p <.032). No post-test differences were found between the DBT and GEN when pre-test scores were controlled. The fact that DBT patients received a higher dose of treatment and were more symptomatic than non-DBT subjects makes it difficult to conclude that the abbreviated, modified version of DBT studied here was less effective than a general group treatment protocol. More research is needed to examine the effectiveness of non-standard versions of DBT as they are conducted in clinical settings. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
