AB  - This investigation tests E. R. Hilgard's neodissociation theory of hypnosis by contrasting pain relief procedures using distraction and dissociation. Volunteers (N >0) were familiarized with hypnosis using standardized procedures. Then, hypnotizability level was assessed before treatment using the Harvard Group Scale of Hypnotic Susceptibility: Form A, and the individually administered Stanford Hypnotic Susceptibility Scale Form: C. High (N = 10) and low (N = 10) hypnotizable subjects were studied to compare the effects of hypnotic analgesia and distraction on cold pressor pain and electroencephalographic (EEG) responses. Subjects were exposed to a cold pressor test of 60 seconds ice water hand/arm immersion in three counterbalanced treatment conditions: waking, distraction, and hypnotic analgesia. A new distraction procedure developed for this study was intended to better separate hypnotic and distraction treatments than previous studies by reducing the likelihood of high hypnotizables entering hypnosis spontaneously. Cold pressor pain findings showed that highs responded with significantly greater pain relief than lows in the hypnosis condition. Lows showed no differences in reported pain in any condition. Quantitative EEG data analysis of 19 monopolar sites according to the international 10-20 system was performed using Fast Fourier technique for peak to peak magnitudes. High hypnotizables showed significantly greater (p <) high theta (5.5-) than low hypnotizables at the parietal P3 and occipital O1 sites during hypnotic analgesia but not during distraction or waking rest conditions. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
