AB  - In the fall of 1996, Westborough State Hospital began a pilot psychosocial rehabilitation program called the Social Competency Model in one of its unlocked, extended care houses, House 1. The aim of the program was to improve social functioning in the most chronically disabled persons with mental illness. The long-term goal was to prepare these patients, who had spent much of their adult lives institutionalized, for the opportunity to live successfully in the community. During the period of the pilot program at House 1, a sister house, House 2, continued to provide more traditional clinical care to its residents. While much research has been done on psychosocial rehabilitation and the factors believed necessary to improve patient functioning in the community, relatively little research has been conducted on the effect of a specific psychosocial rehabilitation model in a state hospital. In this study, 9 chronically mentally ill residents who received Social Competency Model (SCM) care, were compared to 9 other chronically mentally ill residents who received standard clinical care (House 2) in a number of areas of patient functioning to assess whether differences were found between groups. Using the Mann-Whitney U, significant findings suggested that persons with chronic mental illness who had received care under the SCM had fewer and shorter respites (temporary transfers to a locked unit) for dangerous behavior, were less dependent on psychiatric medication for behavioral control, had higher attendance for group treatment, and showed a greater improvement on measures of overall functioning over time than their clinical care peers. These results suggest that the SCM is a valuable model for treatment for the most desocialized, chronic mentally ill, hospitalized individuals. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
