AB  - COVID-19 in a current global pandemic that has been superimposed onto of the existing OUD crisis. Individuals with opioid use disorder (OUD)/substance use disorder (SUD) are at risk for COVID-19 infection, particularly those who require treatment in residential, congregate living facilities. It is important to understand the presentation, challenges, and management options for addressing COVID-19 infections in such treatment settings. Background(s): During the current COVID-19 pandemic, understanding how to manage infected patients with SUD in a residential treatment setting is important. This poster reports on early experience including strategies for screening and testing patients and staff, challenges with social distancing in congregate treatment environment and consideration for use of MAT in infected patients. Objective(s): Screening and testing for COVID-19 infection in patients with SUD. Explore buprenorphine management in patients with COVID-19. Discuss challenges of infection control in residential treatment settings. Method(s): A retrospective case review of individuals being treated for SUD in residential treatment facility who contracted COVID-19. Screening and testing protocols were employed and efforts to maintain community treatment environment while practicing social distancing were made. Challenges around isolation and SUD management were examined. Patient isolation and monitoring for temperature and respiratory status were maintained for symptomatic individuals regardless of COVID-19 testing status. Result(s): Ten patients on buprenorphine maintenance for OUD contracted COVID-19. No changes to MAT dose were needed with no changes in respiratory status observed. Eight SUD cases. No increases in anxiety/depression were seen in any of the cases. Challenges with testing accuracy and difficulty in maintaining adequate isolation of infected individuals due to the congregate living environment were encountered. Conclusion(s): Isolation of COVID-19 patients in residential treatment setting is challenging however those with mildmod COVID do not appear to require significant adjustment to medication plan including buprenorphine. Strict protocols for screening and identification of individuals with COVID- 19 in congregate treatment environments is necessary to manage COVID-19 cases. Scientific Significance: SUD in the context of COVID-19 is novel. This poster contributes to learning about how to manage SUD patients with COVID-19 infections in residential treatment settings.
