AB  - Introduction: Stress plays a key role in the pathophysiology of schizophrenia [1]. The conceptual model of vulnerability-stress-protective factors has been the basis for psychiatric rehabilitation of people with severe mental illness (SMI) [2]. According to the psychophysiological coherence model different emotions are reflected in state-specific patterns in the heart's rhythms (Cardiac Coherence-CC) [3]. Higher coherence reflects increased para-sympathetic coherence and less stress, while lower coherence depends on sympathetic activity [4]. We compared the cardiac coherence in a group of 22 stabilized patients with SMI living in a halfway house with 31 non-psychotic outpatients and people from the community. Material(s) and Method(s): The patients live altogether in a half way house where they participate in a psychiatric rehabilitation program and receive their medication (antipsychotic medication, sedatives, and other psychiatric medications). Many of them have permanent psychotic symptoms. The control group individuals are essentially people without psychotic features. And none of them is under antipsychotic treatment. Tests: Clinical Global Impressions Scale and subscales: Severity (CGI-s) considered the present state and Change (CGI-c) took into account changes since entering the program. Morning cortisol: blood samples were taken at 8 AM. TSST (Trier Social StressTest) [6] was used to measure cardiac coherence. Psychological stress was provoked asking the subjects to subtract in sequence 13 beginning at 1022. The subject has to restart when he/she gave a wrong answer. Physical stress was provoked with the cold-pressor test (CPT) [5]. Blood pressure and cardiac frequency were taken at baseline and at the end of both tests and of both recovery periods. Statistical Analysis. Mann-Whitney U Test for independent samples was performed to test the hypothesis. Results are shown in Table 1. Patients showed higher reaction to physical than to psychological stress and better recovery to physical stress than to psychological stress. Controls showed more reaction to psychological stress and the same rate of recovery to physical and to psychological stresses. Cardiac coherence in patients showed better recovery to physical stress than to psychological stress. Controls showed no differences. We have found significant statistical differences (p > 0.05) between patients and controls in low and medium cardiac coherence during the psychological stress test, and in systolic tension, both in basal line and after psychological test. There were statistical significant differences both in systolic and diastolic pressure between patients and controls after the psychological test. These differences also repeated after the physical stress test, with differences in cardiac frequency. There were also differences in diastolic pressure during the physical test. Discussion(s): We have not found higher sensitivity to stress in psychotic patients versus controls in this sample. All patients are stabilized and medicated, medicine intake is controlled, and they live in a stable environment with a low level of expressed emotion. This sample is small to give definitely conclusions, but it shows that this kind of institution allows even severe SMI individuals to live a normalized life in the community without stress or hospitalization.
