AB  - Background: As the opioid epidemic continues in the United States, there has been an increase in treatment for those suffering from opioid use disorder. Not only have Opioid Treatment Programs (OTPs) increased from approximately 1100 in 2003 to almost 1500 by the end of 2016, the number of patients receiving methadone increased from about 227,000 in 2003 to over 350,000 in 2015 as well. Furthermore, physicians who obtain specialized training may prescribe buprenorphine in an outpatient, non-OTP setting. However, only 21% of facilities without OTPs were providing buprenorphine in 2015 (increased from 5% in 2003) compared with 58% of OTPs offering buprenorphine in 2015 (increased from 11% in 2003). A significant hurdle for facilities without OTPs, such as primary-care practices, from offering buprenorphine therapy may be the difficulty with coordinating medication-assisted treatment with appropriate psychological services in order to maximize treatment effectiveness. Objective(s): The aim of the study was to describe an effective practice model incorporating in-office ancillary cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) with medication-assisted treatment (MAT) for opioid use disorder in the primarycare setting. Method(s): A retrospective review of patients receiving medication-assisted treatment in 2017 and 2018 in a multicenter primary-care setting with ancillary cognitive-behavioral therapy was performed at Alpha Care Medical office locations in Delaware. Inclusion criteria were diagnosis of opioid-use disorder and treatment with prescription buprenorphine for opioid-use disorder. Exclusion criteria were failure to meet 1 hour of cognitive-behavioral therapy permonth, obtaining nonancillary psychological counseling services at an outside facility, discontinuation of treatment because of concern for diversion, and treatment start date after March 1, 2018. Patients were all initially evaluated and inducted on buprenorphine treatment at the treatment facility Alpha CareMedical through the guidance of a DATA2000-waivered physician. Once patients were successfully inducted on buprenorphine, they transitioned to the stabilization and then the maintenance phase with weekly or biweekly in-office ancillary cognitive- behavioral therapy with a Clinical Social Worker or a Professional Counsellor. The primary outcome was treatment retention at 30, 90, and 180 days. Secondary outcomes were maintenance dose of buprenorphine at 30, 90, and 180 days and frequency of in-office ancillary cognitive-behavioral therapy. Result(s): During the study period, 135 patients were included. Of these, 93 were excluded because of meeting exclusion criteria. Forty-two (n=42) patients remained in the study group. The retention rates at days 30, 90, and 180 were 83.3%, 76.2%, and 66.7%, respectively. Number of patients at maintenance dose of 4 mg daily buprenorphine at days 30, 90, and 180 were 0, 0, and 3, respectively; maintenance dose of 8mg daily buprenorphine at days 30, 90, and 180 were 5, 6, and 10, respectively; maintenance dose of 12mg daily buprenorphine at days 30, 90, and 180 were 11, 18, and 16, respectively; maintenance dose of 16mg daily buprenorphine at days 30, 90, and 180 were 22, 17, and 13, respectively; maintenance dose of 24mg daily buprenorphine at days 30, 90, and 180 were 4, 1, and 0, respectively. Conclusion(s): Integrating CBT with MAT can be a successful and effective treatment model for primary-care practices.
