AB  - Early life experiences influence brain development and have significant implications on future health and developmental outcomes. Children raised in environments with significant social stressors, limited stimulation and lack of exposure to positive interactions are likely to have delays in language, vocabulary, and social skills - all factors critical for school readiness. CenteringParenting, a two generation intervention, supports healthy parent-child interactions and early learning through education and experiential learning within the framework of group well child visits. Participants are actively engaged in their child's assessment at every visit: tracking their growth, development, immunizations and oral health. Health assessments, immunizations and developmental screenings follow the AAP Bright FuturesTM periodicity schedule. Their active participation makes parents intimately aware of their child's health status, developmental milestones, and growth & development. The groups explore a broad range of age-relevant topics around attachment, sleep, nutrition, early literacy and safety issues. The two hour group visit format allows the healthcare team to observe the dyad's interactions, model behaviors and developmentally appropriate activities, and for the group to explore the topics that matter most in that moment to them with the gentle guidance of a facilitator. Learning in a group visit is a much different experience than the historical didactic approach in primary care where patients (parents) are passive listeners and health professionals, though wellintentioned, unilaterally provide anticipatory guidance via a scripted checklist. In contrast, Centering is based on the principles of adult learning; that is, when someone is actively involved and engaged they learn and retain information more than when they are lectured to and/or read something. Greater understanding and retention come about with activities that engage many senses as well as a deeper and more meaningful exchange of ideas. Providers have a unique opportunity to listen to the ups & downs of the families' journey as parents and to gain an understanding of the life experiences, cultural beliefs and values that guide their parenting behaviors. Through this information exchange participants begin to claim their own knowledge and are empowered to be collaborative partners in their child's care. Bringing families together for care in this innovative way can profoundly change the way healthcare is delivered. Parents also report decrease in social isolation, increase in their confidence as caregivers, and more developmental awareness. Centering Parenting is offered nationally. We will highlight a robust program at an FQHC in Austin, Texas, which has been conducting groups since 2009 with a population that is medically & socially fragile, and primarily immigrant, many struggling with the challenges of being undocumented. This clinic is also the primary site for University of Texas-Dell Medical School's Pediatric residents who participate in the program during their continuity clinic and ambulatory medical home rotations.
