AB  - Adequate social support is linked with decreased social isolation, reduced psychiatric symptomatology, and improved community functioning among older adults with severe mental illness. Withdrawal and social isolation, two enduring symptoms and social consequences of mental illness limit access to informal social support. Social work interventions using older workers as lay helpers may be an effective and efficient treatment strategy to reduce social isolation and improve community functioning among this population. This project examined the process of a lay helper-older mental health consumer relationship as it evolved over a period of twelve months. Data for this study were obtained from a sample of 20 primarily African-American older outpatient mental health consumers in a rural community. The treatment group received standard outpatient services plus 5-10 hours per week of face-to-face contact from a similar age peer. Treated consumers were matched with a non-equivalent comparison group of older consumers receiving only standard community treatment in an adjacent county. A quasi-experimental mixed-methods design (a) examined characteristics of the consumer-lay helper relationship, and (b) evaluated consumer outcomes related to perceived social support and community functioning at three six-month intervals. Case studies revealed that a bond between an older lay helper and an older mental health consumer could be developed and sustained over 12 months. Relationships were rooted in rural traditions of Christian service and neighborly helping. Physical health status served to limit the range of out of home social and leisure opportunities available to consumers and resulted in more frequent reliance on tangible support as a companion technique. Consumers supported by a lay helper demonstrated significant increases in daily social activities but no changes in perceived social support or instrumental activities of daily living. Attrition in the comparison group prevented statistical analyses of between-group consumer outcomes but provided naturalistic outcome data. One comparison participant was hospitalized for a psychiatric illness, two died, and two discontinued treatment. None of the matched consumer subjects were hospitalized and none dropped out of treatment. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
