AB  - Introduction: Inadequate bowel preparation (IBP) reduces ADR, patient safety and procedural efficacy. Variables associated with inadequate bowel prep (BP) can be broadly categorized into patient and procedure related. Previous studies have found associations between IBP, various medical conditions and demographic factors. The purpose of this study is to characterize the predictors of IBP in a large ambulatory patient population undergoing colonoscopy in an open access endoscopy practice. Method(s): All colonoscopies performed at the Cleveland Clinic, a large healthcare system, between 2011-2016 were included. Demographics, comorbidities, BP agents, procedural timing, medications, endoscopic features and pathologic findings were obtained using a structured queried language (SQL) from the Clarity database, within the EPIC application. The patients included were those prescribed a BP and had the quality of BP documented on the endoscopy report. The primary endpoint was adequacy of bowel prep, defined by the Aronchick Scale. Outcomes were adjusted for patient demographics, co-morbidities, and indication for exam. Descriptive statistics (N, %) and Odds Ratios (OR; 95% CI) were calculated using a multivariable model. Result(s): Of 115,998 patients identified, 91,045 patients met inclusion criteria. The mean age was 58 +/- 12 years, 54% were female, 82% were Caucasian with average BMI of 29, and 93% were ASA I/II. The leading indication for the exam was colorectal cancer screening in 41% (37,021) followed by surveillance in 18% (16,525). The majority of patients were prescribed 4L BP; Golytely, Nulytely and Colyte represented 78% (N = 70,286), followed by 2 L Moviprep (20%; N = 18, 406) and Prepopik (1 %; N = 1,247). IBP was recorded in 14,365 patients (16%). Factors associated with IBP included male gender (1.2; 1.2-1.2), government insurance [Medicaid (1.4; 1.3-1.5) or Medicare (1.9; 1.7-2.0)], non-English speakers (0.89; 0.79-0.99), specific clinical factors such as diabetes (1.5; 1.4-1.6), obesity (1.09; 1.03-1.2), cirrhosis (1.9; 1.7-2.1), dementia (1.4; 1.2-1.6), stroke (1.4; 1.07-1.8), constipation (1.1; 1.05-1.2), exam indication: diagnostic vs. screening (1.3; 1.2-1.4) and weekend vs. weekday (1.6; 1.2-2.1) (Table 1). Conclusion(s): This large population study of patients undergoing open access colonoscopy shows that IBP occurs in 16% of outpatients. Factors associated with IBP include male gender, those with government provided insurance, non-English speakers, those with diabetes, obesity, cirrhosis, dementia, stroke and/or constipation, those who undergo a diagnostic exam and those who schedule their procedure on the weekend. Clinicians counseling patients who fall within these categories must ensure every effort is made with BP dosing instructions and to consider additional agents if effluent is not clear. (Table Presented).
