AB  - Group Formation: The cystic fibrosis (CF) outpatient team at UCSF has created an online teletherapy support group for patients. This group provides much needed emotional support to patients who cannot have faceto- face interaction with their peers. The members of the CF medical team had found that there was a need for such a group among patients and, in hiring a mental health coordinator, indicated that this was a priority for the UCSF program. Critically, in today's privacy-conscious environment, before initiating the group, the mental health coordinator collaborated with the privacy team at UCSF to create a confidentiality agreement for group members. The confidentiality agreement covered topics including HIPAA and guidelines surrounding confidential information. When a potential member wants to attend the teletherapy group, the mental health coordinator sends each potential participant the confidentiality agreement through Docusign. The potential patient must review and agree to the terms before being invited to attend future groups. UCSF consent for treatment contains a provision regarding teletherapy so no additional consent is needed for a patient to participate in the group. The group meets twice a month for an hour and uses Zoom, a HIPAA-compliant software. Typically, the group has 4-6 participants with a mix of returning members and newer members. For the first few months, the group ran as a process group, however, after about 5 months of groups, the mental health coordinator and social worker noticed that adding structure to the group helped facilitate conversation and began announcing topics for discussion prior to each group. Successes and Limitations: Members have expressed genuine interest in the group. The group was originally slated to meet monthly but due to feedback from members, it is now meeting twice a month. Participating members state higher levels of connectedness to peers and treatment procedures. Teletherapy is a new phenomenon and has different limitations than in-person therapy. In the CF group, we surmise that group bonding has been slower due to the use of teletherapy and would potentially have happened more quickly in an in-person group. This lack of cohesion has kept many members from sharing vulnerable feelings more quickly. It has also been harder to contain monopolizing members than if the group met in person. Feedback has also been harder to obtain; although facilitators also would like participants to initiate topics that they want to explore in group, members do not do so outside of group meetings. Future Growth for the Group: We plan to continue working towards group cohesion, offering educational components to the group (dietitian, pharmacist), and venturing to more vulnerable topics as group members become more comfortable. The mental health coordinator hopes to offer specialty teletherapy groups, including a group for significant others, in the future..
