AB  - Purpose: To investigate the impact of an energy conservation programme (ECP) on MS‐related fatigue, quality of life and self efficacy. To explore participants' perspective on the impact of fatigue and the value of the ECP. Relevance: Fatigue is one of the most common symptoms in Multiple Sclerosis (MS). Evidence for energy conservation programmes (ECP) to manage MS‐related fatigue is limited. Participants: Participants had a confirmed diagnosis of MS, scored >4 in the Fatigue Severity Scale (FSS) and were independent for most daily activities. Exclusion criteria comprised co‐morbid conditions, severe depression, a relapse two months prior to the study, pregnancy, previous experience of an ECP or participating in other research trial. Methods: Exploratory randomised controlled trial. Participants were randomly allocated to an ECP group or a peer support group (PSG). Both interventions were delivered in group format once a week for five weeks in two hour sessions. The primary outcome measure was the Fatigue Impact Scale (FIS) and there were a range of secondary measures. All measures were administered by blinded assessors at baseline, post‐intervention, at 6 and 12 weeks follow up. Focus groups were conducted one week post‐intervention by a facilitator and a moderator. Focus groups were audio‐taped and verbatim transcripts were typed allocating participants a code number to ensure confidentiality. Analysis: Quantitative analyses were conducted on intention to treat basis and non‐parametric tests were employed to assess differences over time and between groups since data was not normally distributed. Qualitative data was analysed using a thematic approach. Results: Twenty‐three participants were consented and randomised; the attrition level was 13.04%. There were statistically significant differences between groups in the FIS cognitive subscale in favour of the ECP group (p < 0.001). There were no statistically significant differences for any of the remaining outcome measures, except for the BDIFS over time for the ECP (p = 0.03) and the PSG group (p = 0.002). Nine participants took part in the focus groups. They shared experiences regarding diagnosis, practical problems and mood issues associated with fatigue and MS. Feelings of guilt and frustration were reported more frequently than depression. Participants valued both the ECP and PSG as they provided an opportunity for peer support and practical information on how to cope with MS and fatigue. Participants in the ECP group found some of the energy conservation strategies useful, although they also experienced difficulties with some practice activities used in the ECP. Conclusions: Although participants benefited from both interventions, the ECP was more effective at reducing the impact of fatigue. Benefits could be partially due to participants in both groups being motivated to actively manage their MS. Coping strategies such as acceptance and the availability of social support enabled these individuals to have a good quality of life. Implications: There is a need to support existing clinical guidelines regarding the use of ECPs in the management of MS‐related fatigue. This is of particular relevance due to the current gaps in the care provided to people with MS, especially regarding access to information and neurorehabilitation as reported by a recent audit by the Royal College of Physicians.
