AB  - This study was designed to investigate the long-term efficacy of three cognitive-behavioral interventions in comparison to a no-treatment control group on 85 chronic temporomandibular joint disorder (TMD) patients. The intervention groups included biofeedback, cognitive-behavioral skills training, and combined biofeedback and cognitive behavioral skills training. Patients were reevaluated one year after receiving treatment. It was expected that subjects receiving treatment would show significant change in Research Diagnostic Criteria (RDC) for TMD Axis II scores. As predicted treatment patients demonstrated significant improvement in subjective pain, pain-related disability, and mandibular functioning compared to the no-treatment group. A second goal of this study was to examine the relationship between psychosocial factors and TMD treatment outcome. Previous research indicates that psychosocial variables appear to play a key role in the prediction of pain and pain reduction response. Some pain predictor variables included the presence of a muscle disorder, a high Beck Depression Inventory score and elevated MMPI-2 Scale 3 score. Differences in coping style (Adaptive Coper, Interpersonally Distressed, and Dysfunctional) identified by the Multidimensional Pain Inventory were evaluated. All three coping style groups responded to treatment; moreover, differences in the Dysfunctional group noted at initial and post-treatment assessments were no longer apparent at one year. An analysis of treatment response in subjects diagnosed with a muscle disorder found these patients do not experience a significant reduction in pain one year after treatment. Additionally, analyses of subthreshold and comorbid psychopathology, as measured by the Structured Clinical Interview of the Diagnostic Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fourth Edition, were attempted to explore the relationship between increased psychopathology and TMD. The comorbid comparison was inconclusive and a small sample size precluded a comparison of Axis II Personality Clusters Types. The only difference found between subthreshold or current Axis I disordered patients versus patients without an Axis I disorder was on the RDC Depression scale. Finally, an attempt to explore the impact of therapeutic factors such as compliance and additional treatment sessions on TMD outcome was conducted. No differences were noted between subjects with higher versus lower compliance scores or patients receiving additional &ldquo;booster&rdquo; sessions. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
