AB  - This study was designed to prospectively follow patients with acute Temporomandibular Disorders (TMD) for six months, and to identify risk factors that predispose patients to chronic pain. Based on findings of past studies, it was hypothesized that females and older patients would be more at risk for developing chronicity. In view of the high rates of psychological comorbidity found in other pain populations, it was hypothesized that the acute TMD patients who subsequently became chronic would have higher rates of psychopathology, as measured by the Structured Clinical Interview of the Diagnostic Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fourth Edition, Axis I and Axis II Disorders (SCID I and II) than acute TMD patients whose symptoms subsided to the point that they were deemed to be nonchronic. Additionally, it was hypothesized that acute TMD patients reporting ongoing pain after a six-month period would have a higher number of musculoskeletal disorders than TMD patients whose pain symptoms abated, as assessed by the dual axial system of the Research Diagnostic Criteria for Temporomandibular Disorders (RDC/TMD). Results, in general, supported the aforementioned hypotheses. A gender effect was found in that a greater number of females reported that their pain symptoms continued following a six-month follow-up as compared to males. No differences in age or other sociodemographic variables were observed. The chronic group was found to have higher rates of affective disorders than the nonchronic group. With respect to other forms of psychopathology, prevalence rates between the chronic and nonchronic group were comparable. No differences in prevalence rates of personality disorders were found between the two groups. In terms of specific musculoskeletal disorders, patients in the chronic group were found to have a greater number of Group I muscle disorders and Group III disorders (other joint conditions) than the nonchronic group. In terms of psychological characteristics measured by the RDC/TMD, patients reported greater pain intensity, disability, depression, and somatization when first assessed. In summary, the chronic and nonchronic groups significantly differed along the dimensions of the DSM-IV and the RDC/TMD. A logistic regression analysis further revealed that greater pain intensity, dysfunctional graded chronic pain status, somatization, and a Group I disorder predicted prolonged pain after six months. The results of this study have important implications for understanding the progression of acute to chronic pain, as well as furthering the understanding of the psychological-physical interface in TMD. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
