AB  - Examines the nature & effectiveness of the Antelope Valley Rehabilitation Centers the only directly operated substance abuse treatment programs in Los Angeles County, CA. The centers were established in the 1940s & originally served as custodial centers for predominantly older, middle-class, white, male clientele with little emphasis on treatment or rehabilitation. A dramatic rise in drug abuse among the young & increased availability of federal funds led to changes during the 1960s & 1970s, eg, implementation of chemical dependence programs, group therapy, vocational guidance, & counseling. Through the 1980s & 1990s, the residential population has become significantly younger, lower class, & minority, & increased staff complaints concerning the difficulty of clientele is attributed to an increase in court referrals. Although economic shifts have threatened the centers in the past decade, they have remained a cost-effective alternative to incarceration & hospitalization. Current problems include lack of standardized treatment & effectiveness definitions, posttreatment environment of residents, federal failure to address the issue of polydrug abuse, & lack of communication & cooperation between & among federal, state, & local agencies. However, it is concluded that the centers remain both successful & effective. T. Sevier
