AB  - Introduction: In 2009 the elderly constituted 12.9% of population, by 2030 they will present 21% of population. Substance abuse, particularly of alcohol and prescription drugs, is one of the fastest growing health problems. The substance abuse problems among the elderly are often overlooked, as symptoms of substance abuse often mimic symptoms of other medical and psychiatric disorders. Substance use disorders take a greater toll on affected older adults. In addition to the psychosocial issues that are unique to the elderly, aging induces physiological changes that increase susceptibility to the deleterious effects of alcohol and other illicit substances. There is a general lack of evidence based treatment approaches for substance abuse in the elderly. As a result, much of what is recommended is based on the interventions that have been validated in younger populations. Method(s): We introduced a psychotherapy group focused on substance use and aging in the Geriatric Outpatient Program at a our psychiatric hospital. Result(s): Group members are men and women above the age of sixty with complex medical histories, substance use disorders (predominantly alcohol), and co-morbid mood and anxiety disorders. Group sessions take place weekly and are structured with a "check-in" by each member, a focused discussion of a theme raised by the experiences of one or more group members, and a final segment comprised of a menu of positive activities and strategies for relapse prevention in the upcoming week. Thematic topics range from identifying and managing triggers for substance abuse, coping with increased medical problems with aging, concepts of successful aging, and managing challenging transitions in roles and relationships in late life that increase vulnerability for substance use. Group meetings provide an avenue for socialization, interactive discussions and problemsolving around these topics. Conclusion(s): It is increasingly important to understand specific ways to engage the elderly patient in substance use treatment. Expansion of research is needed for maximum effectiveness and efficiency of the healthcare system serving theses individuals. Here we present guidelines to identifying substance use disorders in the elderly, caveats of diagnosis and treatment, and common challenges that often arise in treatment. We also describe a psychotherapeutic intervention we are using at our institution to engage elders in ongoing treatment, and highlight specific aspects of this intervention that may serve as practical points in developing substance use treatment that is tailored to the elderly.
