AB  - The introduction of the new concept of complex post-traumatic stress disorder (cPTSD) in the International Classifi cation of Diseases of the 11th Edition deserves attention especially In the country, there undergoing there active fighting is taking place [1,2]. If we study the experience of other countries, simple PTSD affects 8% of the US population and research shows that 25% - 50% of people who meet the criteria of PTSD, in fact having cPTSD [3]. Today, we have no experience in the diagnosis of cPTSD in Ukraine there undergoing active fighting is taking place. The objective of our study, along with the diagnosis of cPTSD symptoms, is to try to use a quality of life questionnaire to evaluate the symptoms of cPTSD. We are included in the study a group of patients - 26 people, combatants that former participants of the fighting, male sex, who had pronounced symptoms of PTSD. The average age of patients was 41.3 years, the average length of service in the army - 19.4 months (the maximum was 48 months, the minimum - 8 months), the length of involved in the combat operations on average 8.2 months. Predominated patients with specialized secondary education - 18 men (69.3%), unemployed - 16 people (61.6%) and 17 people were not married (65.4%). Twelve (62%) had no children, and the number of combatants with one or two children was the same - 7 people in each groupe (26.9%). The only 7 men (26.9%) lived within their family settings, the 9 people from the groupe (34.6%) lived alone, 10 people (48.5%) lived with their parents. The level of their financial support was assessed as low by 15 people (57.7%), average for 11 people. (42.3%). We did not find heredity by mental disorders in 23 persons was recorded. (88.5%), the remaining patients - 3 people. (11.5%) among the relatives were any mental disorders. Eleven persons (42.3%) had in his life history was psychological trauma of childhood. The mostly combatant had a military background before taking up arms is largely (18 patients - 69.3%). The result of our study revealed that the level of community and service support was below average level, that indicating the need to improve community social support. The self-realization of the combatants has a low average level, which further influences the level of adaptation after the end of the service. Physical and psychological well-being are also lower, and this indicator to the need for psychological and physical rehabilitation of demobilized soldiers. At the same time, communication with friends and family, performance were at a high level, and these are positive factors that affected for the adaptation to civilian life. Summarizing the research, we suggest that in order to organize the help of the combatants more effectively, it is necessary to evaluate, along with a purely psychiatric diagnosis of PTSD, the level of social and physical adaptation of patients with PTSD. No conflict of interestCopyright © 2020
