AB  - Individuals with co-occurring mental and substance use disorders challenge both clinicians and the treatment delivery system. This reality represents a major public health concern, a matter with devastating human, social, and economic consequences. This study was designed to determine the general prevalence of co-occurring mental disorder symptomology in a substance abuse outpatient treatment seeking population. Additionally, this study identified individuals who experienced high versus low integrated treatment outcomes, classifying participants into four distinct subgroups, based these integrated treatment outcomes. Differences between these four subgroups were suggestive of unique patterns of specific characteristics among adults with co-occurring mental and substance use disorders. Finally, and perhaps the most clinically significant, this study discriminated which Addiction Severity Index (ASI) subscale composite scores were the most significant in predicting the four hypothesized subgroups of individuals with co-occurring disorders. Archival data from 553 adults, admitted into an intensive outpatient treatment program (September, 1995 to August, 2002), were analyzed for the study. Participants ranged in age from 17 to 74 years. Results indicated that the vast majority of participants (95%) experienced co-occurring disorder symptomology. Participants were found to experience a significant increase in general wellness at the end of treatment and were able to maintain this change, over time, at a six-month follow-up point. These results corroborate existing evidence that supports that integrated treatment approaches are more effective with co-morbid individuals. Discriminant function classification analyses signified that participants' ASI psychiatric, substance use and family composite scores are the most important psychosocial functioning areas in predicting group membership based on treatment outcomes. Additional findings indicated that clinicians and treatment providers should assess each patient's risk of suicide, providing every treatment contact with suicide prevention and educational materials. Additional research is needed regarding specific methods of educating counselors, healthcare providers, and the broader community how to remain empathic with individuals struggling with co-occurring mental and substance use symptomology. Furthermore, treatment and follow-up attrition casualties are another fertile area for continuing research. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
