AB  - Objective: The study aims to determine the efficacy of acamprosate in maintaining abstinence in weaned alcohol-dependent patients and to monitor its side effects and its addictive potential. Method(s): Multicenter, double-blind, randomized placebo-controlled clinical trial with 262 outpatient alcoholics recruited from 22 treatment centers in the Benelux. Patients were assessed for abstinence during a six month treatment period (active medication or placebo) and a six month follow-up period. Relapse was defined as any consumption of any amount of alcohol at any time between two visits. Result(s): Patients in the acamprosate group completed the treatment more often than patients in the placebo group (41% vs. 31%), were longer in treatment (102 days vs. 88 days), relapsed later (after 45 days vs. after 15 days), had longer cumulative periods of abstinence (61 days vs. 43 days) and were more often abstinent during the total treatment period of 6 months (20% vs. 10%). Six months after the termination of the treatment, the difference between the acamprosate and the placebo group was still present: 11% of the acamprosate group was abstinent during the total 12 month study period compared to only 5% in the placebo group. However, due to the small numbers at follow-up, this difference was not statistically significant. The active medication was generally well tolerated and no serious adverse events or withdrawal symptoms were observed. Conclusion(s): It is concluded that acamprosate is a potential useful pharmacological compound for the long-term treatment of alcohol dependence. However, treatment retention in both the placebo and the acamprosate group was rather low with 59% of the patients in the acamprosate group not completing the six month treatment period. In future research the effect of combined motivational strategies and pharmacological interventions should be tested against the effect of a simple pharmacological intervention. It is hypothesized that these combined interventions will increase treatment retention and - as a consequence - treatment effectiveness and stable abstinence.
