Y1  - 2009///
AB  - Research has shown that college is a time of high stress and high risk for experiencing symptoms of depression as well as problematic alcohol use. At the same time, treatment options are not always readily available, and there are often barriers for obtaining available services. The present research tested a brief mailed intervention, designed to enable students to get feedback about their mood and alcohol use if applicable, and to begin initiating behavior change. The model is based on existing research showing the effectiveness of self-help, written, and computerized interventions with depressed mood, as well as the graphic feedback and brief motivational enhancement treatment with college student drinking. Students (N= 177) were randomly assigned to intervention or control group following a baseline assessment. A week after completing measures of depression and coping, as well as alcohol use and related consequences, participants in the intervention condition were mailed feedback and information detailing their mood, coping strategies, alcohol use, norms, and related problems, as well as suggestions for enhancing mood and moderating drinking. Results indicated that feedback was effective in reducing depressed mood, hopelessness, and among men, increasing coping at the one-month follow-up. Hopelessness mediated reductions in depressed mood. There was also a reduction in perceived norms of alcohol use. Results support the use of personalized feedback as a low cost, initial intervention for college students suffering from symptoms of depression. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
