AB  - Investigated gender differences in the impact of depression on 1-yr cardiac mortality in patients hospitalized for an acute myocardial infarction (MI). Secondary analysis was performed on data from 2 studies that used the Beck Depression Inventory (BDI) to assess depression symptoms during hospitalization: a prospective study of post-MI risk and a randomized trial of psychosocial intervention (control group only). The sample included 896 patients (283 women) who survived to discharge and received usual posthospital care. Multivariate logistic regression analysis was used to assess the risk of 1-yr cardiac mortality associated with baseline BDI scores. There were 290 patients (133 women) with BDI scores ≥10; 8.3% of the depressed women died of cardiac causes in contrast to 2.7% of the nondepressed. For depressed men, the rate of cardiac death was 7.0% in contrast to 2.4% of the nondepressed. Increased BDI scores were significantly related to cardiac mortality for both genders. Control for other multivariate predictors of mortality in the data set did not change the impact of the BDI for either gender. Thus, in-hospital post-MI depression is a significant predictor of 1-yr cardiac mortality for both women and men, and its impact is largely independent of other post-MI risks. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
