AB  - Investigated the generalizability of clinical findings to treatment settings by comparing 102 patients admitted to an outpatient cocaine clinic who volunteered for random assignment to treatment (individual, individual-plus-group, or intensive group therapy) with 308 Ss who refused to volunteer. Ss completed the Risk for AIDS Behavior Inventory, Beck Depression Inventory, SCL-90 (Revised), and Addiction Severity Index. Refusers received the same treatment provided by the same staff in the same setting as did Ss in the Intensive Group Therapy group. No differences were found in demographics, addiction severity, AIDS risk behavior, psychiatric functioning, or treatment retention. Results of research findings may be more applicable to clinical settings than was previously thought. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
