AB  - Investigated the rate of spontaneous abnormal involuntary movements in schizophrenia or schizophreniform psychosis. 79 patients (mean age 27.7 yrs) with a 1st episode of schizophrenia or schizophreniform psychosis who presented to a catchment area psychiatric service over a 3-yr period, and who were neuroleptic-naive or had been medicated for less than 1 mo, were examined for the presence of involuntary movements with use of the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale. Six Ss (7.6%) had spontaneous dyskinesia, and 9 other Ss had mild orofacial involuntary movements. The Ss with spontaneous dyskinesia had completed significantly fewer years of education than the Ss without dyskinesia. Spontaneous involuntary movements were unrelated to age at presentation for treatment. Spontaneous abnormal involuntary movements were evident among a proportion of patients with 1st-episode schizophrenia or schizophreniform psychosis at baseline presentation and were associated with reduced educational attainment. This finding supports previous suggestions that abnormal involuntary movements in schizophrenia may be related to the pathophysiology of the illness and therefore cannot be attributed entirely to the adverse effects of neuroleptic medication. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
