AB  - This study investigated the responses of African American youth to the police beating of Rodney King, the trial, & the subsequent civil disorders in South Central Los Angeles. Employing a qualitative-quantitative research design, the authors conducted semistructured interviews with 17 focus groups (N = 144) & 32 youth, ages 15-30, recruited through a "snowball" sample. The total sample included 58% males & 42% females with a mean age of 19.3 years. Participants identified five major employment-related concerns: (1) lack of jobs, (2) employer discrimination, (3) few jobs with good wages & benefits, (4) employer preference for African American females, & (5) lack of job training programs. Pervasive feelings of hopelessness, anger, & alienation were associated with high rates of unemployment & lack of economic development in the community. Implications for social work practice & community mental health interventions are discussed. 6 Tables, 44 References. Adapted from the source document.
