AB  - Introduction: Depression is a disabling psychiatric illness, characterized by episodes of low mood, as well as loss of interest and pleasure in daily activities. Though Major Depressive Episodes (MDE) represent the hallmark of MDD, they are also a feature of Bipolar Disorder and can occur in comorbidity with other psychiatric disorders [1]. MDE treatment is mostly based on monoaminergic drugs. However, despite the presence of many antidepressant treatments, research showed that, even when reaching remission, their efficacy is still partial [2]. Aim(s): One potential approach to improving non-response to antidepressants is the use of adjunctive nutraceuticals [3].The aim of this study is to evaluate the efficacy of nutraceutical compounds for the treatment of depressive symptoms in a sample of patients with MDE. Method(s): 50 outpatients with a diagnosis of MDE were assessed through a structured clinical interview based on Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of mental disorders (DSM-5) criteria and the Clinical Global Impression (CGI) scale to measure illness severity, global improvement and change in therapeutic response. For the purpose of the study, the sample was divided into three different groups according to the administration of nutraceutical compound: group a) the nutraceutical compound was administered simultaneously with an antidepressant (starter); group b) the nutraceutical compound was administered in add-on to a pre-existing treatment (add-on); group c) the nutraceutical compound was administered as a single treatment (monotherapy). The nutraceutical was made of three components: N-Acetyl-cysteine (NAC) 500 mg, S-Adenosyl-L-Methionine (SAM) 200 mg, Quatrefolic O-5MTHF-Glucosamine 400 mc (Folic acid). Each patient received one/two tablets of this compound per day for 4-8 weeks of treatment. The treatment efficacy was addressed using the CGI-Severity scale (CGI-S) at baseline and at the end of the treatment and the CGI-improvement scale (CGI-I) at the end of the treatment. Statistical analyses were performed with Pearson's chi-squared test for categorical variables and ANOVA test for continuous variables. Continuous variables at T0 and T1 were compared. Using paired samples t test. All analyses were performed using Statistical Package for the Social Sciences (SPSS) 26.0 software for Windows (SPSS Inc, Chicago, IL, USA). Statistical significance was set at p < 0.05. Result(s): A significant reduction of CGI-S score was found for each group from baseline to follow-up. Each group showed a global improvement rated by CGI-I after treatment. In addition, a significantly greater CGI-I score emerged in the starter treatment compared to other groups. No difference emerged in terms of CGI severity at baseline between groups. Consistently, 86% of the whole sample showed a global improvement after treatment, and 94% did not show side effects. Conclusion(s): Our results showed that the use of nutraceuticals may reduce depressive symptoms and improve overall functioning in already medicated and non-medicated patients affected by MDE in both MDD and other psychiatric disorders. In addition, the versatility and tolerability profile of this nutraceutical compound could be exploited in patients with various severity of depressive symptoms especially in addition to already prescribed or newly prescribed patients. No conflict of interestCopyright © 2021
