AB  - The purpose of this study was to test whether guided imagery or AA was more effective in reducing depression and anxiety in substance abuse clients. The sample consisted of 48 subjects receiving treatment at two outpatient substance abuse clinics. The subjects volunteering for the study were randomly assigned to either a guided imagery (GI) group or a twelve step (AA) group. Before treatment subjects were administered the Beck Depression Inventory and the Clinical Anxiety Scale as pretest measures. After six weeks of treatment the BDI and CAS were administered as posttest measures. Observer ratings of changes in depression and anxiety levels were recorded at six weeks by the subjects' therapists using checklists containing DSM-III R criteria. An analysis of covariance yielded no significant difference between the two groups on either the BDI or the CAS. A Mann-Whitney test of the observer ratings found the GI group improved significantly more than the AA group in depression, but found no significant difference between the two groups in change in anxiety levels. A Mann-Whitney analysis of pretest and posttest comparisons on the BDI and CAS found significant improvement in the GI group on both measures. Using the same analysis the AA group was not found to have changed significantly from pretest to posttest on either measure. Guided imagery was found to be at least as effective as AA in treating the depression and anxiety associated with substance abuse after six weeks of treatment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
