AB  - An issue of great concern in the development community is addressed: how to protect the poor during structural adjustment programs. Emphasis is placed on those among the poor who are adversely affected by adjustment policies. It is argued that these poor households are least likely to be able to weather any negative consequences of adjustment policies. Special temporary programs may be necessary to support them during the period of adjustment. The case of Peru is used to illustrate ways in which household data can be used to formulate policies to protect the poor. One finding is that urban poor and rural poor will be affected in different ways. Moreover, the poor are more heterogeneous and widespread than many discussions seem to assume and methods for identifying them based on analyses of a small number of socioeconomic groups may suffer from serious leakage problems. Recently gathered household survey data can be extremely useful in assessing the direct, first round effects of structural adjustment policies.
