AB  - A recent Health Affairs article found that new medical technology confers positive net benefits for several conditions, including heart attacks, cataracts, and depression. The present article estimates the extent to which uninsured Americans ages 55-64 use these technologies and compute access gaps for each. Based on the previous article's net benefit estimates, it is calculated that more than $1.1 billion is lost annually from excess morbidity and mortality among the uninsured population because of lack of access to new technologies for the treatment of these three conditions. And the potential losses from lack of health insurance will only increase. Medical technology may be worth it, but future research should focus not only on the benefits that technology provides, but on to whom they are conferred. This paper's findings suggest that substantial welfare is being lost every year because the benefits of technology are not reaching the uninsured.
