AB  - This study examined the effects of strengths case management for consumer level of functioning, hospitalization, and cost of care. The research design was an ex-post facto examination of hospital admissions for consumers who participated in a National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) funded research project comparing strengths case management with a primary therapist case management model. Consumers were selected randomly from a computer-generated list of 'open' clients in an urban mental health center. All consumers in the pool had Global Assessment of Functioning Scale (GAF) scores of 50 or less and were certified as seriously and persistently mentally ill (SPMI). Forty-three consumers from the pool were assigned randomly to receive strengths case management, and 48 received case management services from their primary therapist. Hospital admissions and cost-of-care data were obtained by reviewing hospital and mental health records at the conclusion of the primary study. Professional assessment of consumer functioning was obtained by case manager and therapist ratings using the Utah Case Management Consumer Assessment Record (Utah CCAR). After 9 months, experimental group members showed significant improvement in 8 out of 12 levels of functioning, whereas control group members showed no change. Total hospital bed days reduced for both groups; however, the strengths case management group had a 50% greater reduction in absolute bed-day use when compared to the control group. Readmission rates were low for both groups. Length of stay for individual admissions decreased for both groups. Cost of inpatient treatment increased significantly for the control group over the 42 months of the study. Medical comorbidity was the primary reason for increased costs in the control group. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
