AB  - We conducted a qualitative study to explore the pathways by which traditional gender roles may ultimately affect Vietnamese women's interpretation of sexually transmitted disease (STD) symptoms & health seeking strategies. Data on gender roles, perceptions of types of sexual relationships, perceptions of persons with STDs, & STD patient experiences were gathered through in depth interviews & focus groups with 18 men & 18 women in the general population of northern Vietnam. A framework integrating Andersen's behavioral model of health services use & Zurayk's multi layered model was used to conceptualize women's health seeking behavior for STD symptoms. Both men & women noted clear gender differences in sexual roles & expectations. According to participants, a woman's primary roles in northern Vietnam are socially constructed as that of a wife & mother -- and in these roles, she is expected to behave in a faithful & obedient manner vis a vis her husband. It emerged that men's marital & sexual roles are less clearly defined by traditional norms & are more permissive in their tolerance of premarital & extramarital sex. For women, however, these activities are socially condemned. Finally, since STDs are associated with sexual promiscuity, both men & women expressed anxiety about telling their partners about an STD; women's expressions were characterized more by fear of social & physical consequences, whereas men expressed embarrassment. Community level interventions that work toward disassociating STDs from traditional social norms may enable Vietnamese women to report possible STD symptoms & promote diagnosis & care for STDs. 39 References. Adapted from the source document.
