AB  - Describes the case of a 28 yr old married, unemployed male with bipolar illness who was referred to a clinic from a private psychiatric group when his insurance ran out. The author poses 2 questions: (1) Does psychotherapy have a role in the client's treatment plan?, and (2) How can the psychiatrist balance the client's neediness against heavy service demand of a busy public-sector clinic? Psychotherapeutic objectives are organized under 3 headings: (1) adjusting to the reality of the illness, (2) loss, and (3) compliance with medications and other treatment demands. Group therapy, transference, and countertransference are also discussed. The author concludes that both the therapeutic work and the bipolar illness are always challenging. It is imperative to use both psychopharmacologic means and psychotherapy to stabilize patients and enable them to minimize the damaging effects of this difficult and chronic illness. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
