AB  - Many studies have called for expanded alcohol and drug abuse and mental health services on the basis that insuring or providing them would actually reduce costs - or at least would allow them to partially pay for themselves. As reviewed by Mark Olfson, Merrile Sing, and Herbert Schlesinger, cross-sectional studies of the cost impact of global service expansions generally do not support the cost-offset effect. There are several issues, however, that are not discussed in their analysis, which have important policy implications for the cost-offset and cost-benefit approach in mental health services: 1. Mental health services appear to be held to a higher standard than are other areas of coverage policy by having to demonstrate cost offsets to justify coverage. 2. There is little evidence that cost-offset data have been persuasive in making mental health coverage policy.
