AB  - The article focuses on programs to deal with male batterers. It is becoming increasingly evident that men who batter must be more effectively treated if the incidence of wife abuse is to be reduced. Consequently, in the last few years, approximately 150 programs to deal directly with batterers have been developed. Some mental health programs and family services offer psychotherapy for batterers. In addition, an increasing number of women's shelters have started adjunct programs for the abusers. But while there seems to be an emerging consensus that the men who batter must and can be reached, there is little empirical research on the men them- selves and only inconsistent speculation on how to deal with them. To help refine the speculation and to elaborate on one approach, this article submits a descriptive analysis of the batterer from a sample of fifty batterers voluntarily participating in a self-help, group counseling program. The batterer is characterized here as responding to a rigidly defined gender role, one in which control of oneself and others is central. This provisional interpretation supports the approach of self-help oriented programs in which the batterer maintains a serious balance of control.
