AB  - Background: Substance use disorders are prevalent in the veteran population and a significant cause of morbidity and mortality among veterans. Expanding access to care is a priority within the VA and minimization of wait times remains a directive to enhance patient care. This study examines the effect of recent program expansion on our contingency management program for stimulant use disorders and outcomes for the individuals participating in the program. Method(s): The James A. Haley VA Substance Use Disorders Service treatment program underwent a significant paradigm change 3 years ago in an attempt to meet the increasing demand for outpatient services. The program was reoriented from a 12- step facilitation model to cognitive-behavioral therapy oriented therapy, and increased resources were placed on outpatient counseling of varying levels of intensity. Significant to this was the expansion of our long-time contingency management program for veterans with stimulant use disorders. Veterans participating in this program were monitored longitudinally for the presence of stimulants on UDS, and administration of financial incentives was separately monitored. Result(s): Participation in contingency management for stimulant misuse in our outpatient treatment program increased by a factor of 300% between FY2015, when the redesign was completed, and FY2016. We have continued to enroll veterans into our program at a stable and increased level since that time. Completion rates for veterans enrolling in contingency management are comparable to national estimates, both in regard to completion rates as well as negative UDS results (DePhilippis et al, 2017). It is noted that in almost all cases in our protocol, that veterans dropped out of contingency management if they did have a positive UDS result. Conclusion(s): Contingency management is an evidence-based therapy, which has been shown to be highly effective for achieving sobriety among stimulant users and our experience has been consistent with this. Further review for long-term sobriety following completion of contingency management is an identified goal for our program and correlation with Brief Addiction Monitor data would be useful to determine longterm benefit from contingency management in our treatment program. Our program continues to monitor and adjust the protocol based on clinical data. We have recently redesigned our stimulant based contingency management protocol to utilize variable reinforcement as opposed to scheduled reinforcement in an attempt to improve retention of individuals who relapse during the protocol and have expanded to offer contingency management to cannabis users as well. We will be examining the impact of the change to a variable reinforcement schedule. Summary: Contingency management is an effective, evidence-based therapy for the treatment of substance use disorders which can be administered to individuals at lower intensity levels of care to improve outcomes. Our substance use disorders treatment program saw significant growth in the development of our contingency management program for stimulant use disorders when we expanded outpatient services 3 years ago, and with this growth came new challenges in implementation of the program. Maintaining engagement in treatment is a challenge when offering services at a lower level of treatment intensity. Our program continues to grow and evolve in response to these challenges and continues to monitor outcomes including engagement and completion of treatment and have modified our treatment protocol (from scheduled to variable reinforcement) to respond to these challenges. Contingency management is therefore a highly adaptable and useful intervention for individuals with substance use disorders.
