AB  - Background: The rate of illicit drug use among US pregnant women is estimated to be 5.4%. However, at our rural tertiary care academic medical center, approximately 7.4% of births are affected by maternal opioid use disorders alone. Although access to addiction treatment for pregnant women is expanding, obstetrical providers identify access to treatment and communication with addiction treatment providers as ongoing challenges. Women with substance use disorders (SUDs) have high rates of co-occurring psychiatric illness and may benefit from access to maternity care, medication-assisted addiction treatment, and psychiatric care in an integrated setting. Pregnancy is a time of high motivation for self-care and engagement in addiction treatment is most likely at this time. Although integrated psychiatric care has become accepted in the primary care setting, other medical specialties have not adopted integrated care models as quickly. Because many regions with the highest rates of opioid use are rural, it is essential to develop models that enhance access to treatment for individuals with transportation and other barriers, particularly during pregnancy when women require multiple medical appointments. However, research on integrated care for SUDs in maternity settings is relatively sparse. We describe an integrated perinatal addiction treatment program located in a rural academic setting to improve care for perinatal women with SUDs. Intervention(s): We developed the Dartmouth-Hitchcock Perinatal Addiction Treatment Program in 2013 as a collaborative program of the Departments of Psychiatry, Obstetrics & Gynecology and Pediatrics. This integrated model provides maternity care, women's health, psychiatric consultation, group and individual addiction counseling, medication-assisted treatment for opioid use disorder and parenting education and support in a single setting. In recognition of transportation challenges facing women in rural areas, the program runs on a single day per week and offers "one stop shopping" for services. Women enter the program during pregnancy and attend weekly; they are encouraged to continue in the program postpartum. To enhance engagement, women participate in on-site urine drug screening at the beginning of each visit and receive a prescription for buprenorphine or buprenorphine-naloxone at the conclusion of each visit. Result(s): The program has treated over 80 women thus far, with 55% retention after 6 weeks postpartum. Over 65% have a co-occurring psychiatric conditions, primarily depression and PTSD. Perinatal outcomes are positive, with an average age at delivery of 38 weeks and normal mean birthweight. Less than 25% of infants require treatment for neonatal abstinence syndrome. Participants strongly endorse the peer support provided by the program and the integration of maternity care. Conclusions/Directions: Fully integrated maternity, addiction, and psychiatric care holds great promise for reducing barriers, improving access to services, enhancing communication among providers, and improving outcomes for mother-child dyads. Proximate successes include decreasing maternal substance use, preventing adverse perinatal outcomes, and decreasing the duration of infant hospitalization for neonatal abstinence syndrome. Longer-term benefits may include a decreased rate of infant removal by child protective services, reduced prevalence of childhood trauma, and promotion of healthy parent-child attachment. Future directions for this innovative program include expanding services to include treatment for partners, and enhanced pediatric assessment and support.
