AB  - The juvenile legal system (JLS) disproportionally encompasses and confines youth occupying racial, ethic, and socioeconomic positions of social disadvantage (Office of Juvenile Justice and Delinquency Prevention, 2012). JLS-involved youth are at increased risk of experiencing mental health challenges (Schufelt & Cocozza, 2006), despite the rehabilitative mandates of the JLS (JJDP Act, 1974-2002). Critical consciousness (CC) (Freire, 1993) has been theorized to be an antidote to oppression, and its promotion is suggested to enhance empowerment and wellness for marginalized youth (Watts et al., 1999; Brown & Tappan, 2008). However, there is little empirical work examining the mental health correlates of CC (Garcia & Lane, 2010) in general or for JLS-involved youth. Further, adolescent girls are the fastest growing segment of the JLS. Girls' arrests are increasing at a faster rate (or decreasing more slowly) than boys' arrest (Zahn et al., 2008). Research on girls in the JLS has called for the development and evaluation of interventions that are gender-responsive and which address connections between system involvement and oppression (Schaffner, 2006). There are currently no interventions that meet the criteria for effectiveness for JLS-involved girls (Zahn et al., 2010). This dissertation 1) examined the cross-sectional association between youth-reported CC and mental health, and whether this relationship depended on gender through a secondary analysis of data informed by an ethnically and racially diverse sample of 206 youth (N=99 girls) aged 12-18 from six residential facilities in one large metropolitan area; and 2) whether girls participating in a group-based intervention (called Skills 4 Life) designed to be socio-politically informed (n=49) reported different levels of mental health and CC over time as compared to youth randomly assigned to a community support group (CSG) intervention (n=50). Finding suggested 1) CC is concurrently associated with internalizing mental health challenges for girls, and with reduced metal health challenges for boys. Second, 2) girls that engaged in the S4L intervention exhibited significant decreases in negative mental health challenges compared to their counterparts in the CSG. These findings have implications for research and practice, and contribute toward developing theories on sociopolitical development in youth involved in the legal system. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
