AB  - PURPOSE: Cancer survivors use support groups for varied reasons. Outcomes of group‐based interventions may be determined in part by how well treatment goals for the group align with survivors' goals. The aims of the present study were to characterize survivors' goals for using an Internet‐based social‐networking site, health‐space.net, and to evaluate whether intervention‐aligned goals (i.e. managing distress, learning coping‐skills for managing cancer‐related difficulties, social support) were predictive of engagement with health‐space. net. METHODS: Cancer survivors with significant distress (Distress Thermometer > 3; n = 215) were recruited to participate in a randomized trial of a 12‐week, Internet‐based social‐networking intervention. Prior to receiving access to the group, participants were asked to describe their personal goals for using health‐space.net. Goals were qualitatively coded into 11 categories, and participants' time spent using the website was tracked. RESULTS: Participants reported multiple personal goals: meet similar others (30.7%), manage anxiety (23.3%), seek information (21.4%), manage distress (20%), move on from cancer (17.7%), social interaction (14.9%), improve health (13.0%), manage physical symptoms (11.6%), help others (9.8%), gain encouragement (6.0%), and other (14.4%). "Manage anxiety" was associated with more total time (M = 678 vs. 438 minute, p < 0.05), time in the facilitated chat (M = 227 vs. 118 minute, p = 0.03), and guidance modules (M = 75 vs. 43 minute; p = 0.02). "Manage physical symptoms" was associated with more total time (M = 787 vs. 455 minute; p = 0.04) and discussion board time (M = 262 vs. 130 minute; p = 0.05). "Meet similar others" was associated with more discussion board time (M = 192 vs. 125 minute; p = 0.03). However, "Help others" was associated with less total time (M = 279 vs. 517 minute; p = 0.03) and chat time (M = 71 vs. 151 minute; p = 0.02). CONCLUSIONS: Results were consistent with the hypothesis that intervention‐aligned goals would be predictive of engagement with the intervention. To the extent that the treatment goals match participants' interest in using the treatment, participants spent significantly more time engaged with the intervention. CLINICAL/RESEARCH IMPLICATIONS: Efforts to tailor the intervention to specific personal goals could improve engagement with these types of Internet‐based interventions and potentially improve outcomes.
