AB  - THIS EXPERIMENT WAS CONDUCTED FOR A 5-MO PERIOD WITH CHRONIC PATIENTS WHOSE AVERAGE AGE WAS 60 YR., WHO HAD BEEN HOSPITALIZED FOR AN AVERAGE OF 18 YR., AND WERE DIAGNOSED SCHIZOPHRENICS IN 70% OF THE CASES. THE EXPERIMENTAL MILIEU PROVIDED 'A MIXED-SEX WARD, A SHELTERED WORKSHOP DOING PAID INDUSTRIAL WORK, A WARD STORE, FREQUENT TRIPS INTO THE OUT-OF-HOSPITAL COMMUNITY, AND A PROGRAM EMPHASIZING PATIENT RESPONSIBILITY FOR MANY ASPECTS OF THEIR LIVES FROM SELF-CARE TO PARTIAL OPERATION OF THE WORKSHOP AND THE WARD STORE.' ROLE OF STAFF AND COMPARATIVE BEHAVIOR OF A CONTROL GROUP FROM THE REGULAR HOSPITAL WARDS ARE DISCUSSED. 'THESE FINDINGS (GREATLY IMPROVED INSTRUMENTAL SKILLS, GREATER RESPONSIBILITY FOR SELF-CARE, BETTER SOCIAL JUDGMENT, SOMEWHAT GREATER SOCIABILITY, AS WELL AS TEMPORARY CONTAINMENT OF SYMPTOMATIC BEHAVIOR AND A TREND TOWARD ITS ERADICATION) EMPHASIZE THE NEED TO REVERSE THE CONCEPTION OF THE AGED MENTAL PATIENT AS ONE WHO HAS NO SOCIAL CONTRIBUTION TO MAKE AND FOR WHOM CUSTODY ALONE IS INDICATED.' IT IS SUGGESTED THAT A CONTINUATION OF THE THERAPEUTIC MILIEU OUTSIDE OF THE HOSPITAL CAN SERVE AS A STEPPING-STONE FOR THE PATIENT'S RETURN TO SOCIETY. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
