AB  - Examined the characteristics of 447 cocaine dependent, 1st admission outpatients (mean age 31.9 yrs) in relation to their AIDS risky behavior at intake to find out whether there was a reduction in risky behavior at follow-up 9 mo after admission, and whether reductions in risky behavior were related to patient characteristics, group as compared to individual treatment, or time in treatment. Ss engaging in higher AIDS risky behaviors were not characterized by any particular demographic profile or by a lack of knowledge about HIV/AIDS. They had higher scores on the SCL-90-R symptom scales and the Beck Depression Inventory, and higher ratings on the Drug, Alcohol, Family, and Medical scales of the ASI. At 9-mo follow-up, AIDS risky behaviors as measured by the Risky AIDS Behavior Inventory had decreased significantly. The degree of improvement predicted by higher intake problem severity and psychological symptomatology scale scores. While improvement in risky behavior was not related to type of treatment or duration of treatment, it was related to decreased substance use. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
