AB  - As of July 15,2020,137,106 peopleinthe United States had died from coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), according to Johns Hopkins University. There are clear health disparities running through that number: "African Americans are at much higher risk of contracting COVID-19 than the rest of the population, and they are much more likely" than whites to die from it, says a May 2020 Pew Stateline article, which cites APM Research Lab data. Reporters Mike Ollove and Christine Vestal contacted the sixteen states "where black residents make up a larger percentage of the population than the national rate of 13%." In most of those states, "the prevalence of death [from COVID-19] among black residents exceeded their population share"-in two states "by 25 percentage points," Pew analysis found.
