AB  - Results of the eight-year SUPPORT study (funded by The Robert Wood Johnson Foundation [RWJF] and published in November 1995 in the Journal of the American Medical Association), which indicated that end-oflife care in America needs much improvement, as well as the possibility of assisted suicide becoming legal in the United States, prompted the AMA to think of strategies for helping physicians to do better at caring for dying patients. Health Professions Education The Johns Hopkins University, School of Hygiene and Public Health, Baltimore, MD. With this one-time grant, Hopkins seeks "to develop, pilot test and evaluate models for integrating public health-oriented preventive and early intervention services within managed care" and to produce and test materials for training practitioners to use these approaches, according to Pew Charitable Trusts materials. RWJF maintains that "ideology-rather than reality-has been driving substance abuse/addiction policy." [...]one of the project's goals is to disseminate "current, research-based information," which does exist, the foundation said. Except for the issue of parity of coverage for mental health care, "behavioral health policy issues have not elicited the same degree of interest among government decisionmakers or the general public" as have physical illness, delivery of general medical care, and insurance coverage issues, this grantee has found.
