AB  - Investigates the effectiveness of a community support group for human immunodefiency virus (HIV)-infected adults who volunteered from both treatment & from the community. Participation in the group ranged from attending 0 to 20 sessions. Group attendance at 4+ sessions was associated with positive change in time free from drugs, reduction of drug or alcohol use during sex, & disclosing seropositivity status to all sex partners. These positive changes were also associated with completing 28+ days of residential treatment. Neither group attendance nor drug treatment was associated with increases in condom use. Group discussion focused on drug addiction issues rather than safer sex issues. It is concluded that community support groups for HIV-positive drug users appear to be a useful adjunct to drug treatment in reducing drug use, but need to provide an equally strong focus on risky sexual behavior.
