AB  - Patients with coexisting psychosis & substance use disorders typically have poorer outcomes than patients diagnosed with either disorder alone. A review of epidemiology & treatment outcomes reveals organizational barriers that may impede the appropriate detection, referral, & treatment of these patients. Frequently, treatment services for such dually diagnosed patients are not integrated, although there is evidence that integrated treatment of both disorders leads to better treatment outcome & fewer rehospitalizations. Presented are descriptions of a substance abuse consultation-liaison service to all psychiatric inpatient units & the outpatient dual diagnosis program of the McLean Hospital, a 180-bed private nonprofit teaching hospital of Harvard U Medical School. Dually diagnosed patients are identified, group therapy is provided during hospitalization, & referrals are made to treatment for both psychiatric & substance use disorders following discharge. Additional studies are needed to ascertain longer-term treatment outcome & efficacy of this system of intervention. 46 References. Adapted from the source document.
