AB  - A cancer patient who uses heroin can't gain reasonable access to methadone to treat his disorder until he qualifies for hospice.Not long after his discharge from the hospital, my patient-I'll call him James-started using heroin again. Then he missed his chemotherapy appointments. Then his radiation appointments. Then his cancer roared back to life, and he returned to our hospital confused, in pain, and dying. The slim silver lining to his story is that his cancer is now so advanced he qualifies for hospice, which means that his doctors can finally treat him with methadone-all he wanted in the first place.Drug overdose is now the leading cause of death among Americans under age fifty, according to a 2017 analysis of data from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reported in the New York Times. A meta-analysis in the BMJ the same year found that methadone treatment cuts the risk of death from opioid use disorder (opioid addiction) by more than half. It decreases deaths due to overdose, and it also decreases deaths from other drug-related causes, such as trauma, infection, and cancers left untreated. In our current crisis of opioid-related deaths, one might expect that our government, our health care systems-all of us-would be frantically working to increase access to this lifesaving medication.
