AB  - Describes the use of general systems theory and group-as-a-whole concepts to understand the dynamics of the therapeutic milieu on the inpatient psychiatric unit. With this information, the therapist can make interventions on an individual and group level that will help patients understand and deal with their own psychopathology. Another benefit of this process is that it will develop a community on the psychiatric unit in which patients help each other get well. Three clinical examples (a 32-yr-old woman with a history of depression, substance abuse, and brief psychotic episodes who attempted suicide; a 42-yr-old man with severe depression; and a middle-aged schizophrenic man) illustrate the relevance of these concepts. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
