AB  - Performed a study on 105 male alcoholic patients in an alcoholic rehabilitation unit that compared effectiveness of conventional alcoholism treatment with similar treatment combined with nutritional therapy (diet modifications, vitamin and mineral supplements, and nutrition education classes). 50 Ss received the conventional treatment and 55 Ss the combined therapy. On discharge from treatment, no significant differences between the 2 groups were found in anxiety (the Taylor Manifest Anxiety Scale), depression (the MMPI Depression Scale), or health. However, 6 mo following discharge, 37.8% of the group receiving conventional therapy remained sober, whereas 81.3% of the group receiving nutritional therapy in addition to conventional therapy remained sober. Results support theories that biochemical imbalances may cause a vulnerability to and a propensity for alcoholism. A combination of psychotherapy and nutritional therapy may be more successful for achieving long-term recovery from alcoholism than conventional treatment methodology. (37 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
