DA  - 2020///
Y1  - 2020///
AB  - Background: In stimulant use disorder (SUD) drug use potently activates the reward system, with repeated use causing pathological changes that lead to a bias towards drug incentives [1]. During anticipation of rewards this system is also activated, and previous neuroimaging studies investigating these neural correlates have utilised the monetary incentive delay task (MIDT) [2]. This uses monetary incentives to activate regions across the anticipatory reward network. Whilst neuroimaging studies have shown reduced activation of this system in addiction [3], such reductions have not been reported in SUD [4,5]. Therefore, abnormalities in the brain reward system in SUD may instead be reflected through differences in how its regions are interconnected. Objective(s): We aimed to replicate previous findings showing similar levels of activation between SUD and healthy volunteers during monetary reward anticipation, and investigate how connectivity between key regions of the reward system such as the ventral striatum and ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC) differ in SUD by using dynamic causal modelling (DCM). We hypothesised no between-group differences in task-related activation whilst anticipating monetary reward, but drug users would show lower effective connectivity across the reward network, specifically from the vmPFC to the ventral striatum. Method(s): A total of 89 individuals with a mean age of 33.5 (+/-8.2) years were recruited via community treatment centres and advertisements before being grouped into SUD (N=41) and healthy controls (N=48). All participants underwent functional neuroimaging whilst performing the MIDT. After observing a cue signalling a potential monetary reward, participants anticipated a visual stimulus to which they responded quickly with a button press. Neutral cues (i.e. no reward) were also included. We analysed group mean activations by fitting a reward versus neutral contrast to a general linear model and conducting independent-samples t-tests in FSL. DCM analysis was carried out in SPM12. We selected seven regions a priori based on functional evidence and consistent task-related activations in MIDT meta-analyses. Group comparisons of mean effective connectivity were conducted via a Parametric Empirical Bayes model with posterior probability p>0.99. Result(s): Both groups were matched for age, but not gender (X2=9.1, p=0.002). As expected, participants responded faster to monetary versus neutral cues. During reward anticipation, all participants showed robust task-related activation in the brain reward system, including the ventral striatum, vmPFC, dACC and insula. As hypothesised, no between-group differences were found. Our DCM analysis revealed several significant connectivity differences between our reward-related ROI's, including excitatory connectivity from the vmPFC to the ventral striatum in controls but not in SUD, and inhibitory connectivity from the insula to the dACC in SUD, but not in controls (p<0.01). Conclusion(s): Our results demonstrate that whilst SUD patients may show similar activation in the anticipatory reward network to healthy controls, underlying connectivity between regions differs. This is characterised by weaker connections between regions ascribing value and salience to monetary rewards, consistent with evidence showing decreased sensitivity to non-drug rewards in SUD [1]. This study demonstrates that connectivity analyses may be a useful addition to standard fMRI analysis to reveal further insights into reward processing in addiction. Disclosure statement: TWR consults for Cambridge Cognition, Lundbeck, Otsuka, and Mundipharma. He also holds a research grant from Shionogi. The authors declare no additional conflict of interests.Copyright © 2020
