AB  - Objectives: Substance use during pregnancy has a significant negative impact on maternal, fetal, newborn and child health. Although few randomized controlled trials for the treatment of perinatal substance use disorder (SUD) exist, it is important to know what treatments are available for pregnant women and how to counsel women about the risks of substance use, as well as the risks and potential benefits of available treatment options. Method(s): An overview of the prevalence and impact of substance use during pregnancy, including alcohol, tobacco, marijuana, and opioids on maternal, fetal, newborn, and child health, will be provided. Studies to date that investigate pharmacological treatment options for perinatal substance use, as well as their limitations, will be discussed. Findings from these studies will be translated for potential application to clinical practice. Result(s): Despite the methodological limitations of many studies examining the impact of substance use in pregnancy, overall the risks of untreated substance use often outweigh the risks of pharmacological treatments. Engaging patients in shared-decision making can help facilitate informed treatment choices that support patient values and preferences. Conclusion(s): With an understanding of the risks of perinatal substance use and the evidence base for treatment, attendees will be equipped to provide appropriate counseling and treatment recommendations for pregnant women with SUD. SUD, INF, DEVCopyright © 2018
