AB  - Clients who entered and remained in day treatment for at least 2 wks (N = 66, mean age 33 yrs) were interviewed using the Addiction Severity Index, the Beck Depression Inventory, the Symptom Sign Checklist-90-R, and a social support measure, and were interviewed at 6, 12, and 18 mo after baseline. Follow-up rates for each time point ranged from 58%–68%, and 30 clients were interviewed at every time point. Those who were consistently followed did not differ from those who were not consistently followed on demographics or on outcome variables measured at baseline. Comparison of mean outcome scores across time using multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA) showed significant changes from baseline to 6 mo in the areas of alcohol, drug, legal, and social problem severity, and these changes were maintained up to 18 mo postadmission. Outcomes reflecting psychiatric symptoms and employment also changed over time, but showed different patterns of change. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
