AB  - Discusses varying definitions of drug problems & approaches to treatment in Finland in a study of 12 specialized treatment units experienced in drug treatment. The majority of the programs incorporated both detoxification & rehabilitation. Basic treatment methods included therapeutic community, individual & group therapy, & individual treatment decisions. However, each program employed a unique range of methods involving various activities, network therapy, & cognitive behavior therapy. Choice of method was influenced by definition of the drug problem determined through differential emphases on physical, social, & psychological variables; dependence was the most common definition. The majority of units were reluctant, but not absolutely opposed, to use medication for the sake of treatment, & debate regarding the propriety of treating drug & alcohol abuse patients together remains problematic. Although a wide range of services are available, Finland still lacks a consistent medical detoxification & methadone maintenance program, & it is concluded that knowledge of available treatment alternatives may not reach those in need of the information. 1 Table, 2 Figures, 17 References. Adapted from the source document.
