AB  - The increasing population of people with Alzheimer's disease and related disorders, the length of the diseases, and the high cost of long term care have shifted the primary responsibility for care to the American family. Assumption of this responsibility imposes significant burdens on caregivers unprepared to manage the complex behavioral presentations that accompany the functional decline. The Progressively Lowered Stress Threshold (PLST) model is a conceptual framework long used in long term care to simplify care planning and evaluation. Based on concepts of stress, the model identifies six triggers of secondary behavioral symptoms and excessive disability. The model identifies how to modify the routine and environment to minimize behavioral triggers. This study was part of a multi-site randomized control trial where half of the subjects received training in PLST model, and the other half received traditional training, including a copy of a popular lay text on Alzheimer's disease. Subjects were followed for one year with repeated measures of quantitative instruments measuring various mental health concepts. In addition, caregivers kept logs for six months where secondary behavioral symptoms, interventions, critical incidents, appraisals of interventions and distress were recorded and submitted biweekly. This study analyzed qualitative and quantitative data from the 383 behavior logs of fifty (E = 25; C = 25) randomly selected care dyads from the larger sample. Differences emerged between the two groups. Caregivers who received PLST training reported significantly fewer secondary behavioral symptoms in their care recipients and had significantly stronger appraisals of the efficacy of their interventions. Most relevant were interventions used by each group and caregiver reports of their own emotions. Caregivers in the PLST group used activities as their primary intervention strategies, whereas controls used &ldquo;ignoring&rdquo; the behavior and walking away from the care recipient. Controls used more community services, family assistance, and called &ldquo;911&rdquo; more frequently. Controls had more negative appraisals of the caregiving experience, family conflict, and expressed jealousy of family members who were free from daily caregiving responsibilities. These findings suggest the PLST caregivers became more self-reliant than the comparison group. Additional research is needed to validate these findings. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
