PY  - 2022
DA  - 2022///
Y1  - 2022///
AB  - Many veterans exposed to combat develop posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD). The Mantram Repetition Program (MRP) is a novel treatment that has demonstrated success treating PTSD in veterans. Previous MRP trials suggest Existential Spiritual Well-Being (ESWB) and Mindfulness may mediate the effects of MRP on PTSD when delivered across group format. The goal of the present study was to replicate and extend previous MRP research across individual delivery. The current study investigated ESWB and Mindfulness as mediators when the MRP was delivered through individual therapy and compared to a Present Center Therapy (PCT) control group. Secondary analyses were conducted on archival data participants (n = 173) who were randomly assigned to the MRP Group (n = 69) or PCT Group (n = 72), and assessed at pre-treatment and eight weeks post-treatment. Hayes' (2008) PROCESS macro in SPSS was used to test change scores pre - post treatment of ESWB and Mindfulness as mediating the relationship between the MRP and PTSD by measuring the significance of indirect effects. Exploratory analyses were conducted to further understand the involvement of ESWB's subscales, Peace, Meaning, and Faith in the MRP. ESWB, measured by the Functional Assessment of Chronic Illness Therapy – Spiritual 12 (FACIT-Sp-12) scale, significantly predicted a decrease in PTSD severity (b = -1.55, t(131) = -7.05, p < .00), and the ESWB subscale Meaning predicted a decrease in PTSD severity (b = 2.0, t(131) = 2.74, p < .05). MRP predicted an increase in ESWB Peace (b = 1.16, t(131) = 2.0, p < .04). ESWB and Mindfulness were not supported as mediating a reduction in PTSD symptoms across individual delivery. The mechanisms of action when MRP is delivered individually are still uncertain. Future research exploring the role of ESWB, Mindfulness, and their subscales in the MRP is warranted to gain an understanding of the mechanisms of actions in the MRP across multiple different delivery formats. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
