AB  - INTRODUCTION AND OBJECTIVE: Approximately 40% of men with stress urinary incontinence (mSUI) are>= 65 years old and 70% of mSUI operations are performed in men >=70. However, little is known about their health characteristics or ultimate treatment. To help inform patient-centered treatment decision-making, we sought to describe the characteristics of this population and their association to the treatment received. METHOD(S): We identified male patients aged >=65 seen at the University of California, San Francisco Medical Center and the San Francisco Veterans Affairs Medical Center for initial consultation for mSUI from June 2015 - March 2020. Using retrospective chart review and telephone interviews, we ascertained demographic information, incontinence scores, Charlson Comorbidity Index (CCI, score >=4 indicates significant morbidity), frailty with the Timed Up and Go (TUG) test, functional dependence with activities of daily living (ADL), calculated life expectancy, and assessed mental health and quality of life (QOL). Bivariate analysis evaluated associations between subject characteristics and ultimate treatment type (conservative vs. surgery; sling vs. sphincter). Logistic multivariable models evaluating treatment choice were also constructed. RESULT(S): The 130 participants had a mean age of 75, a mean incontinence score of 14.2 +/- 4.4, and a CCI of 5.2 +/-2.0 (79% with significant morbidity). Over 70% had a 10-year mortality risk greater than 50%, 10% needed help with 1+ ADL and 22% were frail/prefrail. Mean physical and mental QOL scores were similar to the general population (51.3+/-9.1, 55.6 +/- 8.4). Anxiety and depression were reported by 3.9% and 10%. Only incontinence score was associated with conservative versus surgical treatment in uni- and multi-variable analyses (p<0.01). Among those who had surgery, those with sphincters had higher TUG scores (p<0.03) and higher likelihood of radiation (p<0.01) compared to those with slings; radiation remained significantly associated on multivariable analysis. CONCLUSION(S): Multi-morbidity, functional dependence, frailty, and limited life expectancy are common among older men with SUI. Despite the potential importance of these characteristics to treatment decision-making, for the most part they were not associated with ultimate treatment type. Understanding the health characteristics of this older patient population may improve individualized assessment, counseling, and treatment decision-making.
