AB  - Background: Each year, more than 600 000 people leave prison and re-enter the Nation's communities. Opioid use disorders (OUDs) are common among those in prison, and relapse and overdose occur at high rates upon re-entry. Many of these individuals have a mental health and substance use disorder, which contribute negative outcomes postrelease, including overdose, death and rearrest. Resumption of drug use precipitates or contributes to much of this recidivism. Treating drug-involved inmates is a potentially powerful strategy for reducing addiction's impact on public safety and public health. Due to the widespread use of illicit substances, specifically opioids, increases in the prison population, prevalence of co-occurring disorders in this population, and growth of systems for mandated treatment as stipulated in parole orders of releasing inmates, there is an increasing probability that the addiction clinician will provide services to this population and/or consult with agencies serving this population. This poster will enhance a clinician's understanding of the unique treatment and support needs of individuals with COD re-entering the community postincarceration and highlight some preliminary lessons learned Methods: This study used an open pilot design to provide MISSION-CJ within the MIREP to assess the efficacy of MISSION-CJ in reducing mental health symptom severity, substance use, and recidivism among men and women reentering the community postincarceration. Participants receive 3 months of pre-release and 6 months of postrelease wraparound services from the case manager and peer support specialist. After 9 months of program implementation, study participants include 95 individuals (45 men vs 50 women) incarcerated within the Michigan Department of Corrections at one male prison and one female prison. Eligible participants include individuals releasing to one of the three most densely populated counties within the state (Oakland, Wayne, and Macomb) and have a cooccurring opioid use and mental health disorder. Data are collected through repeated measures in the form of standardized screens including the K10 mental health screen, NIDA Quick Screen V2.0, and Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) Checklist, which are administered at enrollment, upon release, and upon graduation. In this first round of analyses, basic tests were run to assess general movement and trends among the 42 participants who had been released and also among the seven individuals who had successfully completed the program. Result(s): Preliminary analyses indicate that program participants who had been released showed improvements in their mental health and PTSD symptoms during the first 9 months of operation. Additionally, program participants reported a decrease in drug cravings from pre- to postincarceration. Decrease in drug cravings was seen at a higher rate among those receiving medication-assisted treatment (MAT). Program graduates showed the same trends plus an increase in taking psychotropic medication, obtaining drivers license, and obtaining employment. Qualitative data from the peer support specialists confirm that employment, healthy supports, and MAT are common among successful participants. Participant focus groups and individual interviews highlight the overwhelmingly positive feedback from participants about their satisfaction with the program, particularly their interactions with the clinical teams Conclusion(s): Preliminary analyses show very promising results related to substance use, mental health, and basic needs. Preliminary findings support the inclusion of peer support specialists on treatment teams, particularly those serving individuals in the criminal justice system. After the program is at least one year into implementation, the research team will begin to focus on those who received MISSION-CJ services only compared with those who received MISSIONCJ + MAT. Summary: Based on these preliminary findings, the state has decided to allocate funding to expanding programming to other regions in not only prisons but also jails. The next steps will be to conduct more complex analyses examining intensity of services with outcomes and to include relapse, recidivism, and ongoing mental health services as long-term outcomes. The research team will continue to use findings to inform practice (eg, evaluating and filling service gaps and providing needed training and technical assistance) and policy (eg, discussing MAT policy with Department of Corrections facility staff and community corrections). Policy implications include expanding the role of support services both before release and postrelease. Also, lessons learned include the critical need to work with the provider community to help educate them about working within correctional settings and with criminal-legal involved individuals, and in helping correctional officials understand the roles of behavioral health providers.
