AB  - In 1991 the former acts governing individual rights of liberty in case of psychiatric commitment were superseded by a new law, the ''Unterbrinpungsgesetz'' (UbG), providing new, comprehensive regulations in this difficult area of medico-legal interface. Not only does the UbG provide for shortened intervals between magistrates' verification sessions, it also introduced the new function of ''patients' counselor'', a person endowed with legal power to uphold a committed person's rights of liberty. Additionally, the new law defines valid psychiatric reasons for commitment and treatment very stringently, demanding behavior endangering oneself or others, deriving directly from a psychiatric illness. Using administrative data from 1990 to 1994 from a regional psychiatric hospital (catchment area 350,000) our study demonstrates that certain groups of patients are clearly put at a disadvantage by this new law. Main evidence derives from basic readmission data regarding patients with only short inhospital periods - commitment having been revoked by the magistrates after interverntion of the ''patients' counselor''. These patients are younger, usually single, psychotic persons of both sexes, either schizophrenics or affectively ill(manic), persons with drug/alcohol abuse, and those with acute depressive stress reactions. Therefore, a population of very vulnerable patients is led into a form of revolving-door psychiatry and/or petty criminal behavior, the locally numerous social institutions not being approached by, or reaching, this group. These patients would profit in psychic stability from longer therapy, structured settings, and continuation of treatment after dismissal. The present situation of this patient group seems unsatisfactory in humanitarian medical and economical aspects.
