AB  - Tested the effects of a long-term psychosocial nursing intervention designed to decrease mental distress in adolescents following a catastrophic event. Advanced Practice Psychiatric Nurses conducted the Catastrophic Stress Intervention (CSI) in 2 South Carolina high schools for 3 years following Hurricane Hugo. The CSI consisted of 9 protocols designed to decrease adolescents' mental distress by increasing their understanding of stress and by enhancing their self-efficacy and social support. 1,030 adolescents (aged 13-18 yrs) were randomized to intervention or control groups and completed 1 baseline and 5 post-intervention measures of mental distress, self-efficacy, and social support. The hypothesis was that intervention adolescents would have less mental distress than controls. Repeated measures multivariate analysis of variance, with exposure to the hurricane, self-efficacy, and social support as control variables, showed that intervention adolescents reported less mental distress than controls at 12, 18, and 24 mo but that this difference dissipated by 30 and 36 mo. Implications for the CSI and timing of interventions with adolescents after a catastrophic event are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
