AB  - Background: Failure to respond to treatment for hepatitis C virus (HCV) has become rare with the advent of the remarkably efficacious direct-acting antiviral (DAA) therapies. Sustained virological response at 12 weeks after treatment (SVR12) has been consistently greater than 85% in large international real-world studies. Australia represents one of the first countries to be granted unrestricted access to these medications. We aimed to examine the characteristics and predictors of treatment failure in a real-world setting of unrestricted treatment access. Method(s): Prospective outcome data were collected on all patients initiating DAA therapy for HCV at the Royal Adelaide Hospital between 1 March 2016 and 28 February 2017. Additional retrospective data, including biochemistry, FibroScan score, and comorbidities, were collected, and outpatient letters were reviewed for treatment failures. Cirrhosis was defined as median FibroScan score > 12.5 kPa, aspartate aminotransferase to platelet ratio index (APRI) > 1.0, or prior biopsy finding or clinician assessment of cirrhosis. Rurality was measured using the Accessibility/Remoteness Index of Australia (ARIA). Patients were excluded from this analysis if follow-up was not completed, SVR12 was not checked, treatment was discontinued, or the patient died during treatment. Result(s): Virological failure was confirmed in 27/454 patients (6%) treated with DAA therapy. The median age was 56 years (range, 36-63 years). Of the treatment failures, 20/27 (70%) were genotype 3, 4/27 (15%) were genotype 1a, 2/27 (7%) were genotype 2, and 1/27 (3%) was genotype 1 unspecified. Overall, SVR12 rates were highest for genotype 1 (262/267, 98%) compared with genotype 2 (11/13, 85%) and genotype 3 (153/173, 88%). Nineteen of the 27 patients (70%) were treatment-naive, 21/27 (78%) were male, 3/27 (11%) were incarcerated, and 11/27 (41%) resided in a rural area (ARIA 1-4). Nine of 27 (33%) were cirrhotic, most of whom (7/9, 78%) were Child-Pugh A. Comorbidities noted in the treatment failure group included diabetes (6/27, 22%), obesity (3/27, 11%), alcohol use (3/27, 11%), and hepatocellular carcinoma (2/27, 7%). On univariate analysis, compared with those achieving SVR12, those in whom DAA therapy failed displayed a significantly lower pre-treatment platelet count (mean, 195 vs 231, P = 0.04), lower pre-treatment albumin level (37 vs 39, P = 0.02), and higher FibroScan score (mean, 15 kPa vs 10 kPa, P = 0.02). Treatment failure was not significantly associated with age, sex, rurality, APRI, linguistically diverse background, prior treatment experience, or incarceration. In the genotype 3 treatment failure group, 17/19 (89%) received a 12-week course of sofosbuvir/daclatasvir (SOF/DAC) therapy. Six of these patients receiving 12 weeks of SOF/DAC had suggestion of advanced fibrosis, with an APRI score > 1.0 (5/17, 29%), while one had a FibroScan score of 11.5 kPa despite an APRI score of 0.88, and another patient had a history of hepatocellular carcinoma and was treatmentexperienced despite normal APRI and FibroScan scores. End-of-treatment HCV polymerase chain reaction (PCR) was positive in 1/6 patients (17%). Two relapsers (2/27, 7%) were detected on PCR 24 weeks after treatment, following a negative SVR12. These were both Child-Pugh B cirrhotics, with genotypes 2 and 3. Conclusion(s): Treatment failure with DAA therapy in this real-world cohort was associated with genotypes 2 and 3, cirrhosis and lower pretreatment albumin and platelet count. Consideration may be made for a longer treatment course for genotype 3 patients who display markers suggestive of advanced fibrosis or who are borderline in their assessment.
