AB  - New parents face a number of challenges that are amplified by economic and social disadvantage. These challenges influence the development of parenting self-efficacy, or a parent's belief in their abilities in this role. Parenting efficacy is not only correlated with one's own mental and physical health, but this construct also plays a role in positive child outcomes. A manualized psychoeducation and nutrition program was developed through a Pepperdine University Graduate School of Education and Psychology-Women, Infants, and Children (WIC) partnership with the aim of addressing emotional health barriers within the context of promoting better nutrition in underserved communities. A quantitative pilot study was conducted as a means to examine the effectiveness of two concurrent treatment groups and explore the impact of program participation on parenting self-efficacy and mental health factors. It is hypothesized that participation in the groups will improve parenting self-efficacy. We also propose that parenting self-efficacy will be positively correlated with socioeconomic status and nutrition efficacy and negatively correlated with parent-reported stress and depression. The implications for program development and manual revisions were discussed. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
