AB  - I am a health visitor and work in the UK where health visitors are registered nurses with post-registration training. We are based in the community and traditionally work with all newly born infants and their families in whatever context we encounter them. I undertook the Infant Mental Health course to Master's level at the Tavistock Centre in London which has changed my thinking and ways of working to promote emotional well being with families. I believe not only that an understanding of psychodynamic concepts can assist in the early identification of emotional problems and adverse patterns of relating between parent and child, but also that the health visitor can intervene early and work to promote good emotional development within the family. Knowledge, particularly of Bowlby's attachment model, has helped me to identify parenting qualities and styles of attachment that can predict likely outcomes for infants and can be used to explain the health visitor's role in terms of being a secure base that can be relied upon when needed. Fonagy's 'self reflectiveness' helps me to think about my effect on the family. Another concept, Bion's theory of container-contained, can be used as the rationale for the health visitor's containing role, not only with families but also with the other health practitioners in the team. I will discuss the role of the health visitor in relation to infant and family mental health. I will use the casework of two families: in one a baby died and the other a baby had a profound global disability. I will also show the importance of the space for thought given by the work discussion group at the Tavistock Centre, which enabled me to adapt to a more therapeutic style of working. This was part of the Infant Mental Health Master's course held there. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
