AB  - Purpose of the Study: The primary purpose of this psychological phenomenological study was to explore and capture the unique life experiences of young adult homeless African American males and their perspectives of resiliency as they transition into independent living. The information gained from this qualitative research design may hopefully provide valuable insight into the development of culturally sensitive intervention and prevention programs specific to their needs. In addition, results may assist counselors and other mental health providers in broadening their knowledge pertaining to specific needs of this underserved population in an effort to advocate and promote social action. Method: Utilizing Moustakas' (1994) approach for conducting research, semi-structured and open ended individual and focus group interviews were conducted with five homeless African American males ranging in age from 18 to 21 who resided at the Covenant House's Rights of Passage (ROP) transitional living center, located in Houston, Texas. Interview data were then organized for process analysis and reported as research findings. Trustworthiness was achieved through the process of triangulation by gathering rich and thick descriptions, conducting debriefing interviews, and member checking. Results: By employing Moustakas' (1994) modification of Van Kaam's (1959, 1966) final stage of analyzing phenomenological data, synthesis of information was attained by merging both individual textural and structural description themes of each participant's responses in order to capture statements and illuminate "what" they experienced and "how" they overcame adversity and gained resiliency. Implications and Future Research: In the findings of this research study, the need for cultural specific training and intervention/prevention programs that address the needs of homeless young adult African American males were recommended. Finally, the need for advocacy in funding to improve and build more transitional living programs for homeless youth as well as future research on resiliency pertaining to young African American homeless males were suggested. KEY WORDS: Homelessness, Runaways, Abuse, African American males, Youth, Resiliency, Transitional living programs, Crisis shelters, Culturally competent. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; e-mail: disspub@umi.com
