AB  - The purpose of this study was to examine a group therapy program for socially vulnerable children through the utilization of social perception training, a newly developed cognitive approach for augmenting complex social skills. Participants were recruited from the Child Anxiety Disorders Clinic, Center for Psychological Services, Fairleigh Dickinson University. Those families who were eligible and consented to participate completed an assessment battery pre- and post-group intervention. Dependent samples t-tests were computed among all outcome variables to compare pre- and post-test scores. Results for parent and child self-reports of social skills and social anxiety, as well as assessments of child participants' pragmatic skills and nonverbal accuracy, were non-significant. However, child and parent composite ratings of anxiety severity significantly decreased from pre- to post-intervention (Cohen's d for principal anxiety disorder from pre to post = 1.36). In addition, 58% of the sample met criteria for improved functioning (33% met high end-state functioning and 25% met treatment responder status) at the end of treatment. Forty-two percent of the sample no longer met criteria for an anxiety disorder at post-treatment. The present study contributes to the understanding of the socially vulnerable population, particularly children experiencing comorbid anxiety and executive functioning deficits. Preliminary findings suggest that social perception training may be an acceptable intervention for the socially vulnerable population, given that the gains in the present study are on par or better than standard treatment gains. Further study regarding the comorbid nature of anxiety and executive functioning deficits in the pediatric population appears warranted. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
