AB  - Investigated factors related to the place of treatment of mentally disabled people from the standpoint of their family members. Subjects (Ss) were 1,238 family members of mentally disabled people who belong to the Fukushima Prefecture Joint Association of Families with The Mentally Ill in Japan. Ss were administered a questionnaire about their current and desirable future situations of hospitalization and outpatient care of their mentally disabled family members, Ss' burdens of taking care of their mentally disabled family members, utilization of social resources, and Quality of life in January, 2001. The results showed that: (1) 667 Ss' mentally disabled family members were inpatients and 571 were outpatients; (2) the inpatients were significantly older, suffered longer, had older onset of the mental illness, and there were smaller differences between males and females than with the outpatients; (3) more Ss with outpatients were parents than Ss with inpatients were; (4) 59.3% of the Ss with the inpatients hoped that the hospitalization would continue and 1.7% of the Ss with the outpatients hoped for hospitalization of the outpatients; and (5) the more mentally disabled people were handicapped in life, the Ss hoped for their hospitalization. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
