AB  - Evaluated treatment outcomes of 104 program residents (mean age 31.4 yrs) from 2 psychosocially oriented community residential facilities for patients with persistent and severe mental disorders and multiple failures at community tenure. Hospital and crisis center admissions and days were significantly reduced during the follow-up year. At 1-yr follow-up, a significantly greater proportion of Ss were employed and living independently, and fewer were homeless. Findings suggest that psychosocial residential treatment models can offer cost-effective and clinically efficacious care to persistently mentally ill patients. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
