AB  - Background: 30% of nurses experience burnout at any given time. Burnout is comprised of emotional exhaustion, depersonalization of clients, and feeling ineffective. COVID has exacerbated this already prevalent problem through longer shifts, shortages of resources, disrupted sleep, altered work/life balance, lack of child care, lack of personal protective equipment, fear of contracting/spreading COVID, social isolation, incivility, vaccine hesitancy, and the perception that providers are being asked to provide care that exceeds their level of comfort. Many in healthcare describe these added stressors as robbing them of the very sense of purpose that drives their sense of duty to their professions. Some of the usual buffers against burnout, such as family and social support, have been upended during the COVID pandemic. There is a well-established link between burnout and negative outcomes for providers including suicide, mental health issues and substance abuse. Ultimately, burnout leads to poor patient outcomes, decreased patient safety, lower productivity and increased medical errors. COVID-associated burnout has resulted in significant increases in staff turnover and provider shortages. Method(s): Our team sought to build effective interventions to impact burnout. Interventions to address burnout are often decided at high level administration levels and often miss the mark. We interviewed frontline nurses for their perspectives to inform effective interventions. Result(s): Administrators focused on monetary rewards and short-lived staff appreciation efforts. Interventions recommended by the nurses included: offering free resiliency training classes as part of a weekend "retreat", conducting educational sessions for healthcare administrators to impact culture change at healthcare institutions, survey all staff for their input into concrete methods to impact burnout on-site, offer support groups with ensured staff anonymity, build on-site stress relieving interventions, improve teamwork, lessen staffing shortages, equalize compensation with traveling nurses, and sustain interventions to demonstrate appreciation. Conclusion(s): Obtaining input from intended intervention recipients can help avoid ineffective interventions while promoting interventions expected to have the greatest impact. Asking for input alone can improve staff's sense of being valued and decrease burnout.
