AB  - The article focuses on the role of allied health professionals and self-help groups in the management of patients with life-threatening diseases. Fifty-one organizations are involved in sponsoring the review committees that cooperate in the Committee on Allied Health Education and Accreditation of the American Medical Association, CAHEA system for accrediting educational programs in 26 allied health professions. Millions of U.S. citizens now participate in more than a half million self-help groups that address problems such as chronic health conditions, addiction, family care giving, or mental health. Self-help groups have been described as an effective, inexpensive, and highly accessible adjuvant for the givers as well as for then receivers of care. The National Self-Help Clearinghouse at the Graduate School and University Center, City University of New York: publishes the Self-Help Reporter, which contains information on the activities of state and regional self-help centers and clearinghouses, and "Leading Self-Help Groups: A Guide for Training Facilitators."
