AB  - Jews-in part because they were economically connected with the landlords-were seen as a major factor in those conflicts, which were caused by shortages and great financial difficulties for many ordinary citizens. [...]to a certain degree, general economic distress and therefore general social protest played a role as motives for anti-Jewish conflicts. * Only in a very general way, and only for 1830, could one argue (as Arno Herzig has done)9 that the Jews were seen as the cause of all change and the problems stemming from it. * Because of their being restricted to a position of poverty and low social status, many people might have felt the need to look down on somebody lower still on the social ladder-the Jews. [...]this region for the first time in Russian history was integrated into the world market so that, with the onset of the international agricultural depression of 1878-79, the effects were most profoundly felt in the southern regions of the Russian Empire around the Black Sea. [...]I think, questions of general policy and elements of change brought about by integration into the world market and rapid industrialization were more important in Russia than in Germany-at least more important than Rohrbacher makes out. Jews had to be integrated into different organizations, guilds and crafts, or similar institutions, and this made a change in their situation ever more difficult because it could only be gradual. [...]the fact that pogroms were so much more violent in Russia and more frequent than in Germany may have had to do with this rather peculiar and awkward development toward emancipation, which in any event was rather haphazard and, unfortunately, was no longer the aim of the government after the first wave of major popular violence against the Jews in 1881-82.
