Y1  - 2017///
AB  - Purpose: CF Making Effective Educational Talk and Support (M.E.E.T.S) was created to facilitate, in accordance with infection control guidelines, CF care provider-moderated, live interaction among cystic fibrosis (CF) patients to increase support and education. The goal of the support group was to address the need for social integration amongst CF patients that is appropriately moderated - unlike many social media platforms. Method(s): We pilot-tested 4 web-based support groups, enrolling 4 CF adults. We administered pre/post knowledge-based surveys to each participant linked to topics discussed in each session (mental health, lung transplant, airway clearance techniques and nutrition), needs assessment, and satisfaction survey. Each session was delivered via a live video conferencing platform, allowing patients to see and speak with one another and the moderator, the CF provider (social worker, physician, respiratory therapist, and nutritionist) with expertise in the topic. A CF patient post-transplant served as a guest speaker during the lung transplant session to speak about his experience and answer questions. All sessions were audio recorded and transcribed for thematic analysis. Result(s): We found the web-conference format feasible, cost-effective and easy to replicate. Preliminary data analysis showed a need for clinician- facilitated social support. All 4 participants agreed/strongly agreed that: A) the support group provided helpful information for living with CF; b) they would like to continue participating in the support group; c) they were comfortable asking questions and engaging with other members of the group; and d) the WebEx was easy to use. Conclusion(s): With the advent of infection control guidelines for CF patients, it is vital to find innovative ways for them to interact and learn from one another's medical journeys. Online media presents us with an opportunity to overcome infection control barriers and subsequent social isolation faced by many CF adults. Patients were very receptive to our pilot program and look forward to further education with their clinical team along with fellow CF patients. Creating a comfortable, medically directed platform provides a distinct space for CF patients to interact with one another as well as their clinical team, and create a judgment-free zone to discuss issues particular to CF care. There is a void in what is currently available for CF patients. This pilot program is a means of providing live, interactive and clinically-moderated support groups to our patients..
