AB  - Background: 23 million Americans have a substance use disorder (SUD), yet only 10% receive treatment annually (1). Among hospitalized patients, 22% have an active drug or alcohol use disorder. (2) Several interventions have demonstrated efficacy in improving outcomes for hospitalized patients with SUD, including offering a range of services to non-treatment seeking patients, brief intervention for unhealthy drinkers, initiating pharmacotherapy, and bedside assessment with motivational interviewing and facilitated treatment referral. (3-5) The challenge is implementing these interventions into real-world hospital practice. Method(s): This prospective, longitudinal study compared the 30-day post-discharge outcomes of patients who were or were not seen by an addiction consult team during their hospitalization. The study enrolled adult patients admitted to ten general medical floors with an AUDIT-C of 8 or higher, a positive NIDA single question drug screen, or identified to have SUD by nursing assessment. The addiction consult team was available on eight of ten floors. The decision to consult the addiction team was made by the primary clinicians independent of this study. Intervention patients were those who were evaluated by the consult team and control patient were those who were not. Participants were assessed in-person at baseline and 30 and 90 days later by telephone or mail. Primary outcomes were the addiction severity index composite scores for alcohol and drugs and self-reported days abstinent 30-days post-hospitalization. Secondary outcomes included the percentage of patients with self-reported emergency room visits and hospitalizations in the 30-days post index hospitalization. Analysis: Two-sample t-tests were used to compare mean change over time between the two groups. Due to concern for selection bias between those who did and did not receive an addiction consult, a subgroup of patients was selected from interventions and controls matching on major substance used and corresponding baseline ASI score within 0.05. Paired t-tests were used to compare mean change over time between the two groups within the subgroup. Result(s): Among the 229 who completed 30-day follow-up, 142 received the intervention and 87 did not. Among the 142, 139 had calculable ASI scores. Baseline ASI composite scores were higher in the intervention group (ASI-alcohol0.45, ASI-drug 0.13; n= 139) than in the control group (ASI-alcohol 0.27, ASI-drug 0.09; n = 87). Primary and secondary outcomes improved in both groups however the magnitude of improvement was greater in the intervention group vs. control group for nearly all outcomes (see table): mean ASI-alcohol decreased by 0.23 vs. 0.08 (p< 0.001); mean ASI-drug decreased by 0.05 vs. 0.01 (p < 0.001); days abstinent increased 13.3 vs. 5.4 (p< 0.0001); self-reported inpatient admissions decreased 66% vs. 54% (p = 0.001), and ED visits decreased 64% vs. 58% (p = 0.007). Self-reported overdose decreased by 78% vs. 73% (p = 0.89). To control for baseline differences, a subgroup analysis was limited to the 57 pairs of patients matched on major substance used and baseline ASI. The magnitude of improvement remained greater in the intervention group vs control group (see table): mean ASI-alcohol decreased 0.17 vs. 0.15 (p = 0.50), ASI-drug 0.05 vs. 0.02 (p = 0.05), days abstinent 10.0 vs. 8.2 (P = 0.37), inpatient admission 80% vs. 56% (P = 0.11), ER visit 78% vs. 59% (P = 0.40), and overdose by 92% vs. 67% (p = 0.84). Conclusion(s): Among hospitalized general medical patients with SUD, inpatient addiction consultation decreased severity of drug use at 30-days post-hospitalization. [Table Presented].
