AB  - Mild traumatic brain injuries and posttraumatic stress disorder are considered the signature wounds of the recent Operation Enduring Freedom and Operation Iraqi Freedom conflicts. A common symptom of both mild traumatic brain injuries and posttraumatic stress disorder is impaired sleep. Chronic sleep issues have been associated with functional impairments which broadly encompass decreased physical, psychological, and cognitive functioning. The purpose of this study was to examine how sleep disturbances and posttraumatic symptoms influence scores of functional impairment among veterans with a history of mild traumatic brain injury. Functional impairment was measured by the World Health Organization Disability Assessment Schedule 2.0. Symptoms of insomnia were measured by the Insomnia Severity Index while symptoms of posttraumatic stress disorder were assessed via the Clinician Administered PTSD Scale for the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fourth Edition. Data from 120 participants enrolled in a larger multi-site study conducted through the Department of Veterans Affairs were analyzed via correlation and one-way ANOVA. Data were separated into four groups based on whether individuals met criteria for PTSD, insomnia, or both conditions. The interaction between insomnia and posttraumatic stress symptoms was found to have the largest effect on functional disability. Veterans who did not meet criteria for either PTSD or insomnia were found to have the lowest mean functional impairment scores. Additionally, insomnia did not have as large of a main effect on functional impairment scores as posttraumatic stress disorder alone. Regarding additional analyses, demographic factors such as gender, age, race, and education were not significantly related to scores of functional impairments. However, VA disability ratings and income were significantly associated with scores of functional impairments. The clinical implications of these results, limitations of the study, and future directions are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
