AB  - Background: According to the literature [1], medical students are five-times more vulnerable to suffer from depression than the general population. As burnout and depression issues are rising over the medical profession, targeting stigma, defined as a mark of disgrace associated with a particular circumstance, quality or person, towards mental illness among undergraduate clinicians seems relevant to their future practice and personal health. Objective(s): To determine if (a) the Psychological Medicine module, taking place during the third semester of preclinical education, can reduce the stigma towards mental illness among medical students; and (b) a voluntary attendance based intervention taking place during the same third semester can enhance the reduction of the negative attitudes, if these are present. Method(s): All second-year medical students from the University of Valencia, Spain, were invited to participate voluntarily in an intervention aimed to reduce attitudes and degree of stigma towards mental illness. All participants completed twice an online, anonymized survey including the CAMI, RIBS and MAKS questionnaires twice: before and after taking the Psychological Medicine module. A subgroup of students attended a workshop led by three individuals with mental illnesses, one of them was a peer student. Result(s): From a total population of 296 students, 254 and 154 completed the survey before and after the module (response rates = 85.8 % and 52.02 %, respectively). From the study sample (n = 154 respondents), 24 students attended the workshop. Significant differences were found between workshop attenders (experimental group) and those who only took the Psychological Medicine module (control group). After the intervention, students attending the workshop significantly showed more positive attitudes regarding the dangerousness of people suffering from a mental illness as well as regarding compassion and empathy. These students significantly showed to have more close friends suffering from a mental health illness than the control group. However, controls (n = 130) significantly improved only in the pessimistic attitudes and the level of acceptance towards deinstitutionalisation and the attitudes towards having mental health facilities in the community. These students showed an improvement in their knowledge about the drugs addiction being or not a mental health illness. Both groups of students showed an increase in their disposition to live with someone suffering from a mental illness and to support the statement that mental illness is like any other disease. Conclusion(s): The results support that meeting someone with a mental illness may help reduce stigmatizing attitudes among medical students. Anti-stigma preventive measures should be implemented to educate the next generation of healthcare providers. A follow-up assessment after the intervention would allow to determine if the reduction of stigma remains maintained over the subsequent years in Medical School. Further studies are warranted to establish if long-lasting interventions could result to be more effective than a single encounter.Copyright © 2018
