AB  - Studied the effectiveness of group treatment (GT) for benzodiazepine (BZ) dependence among 11 women and 5 men referred to outpatient treatment in London, England. During 28 wks of treatment, 10 Ss took part in group sessions focusing on cognitive restructuring and coping skills training. The remaining 6 Ss received similar training via the telephone. The treatment program focused on a graded withdrawal schedule designed individually for each S. Ss completed the following instruments before treatment, at weekly intervals during treatment, and at 3 mo and 1 yr follow-up: the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory by C. D. Spielberger et al (1970); the Beck Depression Inventory by A. T. Beck (1978); and a withdrawal symptom scale. Ss in both groups reduced their BZ intake during treatment, but GT Ss showed lower attribution rates and better long-term outcome. Withdrawal symptoms, anxiety, and depression improved with reduction in BZ intake. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
