AB  - Objective: Level of cognitive dysfunctions is important determinant of outcome in schizophrenia. The present study aimed to compare the level and pattern of cognitive deficits in early-onset of schizophrenia (EOS) with adulthood-onset schizophrenia (AOS), but with the same duration of disease (at least ten years). Method(s): Two groups outpatients: EOS (N = 30) and AOS (N = 30) were investigated in their full or partial symptomatic remission. All outpatients were recruited from community-based support system. Paranoid schizophrenia was diagnosed based on ICD-10 criteria. Most of participants were treated with second generation antipsychotics. Psychopathological symptoms were assessed with the Positive and Negative Symptoms Scale, and general functioning with the Global Assessment of Functioning Scale. Levels of cognitive functioning was evaluated by neuropsychological battery. The study included matched healthy control group (N = 80). Result(s): Outpatients group differed significantly between each other: age, level of education, work and level of disability. No differences in the all psychopathological symptoms and general functioning between EOS and AOS group were observed. No significant group differences were found on measure of executive function (planning and problem solving), working memory, processing speed, verbal fluency, verbal memory and learning. Only in EOS cognitive deficits were significantly more severe in outpatients with a greater intensity of negative schizophrenia symptoms. Significant dysfunctions of various aspects of cognitive functions were found in the EOS and AOS, as compared to the control groups, but in some parts of the tests, individuals with schizophrenia and healthy subjects did not differ (measure of visual working memory and processing speed). Conclusion(s): The results suggest that the EOS and AOS groups have the same level and pattern of cognitive functioning after many years of psychosis. Probably EOS might not be associated with deterioration of cognitive functions but with their stabilization or improvement when long-term integrated therapy was implemented.
