AB  - l Objective: To assess whether a difference in venous reflux pattern, ulcer size or duration, regular medications or the daily use of walking aids can predict the healing of a chronic venous leg ulcer (VLU). l Method: In this prospective, randomised, single-centre study, 110 consecutive patients with chronic leg ulcers were assessed. Ninety-nine patients met the inclusion criteria and a controlled, conservative 3-month treatment period was initiated, in which 90 patients were managed with standardised local treatment combined with compression therapy. In this group, 62 VLUs healed within 12 weeks and 28 were still open after 12 weeks. The study group consisted of 22 patients with non-healed ulcers and a control group (n=28), which was selected randomly from the healers. In both groups, venous reflux profiles were assessed using colour-flow duplex imaging. l Results: The study and control groups did not differ in smoking habits, age, gender or daily oral medications. On average, the healing wounds were 5cm? before starting controlled treatment (range 1DS80cm?) and had been open for 7 months (range 2DS48 months); the non-healing wounds were on average 11.2cm? (range 1DS31cm?) and had been open for 26 months (range 8DS106 months). Venous disease severity scores were similar for both groups (12.6 vs. 13.4). Five patients (18%) with healed ulcers regularly used walking aids, the use of which was more frequent (36%) among non-healers (p<0.001). Venous reflux profiles differed significantly between the groups, with isolated superficial reflux noted in 64% of healers, compared with 36% of non-healers. In addition, isolated deep reflux was found in 14% of the healers, compared with 41% of non-healers (p=0.0002). The rate of popliteal reflux was significantly higher in non-healers (59% versus 21%; p=0.0004). l C onclusion: Long duration of a chronic venous ulcer may predict a poor outcome. The presence of deep venous reflux, especially in the popliteal vein, is typically found in those legs with non-healed ulcers. l C onflict of interest: None.
