AB  - Keywords: Service Development; Adults; Mental Health Purpose: The purpose of this work was to gain the perspectives of past/present users of AMH services to co-design future physiotherapy provision within the Trust. Given that individuals with serious mental illness are at increased risk of physical health co-morbidity such as musculoskeletal pain, neurological impairment, obesity and COPD; and that there are disparities in accessing healthcare services for those in this population group it was felt that service provision required updating. Historically physiotherapy provision had been provided by cover from mental health services for older people, however this project aimed to establish whether a more comprehensive service was required, and what any future service should consist of. This work was conducted in parallel with data-driven analysis of current physiotherapy provision also submitted for conference presentation. Method(s): This event took place at virtually via Microsoft Teams on 25/01/2021. Timings were decided in conjunction with participants recruited through the Trust Expert by Experience group headed by the Trauma Informed Approaches Locality Lead. Verbal feedback was obtained during the session with written notes taken by the physiotherapist chair. The perceptions of service users were explored in relation to: * Previous use/experience of physiotherapy services. * Barriers to accessing physiotherapy. * Preferences relating to future physiotherapy provision. * Suggestions for improvements. * Any other questions/comments. Result(s): This session was attended by 4 individuals with varying experience within AMH services. Individuals varied in their geographical location and were able to provide insight into previous/current services across Durham and Darlington, Teesside, and North Yorkshire localities. Participants commented on both their experiences of in-patient admissions and community services provided by TEWV, offering individual perspectives on the provision of physiotherapy services within AMH settings. * All participants felt that there was scope to improve current service provision. * Participants advised that future services should be spread across inpatients, outpatients, and community services. * At times participants considered their physical health to be neglected within mental health services and welcomed increased focus here. * Where possible, physiotherapy care should be provided by generic services however a "handover period" should be considered for ongoing intervention whilst awaiting generic physiotherapy input within the community. * Provide opportunity for patient decisions relating to location and provider for ongoing input following discharge from inpatient settings. Conclusion(s): Greater insight gained into the primary factors for consideration when expanding current AMH physiotherapy service provision. The initial session was conducted well with no issues relating to meeting virtually. Group discussion was productive allowing interaction between individuals to provide a considered viewpoint. Attendees were pleased to learn that the Trust is placing increased emphasis on physical health services. Service user involvement will continue to be embedded at al stages of the service development process. Impact: Alongside data-driven analysis of current provision, this work has helped to inform the design of a future service provision, ensuring that it is focused towards the priorities of service users. Future work will involve service users in the setup of proposed services, with the aim of generating an advisory group to influence future development and ensure that services remain focused on user-defined priorities. Funding acknowledgements: No funding provided for work.Copyright © 2021
