AB  - Research on caregiving by family members of adults diagnosed with a dementia has shown caregiving is stressful. Caregivers are at risk for both physical and emotional illness because of the high level of chronic stress to which they are exposed. Services provided for family caregivers try to decrease that stress by providing education and support. Support group meetings, the most readily available service to caregivers, provide both education and emotional support. The current study compared 12 caregivers who participated in two monthly support group meetings with a matched control group of caregivers who did not attend such meetings. It was hypothesized that measures of physical and emotional symptomatology, and caregiver burden, all indicators of stress, would be high for both groups prior to the support group intervention but would decrease with the intervention. Those caregivers caring for someone diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease were given the Alzheimer's Disease Knowledge Test. It was further hypothesized that those caregivers attending support group meetings would answer more of the questions correctly. The study showed that the support and control groups were equivalent on the dependent measures used except for the Pennebaker Instrument of Limbic Languidity (PILL). The group attending support group meetings reported more frequent symptomatology on the PILL at both baseline interviews and also at the 2 interviews after the intervention had begun. Except for this difference the 2 groups were equivalent on the other demographic data gathered and measures of stressful events, emotional symptoms, and caregiver burden. This equivalence did not change with the intervention or over the 14 weeks of the study. Subjects in both groups were matched closely on variables of gender, relationship to care receiver, and age to subjects in a previous study called Telecare. It was planned that these matched groups could be compared to the results of the previous study. 