AB  - Background: Stroke is the most common cause of lasting disability in older age, leading to both personal and familial burdens as well as high societal costs. The increasing incidence of stroke with higher age and decreasing stroke mortality, combined with an increasing number of people in the higher age groups, may eventually lead to substantially more persons suffering a stroke or having residual symptoms from previous stroke. Optimal rehabilitation is therefore important both for the individual stroke patient and regarding societal costs caused by stroke. Early supported discharge (ESD) is a rehabilitation concept emphasizing early discharge from institution to home for patients after stroke, followed by rehabilitation while home‐dwelling, and often supervised by a specialised multi‐disciplinary team. This has been studied in several randomised controlled trials previously and the results were analysed in an updated Cochrane report from 2012. This meta‐analysis suggested a beneficial effect of ESD for a selected group of stroke patients, but at the same time pointed towards the need for future studies in order to clarify which elements of ESD in the primary health care were important, and for more precise clarification of cost‐benefit questions. With this background the ESD Stroke Bergen study was established in order to 1: compare the rehabilitation results after ESD to rehabilitation as usual, and 2: investigate the effect of community treatment given in two different settings; either in a day unit (ESD 1) or in the patients' homes (ESD 2). The study was a three‐armed randomised controlled trial with two different ESD arms and one control arm. At the same time, the Municipality of Bergen wanted to develop and strengthen their community rehabilitation. This enabled a close cooperation between our hospital and the community during the study period which has resulted in a permanently established rehabilitation chain in our community. Methods and Procedures: Patients with suspected acute stroke living in the Municipality of Bergen were screened for inclusion, with a stroke within the previous seven days, being admitted to the stroke unit within the previous five days, and a National Institutes of Health Stroke Scale (NIHSS) score of 2‐26 as main inclusion criteria. There were no age limits. Exclusion criteria were serious psychiatric disorders, alcohol or substance abuse, other serious conditions of importance to the cerebral disorder or subsequent rehabilitation process, or poor knowledge of the Norwegian language. Patients in two of the three study arms were treated according to the ESD concept. They were followed by a designated multi‐disciplinary ambulatory team consisting of a nurse, a physiotherapist and an occupational therapist, from soon after admission to the stroke unit until shortly after discharge to home. This team originated from the Department of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation (DPMR) and served as a coordinating link between the patient, relatives, hospital personnel and the personnel in the primary health care. The team was particularly important in the discharge process and cooperated closely with the municipal health care in the planning and implementation of further treatment after discharge. The patients in the two ESD arms were discharged to their homes as soon as possible, sometimes after a period in a municipal institution. All patients in the ESD arms were offered rehabilitative treatment for up to 5 weeks by a multi‐disciplinary community health team, consisting of a nurse, a physiotherapist and an occupational therapist. They were also offered out‐patient 3‐ and 6‐months follow‐ups at DPMR with a physician together with a member of the DPMR multi‐disciplinary ambulatory team. The two ESD arms differed by the location of treatment: patients in the ESD 1 group received their treatment in a community day unit, whereas ESD 2 group patients stayed in their homes with home‐visits from the community health team. Patients in the third study arm constituted a control group and were treated as usual without any intervention from the study. The primary study outcome was modified Rankin Scale (mRS) at 6 months, while secondary outcomes included mRS at 3 months, NIHSS, Barthel ADL Index (BI) and patient satisfaction. Patients were tested and scored at baseline as well as at 3, 6, 12 and 24 months, with testers being blinded for the patient's study arm. Results: Three‐hundred‐and‐six patients were finally included with 55% men (mean age 69.6 years) and 45% women (mean age 75.6 years). 78% of patients in ESD groups 1 and 2 received the allocated treatment and the drop‐out rates at retest after 3 and 6 months were 20% in the ESD 1 and 2 groups versus 30% in the control group. The groups did not differ significantly at any point in time for mean values of the primary outcome mRS, nor for BI and NIHSS scores as well as all change scores. However, the two intervention groups generally showed somewhat better scores than the control group. Within‐group analyses from baseline to 3/6 months showed significant improvement for the ESD 1 and 2 groups from baseline to 3 months and a trend for improvement from baseline to 6 months, while there were no significant within‐group differences for the control group. mRS and BI scores dichotomised to independency (mRS ≤2; BI ≥95) or dependency (mRS >2; BI <95) demonstrated non‐significantly better results for mRS in the intervention groups pooled together vs. the control group. Change score for mRS at 3 months was significantly better in the pooled ESD groups than in the control group, but with no difference at 6 months. We also analysed for group differences in discharge destination from the stroke unit, length of continuous stay in hospital or municipal institution after the stroke as well as total days spent in the stroke unit, DPMR and municipal institution during the first six months after stroke. There were no significant differences between the groups, meaning that ESD patients were not discharged earlier than control patients (mean length of stay in the stroke unit was 11 days for all groups). The important determinator for the length of stay was whether the patients could be discharged directly home (mean 8 days in the stroke unit) or having to wait for additional in‐patient care (mean stay 15 days). Comments: The primary research aim was to investigate whether two different ESD regimes were superior to treatment as usual, but we found generally only small differences in favour of the ESD groups, and mostly not statistically significant. We think that the main reason for these rather small differences in rehabilitation outcome between the early supported groups and the treatment as usual group should be ascribed to the quite different and superior treatment of stroke patients today, as compared to 10‐15 years ago when most previous studies were carried out. It is also a possible that the chosen outcomes were too blunt to detect more qualitative differences between the ESD and control treatment, e.g. regarding quality of life. In addition, improved prophylaxis and treatment during the last decade has led to reduced neurological disability after stroke, in parallel with reduced in‐hospital time. The added effect of supported discharge and improved follow‐up seems to be rather modest, and larger patient samples are probably necessary to demonstrate a further benefit with statistical significance. On the other hand, the present study has led to a permanently much improved rehabilitation service in our community which will benefit patients beyond the stroke category. At present four community health teams are established, each serving two of our city's eight boroughs. The final model for ESD rehabilitation, and possibly rehabilitation outside of institution in general, should probably not be a question of treatment either at home or in a day unit, but rather a combination based on clinical judgement by the community health team for the individual patient. Also the duration of rehabilitation outside of institution should be flexible according to patient needs.
