AB  - A recent British Prison Service initiative has focused on providing sex offender programs facilitated by multidisciplinary teams. A 3-wk training program was designed to provide staff with the necessary knowledge, skills and experience to facilitate these structured treatment groups. 81 Ss (56 prison officers, 15 probation officers, 5 psychologists, and 5 staff from other backgrounds) provided background information on their experience and/or training relating to working with sex offenders. Ss completed the Attitudes Towards Prisoners (K. B. Melvin, L. K. Gramling, & W. N. Gardner, 1985) and Attitudes Towards Sexual Offenders (T. E. Hogue, 1993) questionnaires before and after training. After training, Ss were more confident in their ability to provide treatment and showed a greater belief that treatment could be successful. Ss had significantly more positive attitudes toward sexual offenders and offenders in general, and did not indicate undue concern about working in a sensitive treatment area. This pattern continued at 6-mo follow-up, indicating that the training changed attitudes as expected and was seen by Ss as adequately preparing them to provide structured sex offender treatment program in a secure setting. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
