AB  - Aims: The present study investigated the presence of cohort effects on gender differences in the course, severity, & symptomatology of DSM-III-R alcohol dependence in a community-based sample. Design: A comparison of substance-related variables among men & women divided into two groups based on the median birth year of the sample was conducted. Participants: Participants were 468 men & 132 women with life-time alcohol dependence, the vast majority of whom were born between 1941 & 1960. Measurements: Substance use & DSM-III-R substance use disorders were assessed by a structured interview administered in person. Findings: Individuals born after 1951 had higher rates of alcohol dependence. Among individuals with alcohol dependence, those born after 1951 had an earlier onset & longer duration of alcohol-related problems. Significant interactions indicated that these effects were stronger for women than men. Conclusions: Risk for alcohol dependence appears to be rising in younger generations, & particularly for younger women, making them an important target group for prevention & treatment programs. 4 Tables, 72 References. Adapted from the source document.
