AB  - Research has suggested that family functioning is a critical proximal outcome in family-based interventions and mediates the long-term outcomes of youth. However, it is rarely studied and not fully understood as a proximal treatment outcome. Based on the clear need to further understand the impact evidence-based family therapy models have on proximal functioning outcomes, this study measured the impact of a specific family-based treatment (Functional Family Therapy) on multiple measures of youth and parent functioning that are believed to ultimately mediate the impact of long-term behavior outcomes of youth as compared to two other treatments (supervised probation and other mental health services). These alternative treatment groups represent common treatments offered in local communities for youth in juvenile justice systems. A systematic assessment of pre and post parent, adolescent, and outside observer perspectives on youths' behaviors was conducted before and after treatment. In this study, family functioning was found to be an outcome impacted by the types of services (family-focused evidence-based treatment versus non-specific youth-focused treatments) and the quantity of services (fewer services versus more services). Adherence did not prove to be a significant mediating variable for treatment outcomes due to a variety of limitations. The findings indicate that the amounts and types of services adolescents receive can impact their functioning. Youth who receive individual-focused treatments that do not include parents are less likely to have improvements in proximal functioning outcomes that impact distal, long-term outcomes. These are important findings that set the stage for further study. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
