AB  - Background: Homelessness is increasing in Australia underpinned by a range of social, economic and individual factors. Evidence is emerging that people with severe mental illness are becoming increasingly concentrated in the homeless population leading to increased disengagement. Objective(s): The aim of this session is to review the relationship between homelessness and mental illness in Australia over the past 20 years and highlight changing patterns and emerging challenges. Method(s): A summary of relevant national and international literature will be presented followed by a summary of research conducted in Melbourne on homeless men over the past 20 years. Concepts of accommodation history, stability and mobility and community treatment resistance will be introduced and their relationship with continuity of care and outcomes explored. Research on the effectiveness of a housing first intervention will be described. Finding(s): Men with psychosis are spending more time in homeless settings. A small group of individuals are unable to achieve a basic level of functioning despite access to intensive outreach and prolonged residential rehabilitation. Conclusion(s): A new model of public mental health service provision is required to address the emerging population of homeless people with psychosis.
