AB  - Examined is the relationship between racial & ethnic minority status & the utilization of a range of services in a sample of 187 clients from a public case management program serving hospital recidivists, using data from case records that were open during the 12-month study period. An ordinary least squares regression model was developed to examine the individual & joint effects of a group of predictor variables on 5 outcome measures of utilization. The number of psychiatric emergency room visits & inpatient hospitalizations during clients' last 12 months in the program were used as outcomes also. Independent variables included age, sex, race, education, marital status, a measure of residential stability, alcohol consumption patterns, diagnosis, number of psychiatric emergency room visits & hospitalizations prior to program entry, months in the program prior to study, & months in the program during the study. Despite the severe & chronic mental disorder & low social standing of these clients, racial & ethnic minority status continued to be a significant factor in relation to several specific types of service utilization. In regard to minority utilization, types of services offered by mental health programs are as important as service use, such as total number of visits. Possible explanations for the findings, implications for service delivery policy, & directions for future research are discussed.
