AB  - Introduction: Though there is a common perception that the elderly "age out" of illicit drug use, substance abuse in older persons occurs and is considered an invisible epidemic by the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration. Case Description: An 80 year-old man with dementia, hypertension, and a past history of polysubstance abuse presented to geriatric consultation clinic for follow up of cognitive impairment. He described left hip pain on weight bearing, for which he reported taking naproxen with minimal effect. No other analgesics were prescribed. He also described intermittent shortness of breath, though he denied current dyspnea. His daughter had recently moved out of his apartment and he was now living alone. He stated he did not use alcohol or illicit substances. Cognitive testing was notable for impairment of executive function and short term memory. His blood pressure was 160/100, HR 76, pulse oximetry 100%. His lungs were clear, heart sounds normal. The patient was given an urgent care appointment for follow up of respiratory symptoms. On presentation to this clinic two days later, he complained of left hip pain, but denied other symptoms. His blood pressure was found to be 200/120, and an EKG performed revealed inferolateral T wave inversions. He was admitted to the hospital for hypertensive urgency. His creatinine was at baseline, cardiac enzymes were negative, and he remained asymptomatic. A urine toxicology screen was positive for cocaine and oxycodone. A hip film was significant for degenerative joint disease. He reported that he recently began to use crack cocaine again when friends offered it to him at home. Discussion(s): This case highlights that patients may continue to use cocaine as they age, and are at risk for relapse of illicit substance use. In a 2008 study of 117 veterans treated for crack cocaine use, 14.5% began their cocaine use after age 50 years. The most common comorbid medical illnesses were vascular disease and pain disorders. This patient's untreated hip pain and the psychosocial stress of living alone likely contributed to his relapse. The executive dysfunction associated with dementia may be a risk factor for illicit substance use unique to older adults. Given the prevalence of substance abuse in the aging baby boomer cohort, the number of older persons abusing illicit substances is expected to rise.
