AB  - (ProQuest: ... denotes non-US-ASCII text omitted.) Jonathan D. Ablard's book is a welcome addition to the burgeoning literature on medicine, science and social control in Argentina. The treatment of those with supposed mental health problems was frequently abominable, not necessarily because the intended policies were so bad given the state of existing knowledge - though clearly they were frequently far from optimal - but because of the state's failure to provide even minimal services. By discussing the state's mental health policies, the author has raised important questions about the Argentine state's ability to impose social control.
