AB  - Objective: To test the efficacy of an automated telephone intervention (“Care Call”) for people with spinal cord dysfunction (SCD) on pressure ulcers, depression, and appropriate health care use. Design: Randomized controlled trial. Setting: Participants' homes. Participants: Persons with spinal cord injury (n‐106) or multiple sclerosis (n‐36) who use a wheelchair at least six hours per day (N‐142) were randomly assigned to the Care Call intervention (treatment) or standard care (control) groups. Interventions: The control group received standard care and the intervention group received weekly calls from the Care Call system. Care Call is designed to: (1) screen for pressure ulcers and depressive symptoms; (2) educate about prevention of depression and pressure ulcers and appropriate use of heath care services; and (3) alert a Nurse Telerehabilitation Coordinator (NTC) as appropriate for direct follow up. Main Outcome Measures: Pressure Ulcer Scale for Healing Tool (v. 3.0) (0, 6 months); Patient Health Questionnaire (PHQ‐9) (0, 6 months); Cornell Services Index (0, 2, 4, & 6 months). Results: Intervention participants had less severe depression (P‐.0006) and fewer pressure ulcers (P‐.0006) at 6 months than those in the control group. Of note, the magnitude of improvement in depressive symptoms was directly proportional to baseline PHQ‐9 score. Also, the effect on pressure ulcers was only seen among women. There was no impact on health care use, though participants in the intervention group reported that health care was more available to them post‐intervention (P‐.0423). Conclusions: The Care Call results provide promising evidence that a fully automated approach to ongoing chronic disease care can meaningfully impact both the prevalence and severity of secondary conditions among adults with SCD. A qualitative inquiry and redesign of the Care Call intervention is warranted to better target males with SCD, followed by a larger, national trial to expand upon these preliminary findings.
