AB  - Aim: To explore the efficacy of synthetical family treatment for depressive patients. Method(s): The study was conducted in 76 depressive patients hospitalized in the Eighth Hospital of Zhengzhou between November 2002 and November 2003, in accordance with the Chinese Classification of Mental Disorders Version 3 (CCMD-3). All the subjects with no severe somatic disease lived in Zhenqzhou with direct relatives. All 76 volunteers were randomized into study group and control group. Study group: 14 males and 25 females, aged a mean of (36+/-6) years, the age of first onset (28+/-7) years, the course of disease (5.6+/-5.4) years, the times of hospitalization (2.1+/- 2.8) years, the years of education (11.2+/-2.4) years; Control group: 13 males and 24 females, aged a mean of (35+/-6) years, the age of first onset (28+/-7) years, the course of disease (5.9+/-5.8) years, the times of hospitalization (2.2+/-2.6) years, the years of education (10.8+/-5.8) years. There was no significant difference in the variables above between the two groups. The relatives of study group were interviewed at hospitalization to set up the primary doctor-patients relationship, which did not affect the hospitalized treatment. After discharge, study group received family treatment, whereas control group conducted common clinic service. Family treatment was based on the psychotherapy combining psychological behavior and features in Chinese family and developed by overseas family system pattern, psychagogy pattern and cognitive behavior pattern. Family treatment included positive intervention and maintenance therapy. The former one was lasted for 1.5-2.0 years and consisted of drug treatment, psychagogy and family intervention. Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAMD), Clinical Global Impression subscale for severity of illness (CGI-SI) and Social Disability Screening Schedule (SDSS) were used to assess the patients before and after the hospitalization, as well as after the six months follow-ups, including the clinical symptom, therapeutic effect and social function. Social disability in half a year was determined by the scores of SDSS (0% best, 100% worst), while family burden was determined with direct relatives in 6 aspects on the effect of family life (0: no influence, 1: mild, 2: moderate; 3: severe). Result(s): All 76 patients were involved in the result analysis. 1 There was no significant difference between the two groups in HAMD and CGI-SI scores before and after the hospitalization, but was significant difference after 24-month family intervention. The therapeutic effect was obviously better in study group than in control group [HAMD: (7.08+/-4.21), (12.26+/-0.85) point, P < 0.01; CGI-SI: (1.48+/-0.15), (2.63+/-0.12) point, P < 0.05]. 2 During follow-up, the impairment of social function in study group was lightened compared to control group [re-hospital day: (0.97+/-15.41), (7.61+/-26.14) days; full-time working: (86.7+/-048.10)%, (68.4+/-32.10)%; social function disability: (12.10+/-18.10)%, (38.10+/-15.20)%; P< 0.01]. The economic pressure and emotion were less affected in study group compared with control group, and the relative relationship was better than that in control group [(0.78+/-0.61), (1.51+/-0.83) points; (1.76+/-0.52), (2.15+/-0.68) points; (0.43+/- 0.69), (0.96+/-0.78), P < 0.05]. Conclusion(s): The synthetical family treatment is effective for depression.
