AB  - This study examined treatment accessibility and availability on Moloka'i by answering the following questions (1) what are the types, scope, and availability and accessibility of existing services on Moloka'i; and (2) how do the residents of Moloka'i perceive the adequacy of these services and the need for additional or expanded services? Quantitative and qualitative methods involving questionnaires, interviews and focus groups that were designed to be culturally appropriate to Moloka'i residents were employed to comprehensively document and provide evidence of the substance abuse and mental health treatment needs and preferences of this community to provide a foundation for assisting with substance abuse and mental health treatment planning. Qualitative data derived from interviews and focus groups with community leaders, service agency providers, kupuna, and community members centered around themes of increased substance abuse and mental health problems on the island and the negative effects of those increases, accessing services from informal and formal networks, accessibility and availability difficulties in obtaining care for problems, ideas for ideal services, and transformation suggestions. Quantitative data derived from questionnaires collected from 124 Moloka'i residents from Kaunakakai, Maunaloa, Mana'e and Ho'olehua suggested similar perceptions such as an increase in drug and alcohol use and mental health problems over the past years, a blend of preferences for treatment resources, many accessibility and adequacy problems with services, preferences for services located on island, and willingness to access enhanced and more available services. Five service providers detailed their services such as scope, clients serviced, and waiting list provisions. The project culminated in several recommendations to be considered by policymakers on the island. It is suggested that all recommendations be reviewed and approved by community members. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; email: disspub@umi.com
