AB  - Evaluated the efficacy and side effects of naltrexone in the treatment of alcohol dependence. 45 male outpatients with alcohol dependence (aged 34–60 yrs) (yrs of drinking: 10–32 yrs) (no. of hospitalized treatments: 1–6 times) (having met the criteria of the 2nd revision of the Chinese Classification of Mental Disease and the DSM-IV) were randomly assigned to have naltrexone (23 Ss, naltrexone 30 mg/day) or placebo (22 Ss) for 12 wks. In each wk, the patients were asked to visit the clinic under a family member's accompaniment. The craving was measured with the Volpicell method and the side effect was examined with the Treatment Emergent Symptom Scale before and after the treatment. A t test, log-rank test, and the Cox multiple regression analysis were used. The results show that compared with placebo treatment, the naltrexone treatment group had significantly low alcohol craving in 12 wks and higher cumulative rates of abstinence at the 4th, 8th, and 12th wks (91.3%, 77.9%, and 73.3%); and that naltrexone had side effects such as mild nausea, loss of appetite, and dizziness similar to the placebo. The study proves that naltrexone is effective and safe in the treatment of alcohol dependence. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
