AB  - The historical relationship between Black alcoholics and the fellowship of Alcoholics Anonymous is reviewed. A detailed analysis of the growth and development of local AA groups in a Black urban community from 1947 to 1984 and the strength and effectiveness of AA as a treatment resource in the Black community. Survey data indicate that the AA program of recovery will work effectively for Black alcoholics regardless of socio-economic status or class. Finally, a hypothesis of cultural affinity is offered as a probable explantation of the cross-cultural success of the AA fellowship with Black alcoholics.
