AB  - Examines some assumptions about the organically impaired elderly by observing an effective summer day program, studying 1 82-yr-old female participant, and relating a theoretical perspective. 26 elderly participants met with therapists in a group twice a week. The program was structured to relate to the life experiences of group members and consisted of a balance of talking, structured activities, and spontaneous programming. The 82-yr-old S, who was diagnosed as having Korsakov's syndrome, improved in self-confidence and lucidity enough to be able to function in a health related facility. This model is based on the helping principles applied in a life model and interactionally oriented group and milieu approaches. A sense of self is restored through relationships with others. The provision of a positive experience that recognizes and bolsters strength and relatedness makes it more attractive to live fully. (31 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
