AB  - The influence of self-selection effects (SSE) must be assessed in order to accurately interpret outcome data in longitudinal evaluations of mutual help organizations. One approach to adjusting for or SSE is to control for covariates that predict outcome using statistical procedures such as analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) and hierarchical regression. This approach has considerable power, but is less useful when an evaluator is interested in modeling the process of entry into a program and incorporating information on the factors affecting self-selection into estimation of program effects. Two-stage sample selection models are designed to address such situations. These models rely on regression procedures in which program participation is modeled in an initial equation, which yields a sample selection correction factor. The correction factor is included with participation in a second equation that predicts outcome. This 2-stage procedure allows the evaluator to interpret the observed effects of a professional service or a self-help group in the context of the magnitude and direction of selection effects. In this article, the use of covariate control and sample selection models in a longitudinal study of the effects of participation of in Alcoholics Anonymous on drinking behavior in 218 Ss are compared. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
