AB  - Presents two-stage sample selection models that allow evaluators of mutual help organizations to directly model the participant entry process & incorporate information on factors affecting self-selection into longitudinal evaluation. These models rely on regression procedures in which participation is modeled as an initial equation that yields a sample selection correction factor, which is included with participation in a second equation that predicts outcome. This strategy is compared & contrasted with the covariate control approach in a longitudinal study of the effects of Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) on the drinking behavior of 218 US participants. The sample selection approach supports the idea that there is a nonsignificant selection effect in predicting AA success & is concluded to be useful. 3 Tables, 37 References. Adapted from the source document.
