AB  - Background: Substance abuse has been considered as a severe public health issue since last century. Most of all, opioids dependence is an emerging crisis in Taiwan in last several years because of its close relationship with surge incline in HIV infection rate among most intravenous drug users. A large scale methadone replacement program has been promoted everywhere in Taiwan for 6 consecutive years, and an apparent decline in HIV infection incidence rate emphasizes the success in initial control of people with opioids dependence after this intervention. However, it seems that methadone maintenance program can only achieve the goal of alleviating subjects' physical or psychological tolerance in some degree to avoid some high risk IV injection behavior or crime-related activities. It's still difficult for patient to deal with the craving or drug seeking behavior.Past publications showed that a common used mucolytics, N-Acetyl-Cysteine, which restores the basal glutamate level in the accumbens by introducing extracellular glutamate. The restoration of glutamate levels that results from this exchange produces a reduction of reinstatement of drug seeking behavior or craving behavior. Method(s): This is an open-label clinical trial of adult outpatients with a diagnosis of opioid dependence or opioid abuse based on the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fourth Edition, Text Revision (DSM-IV-TR). Eligible subjects will have laboratory and clinical assessments completed. We expect to observe the drug use behaviors in maintenance program by adding N-Acetyl-Cysteine (NAC) augmentation with methadone among people dependent on opioids are significantly reduced than methadone treatment along. Result(s): Our result implies that the maintenance rate of methadone replacement therapy is significantly better in the NAC intervention group compared to the placebo group. However, the reduction of methadone dosage between these two groups fails to reach the significant difference level. We can still observe the trend that the reduction degree is greater among the NAC intervention group. Conclusion(s): These preliminary results suggest that NAC is well tolerated in patients in methadone maintenance therapy and may reduce heroin-related craving behavior.
