AB  - The article discusses the review of Howard J. Clinebell Jr.'s book "Understanding and Counseling the Alcoholic," by Gene Jennings of the Blair County Mental Health Clinic, that was published in the January 1969 issue of "The Family Coordinator." There is no empirical evidence is cited to support the claim by Clinebell that Alcoholics Anonymous is the most valuable and the most effective referral source available. At the same time, although therapists of one school question the effectiveness of therapists of other schools, all agree about the effectiveness of Alcoholics Anonyonous — even though they may ascribe different reasons for this effectiveness. It is important for one to be aware that their helping activities based upon traditional psychodynamic concepts are not really achieving the purposes that they claim for the alcoholics. At at the same time many of them tend to deprecate peer self-help psychotherapy groups which are a peoples' movement effectively sending many people with much less theorizing and for much less time and money than conventional psychotherapy.
