AB  - Context: Opium addiction is a worldwide problem that individuals find difficult to overcome and that has high relapse rates. Practitioners have used hypnotic aversion therapy and hypnotic relaxation successfully to help control and ultimately end withdrawal symptoms. Furthermore. some studies have reported the positive effects of hypnotherapy for alcoholics after drinking cessation. Much of the evidence in the field, however, involves case reports and uncontrolled studies, and some investigators believe that hypnosis has only a limited effect on addiction cessation. ObJective: The study intended to determine the effect of hypnotherapy, in addition to consulting psychotherapy. on the relapse rate after detoxification fur individuals with opium addiction. Design: The research team conducted a randomized. controlled trial. The team assigned participants either to a hypnotherapy (n = 11) or a control (n = 11) group after their rapid detoxification. Setting: The research team conducted this study at the Behavioral Sciences Research Center ofIsfahan University ofMedical Sciences, Iran. Participants: The participants were 22 male opiate addicts. Intervention: Both the hypnotherapy and the control group attended identical psychotherapy sessions, and the hypno-therapy group attended five additional hypnotherapy sessions. The research team focused the hypnotic suggestions on deep relaxation, reduction of withdrawal symptoms, ego strengthening, and drug aversion and compared the relapse rate for the two groups after a period of 6 months. Outrome Measures: The research team used the Fisher's Exact Test to compare the 6-month relapse rates between the two groups. The team used the independent sample t-test to compare parametric data between the two groups. The research team considered a P-value of<.05 to be statistically significant. Results: The mean age of participants was 31.2 (±4.5) years. The relapse rate was 40% (4/11) in the hypnotherapy group compared to 73% (8/11) in the control group. P >.05. For the participantsin thehypnotherapygroup who reported the symptoms prior to hypnotherapy. the benefits included improvements in insomnia. 88% (7/8); restlessness, 80% (8/10); pain. 60% (3/5); and autonomic disturbances, 33% (2/6). Conclusions: The study'S results did not support a significant effect on relapse rate for adding hypnotherapy to consulting psychotherapy in the treatment of opium addiction. Clinically significant effects for hypnotherapy on relapse rate and withdraWal-symptoms relief. however. indicate further trials are warranted in this field.
