AB  - Objectives: Mounting evidence links PTSD symptoms to worsened school functioning. This study seeks to describe the relationship between PTSD symptoms, school factors, resilience of students, and their utilization of mental health services. These results have important implications for schools and mental health professionals providing consulting services to schools. Method(s): We examined secondary data of 623 ninth-grade students from a low-income, large public school district who had received a baseline survey prior to delivery of a classroom resilience-building curriculum. Students were asked about help seeking, perception of school safety, PTSD symptoms, and resilience. Survey results were linked to school attendance records. Descriptive statistics and multiple logistic regression models were used to examine factors associated with PTSD symptom severity. Result(s): Of those surveyed, approximately half of the students were male (54%), and the majority (87%) were Latino. A majority of students (81%) had not sought counseling in the past year. Of the 18 percent of students who were at high risk for PTSD, 69 percent had not sought mental health services in the past year. Sixty-one percent of students reported feeling safe or very safe at school. Resultsdemonstrated gender differences. Female students had significantly higher scores on empathy (P < 0.0001) and problem solving (P = 0.0012), whereas male students had significantly higher scores on selfawareness (P = 0.0004). A significantly higher percentage of females (25%) were at high risk for PTSD, relative to males (12%) (P < 0.0001). Higher PTSD symptomology in female students was associated with a higher probability of at-risk attendance (OR = 1.31, P = 0.0252). Higher PTSD symptomatology was associated with a lower probability of feeling safe at school (OR = 0.77, P < 0.0001), and this association was particularly strong among females. Conclusion(s): This study demonstrates a significant relationship between PTSD symptoms, at-risk attendance, and feeling safe at schools, particularly for females. Despite high rates of trauma exposure among students, most do not seek mental health care. Initiatives to improve school climate and youth resilience, such as whole classroom interventions, are needed to improve academic and mental health outcomes among highrisk youth.
