AB  - Purpose of the study: Increased reactivity of hypothalamic‐pituitary‐adrenocortical axis (HPA‐axis) has shown to be of high importance in depression [1]. There is quite robust evidence on the positive effect of Aerobic exercise (AE) on emotional and cognitive symptoms of depression [2, 3] but involvement of underlying biological systems such as HPA‐axis are still poorly understood. Hence we started this study to evaluate the effect of aerobic exercise on Cortisol awakening reaction (CAR) as a measure for HPA‐axis activity in depressed in‐patients. Methods: In‐patients with a diagnosis of depression and a score >15 in the 17 item Hamilton Depression Scale (HDRS‐17) were randomly assigned to either endurance exercise on indoor bicycles (17.5 kcal/kg/week) at 60‐75% of maximal heart‐rate or a stretching and coordination program 3 times per week for 6 consecutive weeks as add‐on therapy to multimodal antidepressant treatment including guidelines‐oriented pharmacotherapy. Patients were recruited on the depression wards of the Psychiatric University Hospital Basel and the Psychiatric Services Solothurn between October 2013 and February 2016. HDRS‐17 and CAR were measured before (baseline), at 2 weeks and at the end of the 6‐weeks intervention‐period (post). CAR was performed by measuring salivary cortisol directly after waking up and +10, +20 and +30 minutes later. Results: 17 female and 17 male patients with a mean age of 38.9 (±19.9) were recruited into the study (17 per group). Three had a diagnosis of bipolar depression, 15 suffered from recurrent and 16 from a first depressive episode. There were no significant group‐differences for demographic variables (Chi2, ANOVA). Mean HDRS‐17 was 21.5 (± 3.6) at baseline and decreased significantly to 8.56 (±6.66) with no significant group difference over the 6 weeks intervention period. CAR showed no group difference at baseline regarding measured cortisol levels as well as total area under the curve (AUCtotal) and basal AUC (AUCbasal). After 6 weeks salivary cortisol showed a markedly shallower increase in the intervention group with a statistical trend for significance at +30 minutes (p = 0.085, ANOVA). AUCtotal decreased significantly after 2 weeks in both groups (intervention: MD = 85.56, p = 0.009; Control: MD = 84.43, p = 0.027; paired t‐test) the difference towards baseline stayed significant after 6 weeks in the intervention group (MD = 87.67, p = 0.029) whereas in controls it increased again, nearing levels at baseline (MD = 26.76, p = 0.580). When basal cortisol‐secretion was evaluated (AUCbasal) the effect was even stronger with a significant time effect in the intervention group from baseline to post (MD = 113.82, p = 0.005), but not in controls (MD = 12.76, p = 0.780). Conclusions: AE as add‐on for depressed in‐patients showed to significantly reduce HPA‐axis activity over the course of the 6 weeks intervention period. Since HPA‐axis activity plays an important role in the neurobiology of depression AE might be a possible treatment‐option to facilitate HPA‐axis normalization especially in subjects with dysregulation of HPA‐axis activity. However, AE had no additional effect on emotional symptoms. Since patients receive a highly effective treatment regime this might be due to a ceiling effect.
