AB  - In early 2019 a veteran died by suicide on an inpatient mental health unit in the Veterans Health Administration (VHA) as a result of hanging. The veteran used the top of a corridor door as the anchor point. This incident was investigated by the Veterans Affairs (VA) Office of Inspector General (OIG). The OIG found that bedroom doors which led into the corridor were not recognized by the facility as a hazardous vulnerability for hanging. The VHA has 152 medical centers, of which 113 (74.3%) have acute inpatient mental health units accommodating 77,000 mental health admissions per year. An August 2019 survey identified that 80 (70.8%) VHA mental health inpatient units have over-the-door (OTD) alarms. The goal of this study was to analyze suicide attempts and suicide deaths on VHA mental health units to determine the impact of OTD alarms when in place. The authors conducted a retrospective chart review of the VHA National Center for Patient Safety (NCPS) patient safety database and the Joint Patient Safety Reporting (JPSR) system for suicide deaths and attempts on inpatient mental health units from January 2008 (when VHA began using OTD alarms) to June 2019. A total of 389 suicide attempt and suicide death events were analyzed. These included 179 (46.0%) reports of hanging, of which there were 6 deaths; 92 (23.7%) reports of strangulation, and 74 (19.0%) reports of cutting. The seventh death occurred by asphyxiation. This study identified an association between OTD alarms and prevention of suicide attempt and death events by hanging on mental health units. Overall VHA suicide death rates on mental health units are low and thus challenge future formal prospective study. Moreover, VHA has accepted the cost of OTD alarms and elected to mandate installation of this technology on all inpatient mental health units to mitigate the environmental hazard of using doors as anchor points for suicide by hanging, effective January 2020. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
