AB  - Evaluated 58 juvenile delinquents (aged 12–18 yrs) referred for psychiatric evaluation by the juvenile court in Tel-Aviv. Ss were divided into 3 groups: 18 Ss evaluated on an ambulatory basis, 24 Ss requiring additional inpatient observations following the preliminary ambulatory evaluations, and 16 Ss requiring further inpatient treatment. Comparison of the Ss evaluated on an ambulatory basis and the 2 groups requiring further inpatient interventions revealed that the latter demonstrated decreased intelligence and greater dysfunction of the family. Ss requiring inpatient evaluations also demonstrated increased rates of suicidal behavior and neuropsychological dysfunction. The evaluating psychiatrists tended to hospitalize those delinquents who demonstrated increased overall vulnerability (i.e., emotional, cognitive, and familial). (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
