AB  - The new Halfway-House for psychiatric patients in transition from or to the mental hosp, offered an opportunity to test certain basic assumptions about `cooperative group therapy' as derived from the author's investigations in Sociol of Cooperation. Internal difficulties in a number of cooperative groups studied indicated the need for a therapeutically oriented examinatior instead of mere factfinding. This necessitated the employment of precise diagnostic tools, such as the sociometric test as well as the cooperative potential & resoluteness tests developed for this purpose by the author. The main assumption on which the exploration of the Halfway House was based implied that the tools effective in the therapy of groups would be useful, in a modified form, in a group psychotherapy in which cooperation became a therapeutic factor. The Home accommodates 20-25 individuals served by a multi-discipline staff of 7. The duration of residence being limited to 3 months called for a directive rather than permissive approach. Attendance at the group sessions was open & voluntary. 3 sets of sociol'al techniques proved effective: (1) techniques designed to enhance the kind of mutual understanding that helps create a group out of a mere aggregate of people, such as the Group Biographical Interview or the related topical reminiscence; (2) techniques of group therapy proper, including psychodrama in its simplified version of 'proto-psychodrama'; (3) & a soc org along cooperative lines, out of which developed a cooperative practice in response to felt needs, such as that for mutual financial help through a credit union. The Home could not meet all the requirements for a `therapeutic community' since residents were without an opportunity for real, though sheltered work; nonetheless, the cooperative atmosphere created proved to be of definite therapeutic value. Future res should yield a more exact measure of the effectiveness of the sociol'al approach used & systematically applied at the Home to date. AA.
