AB  - INTERVENTION: Intervention 1: Intervention for control group (occupational therapy): The control group will receive psychosocial services including group therapy (group discussion), leisure activities, arts and structured activities. Like the intervention group, the patients in the control group will attend occupational therapy sessions three times per week at outpatient centers and five times per week at inpatient centers, for 90 to 120 minutes per session. Intervention 2: The treatment for the intervention group is the independent and social living skills package. These skills include medication and symptom management, leisure management and basic conversational skills. Also family members of the intervention group will participate, as a generalization agent, in separate sessions (four sessions) where they will be provided with information concerning learned skills as well as problem solving and home assignment. Moreover, the family will be contacted by phone (once a month) during the follow‐up period (transition of skills to the home). Interventions will be conducted three times per week at outpatient centers and five times per week at inpatient centers, for 90 to 120 minutes per session. Intervention for control group (occupational therapy): The control group will receive psychosocial services including group therapy (group discussion), leisure activities, arts and structured activities. Like the intervention group, the patients in the control group will attend occupational therapy sessions three times per week at outpatient centers and five times per week at inpatient centers, for 90 to 120 minutes per session. Rehabilitation The treatment for the intervention group is the independent and social living skills package. These skills include medication and symptom management, leisure management and basic conversational skills. Also family members of the intervention group will participate, as a generalization agent, in separate sessions (four sessions) where they will be provided with information concerning learned skills as well as problem solving and home assignment. Moreover, the family will be contacted by phone (once a month) during the follow‐up period (transition of skills to the home). Interventions will be conducted three times per week at outpatient centers and five times per week at inpatient centers, for 90 to 120 minutes per session. CONDITION: f20‐f21‐f2 Schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders Schizophrenia. ; Schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders PRIMARY OUTCOME: Quality of life. Timepoint: Before intervention, upon completion of intervention, and 6 months after completion of intervention. Method of measurement: WHO‐Quality of Life Questionnaire. Severity of Psychopathology. Timepoint: Before intervention, upon completion of intervention, and 6 months after completion of intervention. Method of measurement: Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS). SECONDARY OUTCOME: Drug Compliance. Timepoint: Before Intervention, upon completion of intervention, and 6 months after completion of intervention. Method of measurement: Drug Compliance Questionnaire. Family Burden. Timepoint: Before intervention, upon completion of intervention, and 6 months after completion of intervention. Method of measurement: Experience of Caregiving Inventory. Family Quality of Life. Timepoint: before intervention,upon completion of intervention, and 6 months after completion of intervention. Method of measurement: WHO‐Quality of Life. Global Function. Timepoint: before intervention, upon completion of intervention, and 6 months after completion of intervention. Method of measurement: Global Assessment of Functioning. Patient Satisfaction. Timepoint: Before intervention, upon completion of intervention, and 6 months after completion of intervention. Method of measurement: Client Satisfaction Questionnaire. INCLUSION CRITERIA: Inclusion Criteria 1 Informed willingness and consent of the patient for participation in the study. 2 Being Diagnosed with schizophrenia according to Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 4th Edition, Text Revision (DSM‐IV‐TR) criteria; however, after the active phase of illness. 3 Scoring between 40 and 70 on Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF). 4 Having been hospitalized at least for one time. 5 Ranging between 18 to 55 years old. 6 Living in the vicinity of the therapy center. 7 Being literate. 8 Living with family. Exclusion Criteria 1 Any known current drug dependence or abuse and mental retardation. 2 Seizure disorder requiring medication. 3 Serious psychotic disorders other than schizophrenia. 4 Clinically significant physical or neurological disorder. 5 Risk of harm
