AB  - Objective: Repair of abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAAs) has shifted from open surgical repair (OSR) to endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR) in the United States with improvements in endograft design. We sought to explore a contemporary database that includes analysis of newer generation devices to understand the factors influencing mortality after AAA repair in the EVAR-dominated era. Method(s): We investigated nonruptured AAA repair in a 20% sample of Medicare patients from 2006 to 2015. A Charlson Comorbidity Index was calculated from outpatient and inpatient International Classification of Diseases, Ninth Revision codes. We used Kaplan-Meier survival analysis and Cox regression to calculate hazard ratios (HRs) for mortality. We used nearest neighbor propensity matching using all available input covariates including age, ethnicity, and comorbidities to evaluate the treatment effects of OSR vs EVAR. Result(s): A total of 23,554 patients underwent EVAR and 3412 patients underwent OSR (12.7%) during the study period. Patients were older in the EVAR group (76.6 +/- 0.1 years) compared with the OSR group (74.8 +/- 0.1 years; P <.001). A higher percentage of women were treated with OSR (33.9%) compared with EVAR (20.3%; P <.001). The median follow-up was 4.1 years (interquartile range, 2.4-6.3 years). The unadjusted 1-year mortality was higher for OSR (14%) compared with EVAR (10%; P <.01), whereas the unadjusted mortality at 8 years was higher for EVAR (63%) compared with OSR (53%; P <.01). Treatment with EVAR was not associated with higher 8-year mortality when adjusted for age, sex, and comorbidities (P =.13; Fig). Factors affecting mortality included age (1.05; 95% confidence interval [CI], 1.05-1.05) and female sex (HR, 1.06; 95% CI, 1.02-1.10); the comorbidities with the greatest hazard for mortality were dementia (HR, 1.62; 95% CI, 1.51-1.75) and congestive heart failure (HR, 1.53; 95% CI, 1.47-1.59; Table). After propensity matching, 1159 patients were identified; EVAR was found to be associated with slightly lower mortality (coefficient, -0.032; 95% CI, -0.013 to -0.051; P =.006). Conclusion(s): Our findings demonstrate that contemporary EVAR, after wider adoption in the United States, has not been associated with higher rates of long-term mortality when adjusted for patient demographics and comorbidities. After propensity matching, EVAR actually is associated with a slightly lower risk of overall mortality. This study highlights the complex decision-making process that physicians face, given variations in patient demographics and comorbidities. Rather than an "all-endo" or "all-open" approach, each patient should receive individualized counseling and consideration during treatment planning. [Formula presented] [Formula presented]Copyright © 2020
