AB  - Presents data on the characteristics and service needs of 1,050 mentally retarded residents of 118 group homes, noting that variations in service needs reflected by residents' characteristics and the failure of community service systems to anticipate such variations may affect program–resident compatibility. A needs-assessment device was used to summarize Ss' demographic characteristics, functional ability, program and service needs, and adaptive functioning on an individual basis. The grouping of the 118 homes according to high or low (1) mean age of residents, (2) IQ level, and (3) number of diagnosed disabilities resulted in 8 group home types. Resident mean ages for these 8 home types varied from 12–52 yrs, and mean intellectual levels ranged from mild to profound retardation. Different types of group homes were characterized by varying incidences of sensorimotor deficits and differing levels of community-living skills among residents. The provision of therapeutic and community generic services also varied with type of residence. Findings illustrate the diversity of the populations served in group homes and the need to individualize intervention and consultation services on the basis of the abilities and needs of the residents of each program. (45 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
