AB  - In recent years, the plight of children exposed to domestic violence has received increasing attention, but awareness of the problem has not always translated into public action. This forward-thinking volume brings together leading mental health, legal, education, and human service professionals from the United States and Canada to demonstrate how domestic violence and its effects on everyone involved can best be mitigated through community collaboration. Focusing on multiple forms of violence in the home, including child abuse and partner abuse, this important book will inform the daily work and training of the wide range of professionals who serve children and families at risk. The book first provides an overview of the problem of child witnesses of domestic violence and considers the most promising assessment approaches. Specific interventions with victims and offenders are then described, beginning with individual and group interventions and moving on to broader efforts involving schools, courts, the media, and law enforcement. The importance of teaching healthy relationships (the fourth 'R') in school curricula and programs at elementary and high school levels is emphasized as a vital step in preventing domestic violence in the next generation. The critical role of the justice system in responding to children exposed to domestic violence is explored in the context of child custody disputes, and the importance of community collaboration in assisting child victims as well as adult victims and perpetrators of domestic violence is highlighted. Also addressed are the complexity of working with families from diverse cultural backgrounds and the often-ignored subject of how to enhance the roles of fathers in their children's lives. An illuminating work offering groundbreaking perspectives on a major social problem, this volume will be welcomed by practitioners and policymakers--including social workers, psychologists, child psychiatrists, child welfare workers, domestic violence advocates, and family law and criminal justice professionals--who encounter victims and perpetrators of domestic violence. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
