AB  - Research Objectives: To identify physical therapists and occupational therapists perception of their needs, barriers and facilitators for the development and implementation of specific workstations and activities integrated in a circuit therapy as an adjunct to usual therapy with the aim to reach the clinical recommendations for post-stroke rehabilitation intensity. Design(s): Qualitative design study. Setting(s): Inpatient rehabilitation stroke setting. Participant(s): Focus groups (n=2) with physical therapists (n=5) and occupational therapists (n=8). Intervention(s): Not applicable. Main Outcome Measure(s): Deductive content analysis based on the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) was used to identify needs, facilitators and barriers to implementing. Result(s): Physical therapists and occupational therapists are favorable to the implementation of workstations as both groups raised the need of increasing the duration of different interventions in order the reach the clinical recommendations for post-stroke rehabilitation intensity. Facilitators to the implementation included the clinicians' perceived need to implement Circuit Therapy (CT) as an adjunct therapy especially those in isolation; design of the CT (e.g. group activities, attractive activities to patients; clear instructions); the adaptability of the activities included in the CT (e.g. should include various levels of difficulty to be appropriate for patients with different levels of disability, easy to use) and the provision of adequate resources (e.g. training for clinicians staff for the supervision of patients). Clinicians identified barriers related to the different patients' levels of disability (e.g. cognitive disorders, physical functioning, fatigue) that can impede participation in CT; concerns about patients' safety when using CT without supervision and the lack of available resources (e.g. time, staff, equipment). Conclusion(s): This study allowed the identification of needs, barriers and facilitators of clinicians for the development and implementation of CT. These findings will inform the development of implementation interventions tailored to the local context with the aim to overcome these barriers. Author(s) Disclosures: The authors declare that they have no competing interest. Keywords: Implementation, Circuit Therapy, Clinicians' Perception, Barriers, EnablersCopyright © 2020
