AB  - Objectives/Introduction: The aim of the study was to compare the implementation and effectiveness of group and self‐help based cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT‐I) and sleep hygiene guidance in a randomized controlled design among shift workers with insomnia. In addition, we detected participants for features of shift work disorder (SWD) and analysed if they affected the results. Methods: A total of 59 shift workers with insomnia disorder were recruited by six occupational health (OH) centers and participated in: a) group‐based CBT‐I, b) computerized self‐help CBT‐I, or c) sleep hygiene guidance. The interventions were delivered by trained OH nurses and psychologists. The outcomes were evaluated before and after the interventions and at 6‐, 12‐and 24‐month follow‐ups using questionnaires, sleep diary and actigraphy. Results: Perceived severity of insomnia (p < 0.001), sleep‐related dysfunctional beliefs (p < 0.001), burnout symptoms (p < 0.001), self‐reported restedness after a sleep period and recovery after a work shift (both p = 0.006), as well as actigraphy‐based total sleep time (TST) (p = 0.037) improved significantly after all three interventions and these improvements were mainly maintained at 12‐and 24‐month follow‐ups. Mood symptoms decreased after group‐based CBT‐I up to 12‐month follow‐up (p = 0.031). Half of the participants had features of SWD. Participants without SWD features suffered significantly more common from mental disorders, had more common sleep‐promoting medication (SPM), and reported having more pronounced insomnia severity, dysfunctional beliefs and mood symptoms than participants with SWD features. Participants without SWD features also showed greater post‐intervention improvements in dysfunctional beliefs, actigraphy‐based TST, and SPM usage than those with SWD features. Conclusions: Our results indicate that all three interventions can be effectively implemented in OH centers. The benefits of both CBT‐I interventions and sleep hygiene guidance are rather similar and long‐standing among shift workers. The main added value of group‐based CBT‐I seems to be the alleviation of mood symptoms. Having SWD features may reduce the benefits of the interventions.
