AB  - Purpose: Disadvantaged young people have, or are at risk of having, poorer health than their peers. Services can play an important role in promoting health equity for adolescents and young adults. NSW CAAH consulted with a range of services to determine their views about the challenges involved in supporting the health of young people and to seek their advice on the content for a proposed resource kit. The kit is being designed to support health and related practitioners to work well with young people. Method(s): Ten focus groups were conducted with service providers between March and June 2012. A range of services caring for young people was sampled ensuring representation from: * Youth-specific health services employed within health, including community- and hospital-based * Non-youth specific health services whose target group includes young adults * Youth specific services that do not have a specific health focus * NGOs * Services outside the Sydney area * Support services * Clinical services. Result(s): Service providers identified that the challenges of working collaboratively with other services were greater than the specific clinical challenges posed by the young people themselves. Common concerns included: successfully referring young people to other service providers; negotiating service pathways; experiencing a lack of partnership; feeling the pressure on services to fill service gaps; and creating sustainable inter-service collaboration rather than ad hoc systems. For practitioners, collaboration means: Sharing information * Attending local network people, forums, do lots of promotion . . . we'll talk about our service, our organisation as a whole (youth health manager)Making the links and finding the pathways * It's making sure that we as service providers have those links, those pathways, the connectiveness (youth health worker)Planning, knowing when to refer * Like if you've only got one mental health worker in town and you know that if you refer that young person now, it may well fail . . . you're actually better off to [hold off]. Because everybody's time is limited and you have to make the most of your referrals (youth worker) Exercising judgment * What you might share with one service isn't appropriate to share with another (youth health worker) Negotiating between service systems * I guess maintaining partnerships is really hard when you've got a big case load of complex clients, it isn't always easy to engage . . . you're trying to work with the young people and you're trying to work with the systems of care . . . actually making sure that there's a really clear understanding of each part of the system's model of care and philosophy of care . . . people are misunderstanding mental health. (youth mental health worker). Conclusion(s): In order to work effectively towards health equity for young people, services need to work well together. Inter-service collaboration involves more than information-sharing, it requires complex skills and knowledge, including negotiating between different service delivery philosophies. Services need policies and resources that acknowledge and develop these important skills. The Youth Health Resource Kit being developed by NSW CAAH will provide one such resource.
