AB  - Describes an experimental study that examined the use of telephone group counseling for the visually impaired. 24 35–84 yr old blind veterans were divided into experimental or control groups and administered the Wakefield Self-Assessment Depression Inventory, UCLA Loneliness Scale, and Personal Assessment of Role Skills. The experimental group differed significantly from controls after treatment on measures of social involvement but not on measures of depression or agitation. Ss' achievement of self-selected goals were related to the expectations of their support network. Findings suggest that group telephone counseling can effect positive changes in the physical and social behavior of visually impaired people. (10 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
