AB  - Carers are known to have valuable information regarding patient functioning. It has been repeatedly cited that failure of communication between mental health services and the carers of patients is related to critical incidents and failures in patient care. Despite this, there are no structured interventions for carers to participate in patient care, let alone assist with measuring patient progress. This study builds upon and expands on a previous pilot study of a similar measure that was developed for a specific oldage population to create one suitable for general adult use. Development of the Carer Appraisal Scale was based on a grounded theory approach, beginning with semistructured interviews with staff members working in the mental health service of a major tertiary referral centre, with intent on refinement by focus groups, carer feedback and eventual reduction in total number of items via factor analysis to create an accessible and brief measure suitable for regular clinical use. It is proposed that this measure provides additional value for services in engaging the carers of patients in a clinically meaningful way that will greatly impact on patient care. IMPACT OF CLIMATE CHANGE ON MENTAL HEALTH J Topp Doctors for the Environment, Australia Objective: It is a given that our world faces potentially catastrophic climate change and we have limited capacity to adapt to rapid or extreme climatic changes. Change in the climate due to greenhouse gas emissions is accelerating, bringing with it more frequent and severe extreme weather events and consequences such as bushfires; in early 2019, we experienced severe bush fires in Southern Tasmania - a sobering reminder of our predicament and vulnerability. As a result, we can expect significant adverse impacts on health. This includes mental health, a major and growing global concern. The aim of this paper is to elaborate on this by drawing on the extant literature with reference to the impact on the mental health of young people. Conclusion(s): Climate change will affect mental health directly through increasing exposure to trauma and indirectly through harming physical health and damaging the physical environment on which economic opportunity depends. Disadvantaged people and communities, especially in rural and remote Australia, will be hardest hit. Young people are becoming increasingly distressed. Furthermore, our awareness of the looming threats and current risks and impacts of climate change presents challenges to our emotional and social well-being. Many are increasingly concerned by the very evident changes in our climate as well as the degradation of our environment. As doctors we have a duty to care for human health and to alleviate suffering; thus, to maintain an awareness of these changes and to address them.
