AB  - Using event history methodology, this study measured the relapse to opioid use of 590 clients from 21 methadone clinics. Clinics were grouped into Group ERLT (early relapse and long tenure in treatment), Group LRLT (late relapse and long tenure), and Group MIX (remaining clinics with mixed relapse times and treatment tenures). The analysis of client characteristics at entry found that mental health problems were significantly associated with lower relapse rates in the total sample, while employment problems were associated with higher relapse rates in Group LRLT. Lower dosages resulted in higher relapse rates for Group LRLT. The article describes other noteworthy predictors such as clinic philosophy, clinic size, take-home privileges, and services. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
