AB  - Background: The prevalence of dementia is expected to increase in the entire world. Due to insufficient accessibility to suitable short‐ and longterm care in nursing homes, there is a need to optimise services for home dwelling patients and their family carers. The randomized controlled study “A psychosocial intervention programme for persons with dementia and their family carers” aimed to develop such programmes in the municipalities. It included training health care personnel to enable them to recruit participants and carry out the intervention, and to investigate its effect. Aim: First, to investigate to what extent the intervention contributed to increased knowledge about families with dementia among the health care professionals; second, to explore possible changes to services for these families in the participating municipalities. Method: Content analysis, a qualitative method, with interviews of 19 informants representing three focus groups and six individual interviews, during 2012. The informants, were aged between 34 and 61 years, all women except for one, and came from 13 urban and rural municipalities. Findings: The informants' experiences are presented in three thematic categories relating to the main themes covered in the interviews with one or three subcategories presenting different aspects of the themes. The main themes were; (1) Challenges, with subcategories preparing for the intervention, recruiting participants and conducting the programme; (2) New knowledge, with subcategories about persons with dementia, about the situation of family carers, and about the health care workers themselves; (3) Service development, with the subcategory change of practices. Conclusion: The study revealed a lack of knowledge concerning the capacity of the persons with dementia to express their needs and interest in knowledge about their condition. Furthermore, it seems beneficial to offer structured individual as well as group based interventions in the early stages of the disorder and throughout the course of the dementia.
