AB  - Insufficient attention has been given to prenatal psychosocial health among low-income women of diverse ethnicities. Guided by a transactional model of stress and coping (Lazarus & Folkman, 1984), this dissertation examines (a) the prevalence of psychosocial stressors, prenatal depression, general anxiety, and pregnancy-related anxiety; (b) expectations regarding the role of pregnant women themselves and supportive others in managing these stressors; and (c) psychosocial service needs in a sample of 60 African American and 60 immigrant Latina prenatal patients recruited from a primary health care center in urban St. Louis, Missouri. Structured in-person interviews and focus groups were conducted. Results indicate that the women experience a very high level of stress during pregnancy, including perceived discrimination, daily hassles, stressful life events, ethnic-specific stress, and interpersonal violence. High levels of prenatal depression were found among African Americans but not among immigrant Latinas. Levels of general anxiety and pregnancy-specific anxiety were not elevated in either group, with the exception of the fear of giving birth subscale, which was high in both groups. Women perceived prenatal emotional health as very important for their babies' well-being and felt responsible for minimizing negative emotions during pregnancy, an expectation that created stress. Women with partners expected support first from partners, followed by family support. African American women also relied heavily on strong female friendships for support. The most commonly endorsed need was for more support from others, followed by help dealing with stress, help with feeling down or blue, and help because of physical limitations. Participants expressed a desire for more understanding from others regarding the experience of pregnancy. They were interested in attending groups that offer information about perinatal issues and opportunities to interact with other pregnant women. They also suggested increasing the engagement of partners and family members. This study provides useful insight into the experiences and psychosocial needs of low-income African American and immigrant Latina women during pregnancy. Additional research on preparatory coping, pregnancy-specific anxiety, and interventions to reduce stress is warranted. Practitioners can support women by providing opportunities for interaction, addressing interpersonal violence, and engaging partners to enhance support of women during pregnancy. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; e-mail: disspub@umi.com
