AB  - This paper reviews the literature on the efficacy of group treatments for personality disorders. The formal change theory literature on group treatments of personality disorders is sparse, with the exception of dialectical behavior therapy for borderline personality disorder, which has been the subject of several randomized trials showing positive outcomes. In addition, group programs based around CBT or 12-step principles appear to be efficacious for patients with antisocial personality disorder who are seeking substance use treatment. Finally, group exposure alone brings about symptom reduction for patients with avoidant personality disorder, with some additional benefit offered by providing specific, skills-based help developing intimate relationships. Group-based day treatment programs for patients with mixed personality disorders are commonly used, but very few trials have evaluated the role of formal change theory in these programs; more could be done. Research on the roles of structural factors, patient factors, and group factors in group treatment of personality disorder are also briefly reviewed.
