AB  - Background: The opioid epidemic is felt in our communities in Western Massachusetts, including approximately 22% of people entering Hampden County Correctional Center (Ludlow, MA) whom report non-medical use of opioids. The incarcerated population is especially vulnerable to opioid relapse and overdose death following release [1]. Several studies have shown that when methadone [2], buprenorphine [3], and extended release naltrexone [4] are accessible during and after incarceration, treatment retention and rates of relapse in the community improve. Injectable extended-release naltrexone (XR-NTX, Vivitrol) has been shown to prevent opioid agonist reinforcing effects and thus relapse [5]. This pilot program was designed to promote addiction recovery through collaboration of jail and community-based centers providing counseling and XR-NTX pre-release and continued in the community. During this period, referral for community, but not pre-release, initiation of treatment with buprenorphine or methadone was available. Method(s): Prospective CQI cohort with historical comparison group with six-month outcomes. Setting(s): Hampden County Correctional Center, Baystate Brightwood Health Center, Clean Slate Addiction Treatment Centers Participants: From April 2013 to December 2014, 67 opioid-dependent inmates interested in medication-assisted treatment (MAT) with XR-NTX were referred to the program and underwent further education, health screening prior to referral or initiation of treatment and referral. Measures: Utility of the program was assessed by determining MAT retention rates at 4, 8, and 24 weeks. Overdose deaths were determined by continued contact with patients, their contacts, and community health providers. Finding(s): 47 (average 35 years-old, 10% female) were commenced on XR-NTX approximately 7 days prior to release. 20 (average 35 years-old; 5% female) were referred to commence XR-NTX at outpatient addiction treatment centers. Rate of retention at week 4, 8, and 24 was higher in group with treatment initiation prior to release as compared to those started in community: week 4: 55% (24 NTX + 2 other MAT of 47) versus 25% (4 NTX+1 other MAT of 20) (p = 0.03); week 8: 36% (13+4) versus 20% (2+2) (p = 0.25); week 24: 21% (6+4) versus 15% (1+2) (p = 0.74). 3 of the 49 (6%) patients in the pre-release group died, all from overdose at 3-5 months after release and 2.5 or more months after stopping XR-NTX, compared to none of 20 in community group (p = 0.55). Limitation(s): Non-random historical cohorts. For those no longer engaged in the original community treatment site, information was largely self-and contact-reported, so substance use could not be consistently determined. Overdose deaths in MA occurred partly in clusters, limiting historical comparisons. Conclusion(s): Receiving XR-NTX prior to jail release for opioid dependence appears to increase the treatment retention rate as compared to commencing after release. The striking rate of overdose deaths in the patients starting treatment prior to release is concerning, and supports the need and availability for additional evidence-based treatments prior to release and in the community.
