AB  - In the United States, burden of disease data indicates that mental disorders account for 15% of health care expenses and disability (Surgeon General's Report, 1999). With health care costs dramatically rising in the last several decades, prevention strategies have been gaining prominence. In spite of the recent upsurge in wellness research, teachers have yet to be studied in relation to wellness interventions. This lack of research is particularly problematic because teachers are critical members of our communities and also experience one of the highest stress levels of any occupation (Kyriacou, 2001). This dissertation reviews the literature pertaining to the theoretical foundations of various wellness models and interventions, and explores the history of teacher stress and interventions. A quasi-experimental intervention is described and the results of this intervention are presented and discussed. Findings suggest that experiential counseling groups are related to a statistically and clinically significant reduction in teacher stress and although the teachers' occupational stressors increased, their perceptions of stress decreased during this intervention. There were no significant findings regarding teachers' wellness levels but exposure to this intervention did change some of the teacher's perceptions of counseling and the obstacles and concerns associated with it. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
