PY  - 1999
DA  - 1999/09//
Y1  - 1999/09//
AB  - Unemployment is a major life stressor that places people at high risk for negative mental and physical health outcomes. Relatively few psychosocial interventions have targeted unemployed populations. This study examined the efficacy of a guided imagery and relaxation intervention, relative to a goal-setting imagery control in promoting reemployment and enhancing health in managers and executives who had been laid-off their jobs. The theoretical basis for the guided imagery intervention was the correlational and clinical literature on unemployment. Specific imagery components were developed to induce relaxation, promote emotional confrontation and neutralization of affect, promote cognitive reframing of the loss, enhance self-regard, facilitate cognitive rehearsal of a typical job interview, and enhance perceptions of control. Specific scripts were constructed, as well as adapted from the mental imagery literature. Participants were 52 unemployed managers and executives recruited from outplacement firms in California (31 males and 21 females; 17% ethnic minorities; mean age = 47, mean unemployment duration = 2.25 months). Participants were randomly assigned to guided imagery and control groups. Participants in both groups attended group-specific 1-hour orientation meetings and then participated individually in six 20-minute sessions over 2-3 weeks. Manipulation checks confirmed that both groups appear to have worked as designed for the first three sessions, with the treatment group experiencing significantly more relaxation and more emotion including anger, than the controls. These differences were less pronounced at Session 6, with only relaxation differentiating the groups. Further, in order to determine whether the intervention and control groups were equally credible and engaging as intended, analyses confirmed there were no significant differences between groups on confidence that the sessions would help them get a job, or on focus and involvement in the sessions. Between-groups differences at post-treatment and two-month follow-up were assessed using group x time ANOVAs (a chi-squared test for the reemployment measure). Results indicated that 61.5% (16 participants) of the intervention group, compared with 11.5% (3 participants) of the goal-setting control group, had begun full-time, permanent work at the two-month follow-up. Anger, contrary to expectations, was not significantly reduced. When baseline anger scores were compared to norms, it was discovered that these participants were not angry, a finding contrary to the literature on this population. The balance of psychological and health outcome measures yielded no statistical significance. With the exception of reemployment and anger, results indicated significant main effects of time in both groups for most psychological outcome variables, including depression, intrusive thoughts, avoidance, and the exploratory outcome variables of self-regard, perceived control, job-seeking self-efficacy, and global coping efficacy. Findings support a direct link between guided imagery processes and the ability to quickly gain reemployment following lay-off. The mechanism of psychological success, however, is not clear and we are left without a distinct etiology. Further research is warranted. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
