AB  - The psychological distress of healthcare workers who care for victims of terrorist acts has become a central and important issue for mental healthcare services in Israel. There is a need to develop a tentative plan for early group intervention to support hospital staff members in their coping with the emotional burden and stress reactions related to traumatic events and extreme situations. Our own personal experience with a suicide bomber at our hospital taught us the necessity of having an organized plan that could be implemented soon after the attack. Although a single-session, semistructured crisis intervention will not prevent posttraumatic stress, the use of early supportive intervention as part of a comprehensive multifaceted approach to the management of acute stress related to a critical incident is recommended. Our experience led us to develop a tentative plan for such situations. This plan has since been put into operation in instances where staff, especially those in the ER, has had to cope with extreme situations such as road accidents, criminal incidents, etc. What stood out for us was the fact that we, who are trained to give help, were suddenly in need of help ourselves, and that our organization was strong enough and the members of the management caring enough to realize this need and implement a plan. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
