AB  - In 2015, the Mentoring, Education, and Clinical Tools for Addictions: Primary Care-Hospital Integration (META:PHI) project was set up, and evaluated rapid access addiction medicine (RAAM) clinics in 7 communities in Ontario, Canada.With government funding, RAAM clinics have now been established in multiple communities throughout the province. This presentation will describe the RAAM clinics and give preliminary evidence of their impact on health care utilization. Alcohol and opioid use continue to be a major cause of morbidity and mortality in Ontario. However, the addiction treatment system is disjointed and difficult to navigate for people struggling with substance use disorders. Only a small minority of patients with alcohol or opioid use disorders are prescribed buprenorphine or anticraving medications, even though these treatments are inexpensive and effective, are covered under the provincial drug plan, and can be prescribed without special training. Patients presenting to the emergency department with a life-threatening complication of substance use are usually not given any treatment, advice, or referrals for the underlying addiction. Psychosocial programs have long waiting lists and complex intake procedures, and they usually do not provide or even permit pharmacotherapy. RAAM clinics are designed to bridge the gaps in the addiction system by providing convenient and low-barrier access to medication- assisted outpatient treatment. Clinics are generally open for walk-in hours 2 to 5 times per week; patients can attend a RAAM clinic during these hours without an appointment or a formal referral. At the first appointment, patients are assessed, given brief solution-focused counselling, and prescribed addiction medications whenever appropriate (eg, buprenorphine for opioid use disorder; naltrexone, acamprosate, or disulfiram for alcohol use disorder). Patients are offered follow-up appointments and connections with other addiction, mental health, and primary care services. During the META: PHI pilot, RAAM clinics were established in 7 Ontario communities. The pilot demonstrated that RAAM clinic patients had a marked reduction in emergency department visits and hospitalizations from 3 months before their first RAAM clinic visit to the 3 months after. Following the success of this pilot, the Ontario Ministry of Health and Long-Term Care funded the establishment of approximately 55 RAAM clinics across the province. META: PHI's long-term objective is to support new RAAM clinics and facilitate the establishment of integrated care pathways, ensuring that evidence-based addiction treatments are available to patients in all parts of the health care system, including emergency departments, hospital units, withdrawal management services, and primary care. The RAAM model of treatment holds promise as an approach to delivering evidence-based addiction medicine to patients who most need it.
