AB  - Studied the efficacy of once-weekly psychotherapy, family therapy, or group therapy led by paraprofessionals among 122 outpatients (aged 20–49 yrs) with cocaine use disorders. Ss were randomly assigned to 1 of 3 forms of therapy and were interviewed 6–12 mo later. Pre- and posttreatment scores on an index of addiction severity were compared. Significant improvements were observed for the cohort as a whole, but virtually all of the improvement was restricted to the 23 Ss who were not using cocaine at follow-up. There was a strong relationship between abstinence from cocaine use and absence of addiction-related problems, especially psychiatric symptoms and family problems. There was no association between number of treatment sessions and improved outcome. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
