AB  - This qualitative, descriptive study focusses on the experiences of mentally ill persons who have abused drugs and alcohol and are engaged in efforts toward recovery. Methods used include participant observation in two different 'dual diagnosis' treatment/support groups; in-depth interviews with 20 persons who attended these groups; and collection of data from mental health providers on informants' severity of substance abuse and level of functioning. Qualitative analysis of data from interview and field notes used a six-step procedure to identify themes and core analytic categories, and to compare experiences of different subgroups. Informants had long histories of alcohol and drug use involving a variety of substances. Their motives for substance use were initially social in nature, but often came to encompass 'self-medication' of illness. They experienced numerous problems related to their substance abuse, including exacerbation of psychiatric illness, arrests, physical health problems, financial problems, housing loss, and disruption of significant relationships. Informants' decisions to become sober were usually highly influenced by external sanctions for continued substance use. Those who continued in recovery experienced it as a process of personal growth, which involved gaining control over illness and reaching out to help others. Prayer and spiritual development were considered by most to be an important part of recovery. Dual diagnosis groups were an important support to recovery, as were 'clean and sober' supported housing and a social network supportive of sobriety. Some informants found Twelve Step groups helpful, but many felt uncomfortable disclosing their mental illness, or were discouraged from taking psychotropic medication. Implications of these results for psychiatric nurses and other mental health providers are discussed. An unexpected finding was that almost a third of the sample spontaneously referred to histories of physical or sexual abuse. Further research is needed on the effects of abuse histories on development of substance abuse and the course of dual diagnosis treatment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
